      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>VAN HOUTENT'S</head>
        <p>VAN HOUTENT'S</p>
        <p>The Best Cocoa.</p>
        <p>skirted• with very high waists, quaint rucked sleeves, finishing in frills of lace at the wrists, and tiny scarves of lace drawn over the shoulders being seen everywhere. White and ivory are preferred to colours, and for parties soft Ottoman silk is in favour. Empire coats( and poke bonnets appear out of dcors, one there is no possible doubt that little girls at the moment are look- ing particularly well. Sealskin Again Pcpular.—The revival of eealskin recalls the fact that this nes one of the favourite furs of mid-Victorian days, and it has hroneht into favour again the sealskin cop, a sort of glorified resurrection of the kind worn in those times. The fact that seal- skin is once fashionable, will be welcome to many who possess a wrap of the coat, of ancient pattern, but which can be remodelled into a stylish winter garment. Skeleton Leaves.—Thoso among us who can recollect the craze of a Illitirter or a century ago, for the fashioning of skeleton leaves, arranging them in groups, and placing them under glass shades, will doubtless experience a feeling of surprise when viewing some of the smartest headgear. Like all trimmings in the millinery world, the up-to-date skeleton leaves are enormous and the very latest idea is to tint them, instead of allowing them to remain their natural and somewhat weird colour. Of course thee° leavts do not spell economy the mists and fogs especially, reducing them to the most cielapidated fragments, and altogether it would seem far hotter to pass them by and cheese, instead, a sn.art wing, or a cluster of flowers. Petal Ruches.—The new ruches for the hat and neck, made of huge chrysanthemum petals, are particularly charming, and when Wilt of two ortbree shades, are often exceedingly artistic. I Lay look, at h distance very like uncurled cstrich feather ruffits, hut hey promise to wear better, and moreover come, triumphantly out of a mist or shower of rain—an item well worthy of consideration.</p>
        <p>. MADGE.</p>
        <p>DRUNK.</p>
        <p>On Saturday at the Occasional Court, before Mr: E. T. Lvans, Sandtel hill TES charged with being diunk on Friday, October 30th, and was tined 2s. ed. and costs. On the same day for a similar offence, Martin McCannon and John Quinn, were ouch fined atd costs, and Peter Cosgrove, ss. nod coats.</p>
        <p>In an article on " 11Th Women : Overdress?" in London Opin (in, a. Hugh Adams de- clares that it is the craze for smarteess that bas so hopeleealy perverted niodern •taste. Deepite the enormous soles spent at the pro- aent time upoe dress and P. s attendant - de- tails, never' have Engliino omen sueceeiled in making such hopeleas caricatures of them- selves. Freak deeesing is the pi it order of the day. All that is gaudy, all that is glar- fripa--all .that is extravagant and ontr&amp;--is the result of this' tenter-de- craze for smartnese. The more unlimited- a wornan'e nicailS, appa- rently', the more Lize.ero and sroteerine are her eh:ethos,- Sweet aimplicity-is- a. thing of the past, start ; u;garity now reigns in its stead. ORFOHD NEW CHURCH. Mae. DESPAED. • • One of the meet interesting of the leaders Consecration by the Bishop 0. .of the suftraaettes Is Mrs. Charlotte DFspard, :I who no dotal:A gets sonic of . her fighting ina Liverpool. stincts from her 'brother, General French. It is . 'Saturday saw the consummation of the desire long many yearn now. since Mrs. Deapard be. cherished by the people of Orford to have a handsome came internsted in the women's movement, church adequate to meet the needs or this growing and although "Votes for : Wciir.en" is her !neighbourhood. In the presence of a crowded con. chief cry, she is also well-known as a Soeialict gregation the Bishop of Liverpool (Right Rey. Dr. speaker and as a worker among the poor. Chavaape) consecrated the church of St. Margaret's ; Ae NATTY LITTLE OVERCOAT.. and All Hallows which has been erected at a cost of,' .£4,0e0. The building, which is "to perpetuate for all If one has net already tackled the question time the memory of tee name of Belmont," generally of winter clothing for the smallar members of exemplifies the possibility of combining all the elements_ the family,. it is high time to do so, for winter of usefulness ith an architectural treatment in an is very close at harel no: Inexpensive manner. It contains a chancel with stalls 4a and a spell of cold weather m for two clergrand a choir of thirty-two, with seating time. ene may accompanying in sketch illustrates a accommodation for 251. Provision lure been made for future extension on the south side of the nave arcade: particularly attractive little overcoat suitable The walls have been constructed and faced with St. for n boy of from four to eignt years. This Helens. grey bricks externally and internally, with the smart Httle garment has 'quite a fashionable exception of a rod brick clado to the internal surfacers- mannish cut, and will delight the heart of any of the nave and chancel walls. The nave has a lofty small boy, I am sure. The original WES car- open-timbered roof and the chancel a barrel vaulted timber ceiling. All external woodwork in dcors, lcuvres, am., is of Dental° csk, and all internal wood- work of Oregon pire. The floors of the nee, ttc., have been laid with hardwood blocks and the floor of the chancel with tiles. The leaded glazing to the windows is of pale green clear sheet glass in 3-81 n leads. The church Is lit by gas from suitably ?esiened fittings, and the warming is by hot water on the low pressure system, and there is ample provision for ventilation. The work is se credit to the architect, Mr. Albert Warburton, A.R.1.13.A., of Bold-street Chamters, and to the characters, Messrs. Jcsaph Dolan and Son, of Napier-street. 'the ceremony on Saturday attracted a very large and representative gathering of clergy and laity from War- rington and district, every seat in the 'church being occupied a few moments after the opening of the doors. Prior to the consecratinn, the bishop, his ch,plain, carrying the pesters! staff, the clergy,choir,and church- wardens assembled at the principal entrance of the church, end the bishcp then proceeded with the cere- mony, assisted by the Rev. Canons Pernhyn, Willis nnd Stevenson, -and the Hers.- G. Vale-Owen (curate-in- charge of Orlord), W.,Pollock-Hill, 13. Percival, J. R. Jones, and G. W. Dickeuson. The hymns "All people that on earth do dwell," " Christ it cur corner-stone," and "Crown Him with many crowns" were sung. Mr. J. Gibson was at the organ. The BlStiop preached from the text "And he said to me, these are they who came outs of great tribulation end have washed their roles end made them white in the Llood of the Lamb." lie remarked they were taking part in a notable event, on a notable day. For six rears past the people of Orford had been denying themselvea, and working with exemplary energy end perseverance. to provide themselves with a church, and that day their proems vrere answered. Their Efforts had been crowned with success, end 'their highest hopes fulfilled. They had built that chuich as a memorial to the late Mr. and Mrs. Beamont. It was never his good fortune to know Mr. Bearncnt but from what he had heard of him he had no hesitation in saying thathe was one of the most term-liable and exemplary churchmen in that part of Lancashire. Mr. Bearnont was the first Mayor of Warrington, and be took the deepest and wisest interest in everything connected with the welfare of his fellow-creatures. He built and main- tained the echool opposite to that chinch, and he was a liberal suaeorter of the infirmary and of all IVarrington ; charities. Mrs. Bearnont was worthy of him, and was as cultured and refined as her husband, taking a kindly interest in all that bad to do for the welfare of thou) ' about her; the remembrance of whose lives would live long to those who knew them; and that church com- memorated them. The Bishop then spoke impreisiiely of the meaning of consecration and of the lessons of All Saints Day and in conclusion be urged his hearers to follow Christ, as those who had goee followed Him, and after they would go the land where sorrow was unknown and where sin cover entered. At the mcreing service the preacher was the Rev. E. R. T.anatecm, of Altrincham, formerly of St. l'aul's, Warrington, who took for his text the words " And the gates or it shell not he fillet at all by day, for there shall be no night there. And they shell bring the glory and honour 0r the nations into it. And there shall in ne wise enter into it anything' that deflleth, neither whatsoever worketh abomination, or maketh a lie, but they which are written in the Lamb's hook of life." The preacher in the evening was the Venerable Archdeacon SPOONER, who took for his text the words "So the House of the Lord was perfected." It had been a great privilege to hint, the preacher said, to be amongst them at their opening serieices, ard he rejoiced with them in offering their thanksgivings to God for His blessing vouchsafed unto theca He spoke in appre- ciative terms of the Rev. G. Vale Owen, whom be bad known for many years. They had iudeed a very beautiful Church. sod Le hoped it would alwae s be filled from end to end its it was that night.</p>
