      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Coster-elsss Ideas, ees getting marrias—' Canpn deplason, who conducted the marriage service at some- thing like 8,000 weddings, during the time he was vicar of Walworth Church, in the slums of .Sonth London, tells some funny stories of his experiences. As many as thirty Cr fee ty couples have bevn married there at one time, many being of the cester-class, and on one occasion the bride obstinately refused to make the promise to" obey." The vicar tried several ways to get over the dilemma, when the bridegroom interrupted and said" never mind guy-nor, git on with the job, I'll make her say obey' when I git her home." Another story relates to a young man, who, after his banns had been twice cntled, told Mr. Jephson that he wanted an alteration--"'Well," said the vicar, " What is wrong." " Oh, its the ,gill's name," said the prospective bride- I groom. " want another name for the third calling, as i I've changed rem mind, and would rather marry Mary t 'Arris instead of Sarah Jones." The vicar thereupon pointed out that it would be necessary to start all over again. "'What, and pay another shillings," gasped the ckle lover.' " Yea" said Mr. Jephson. " Well, in that case you had better let it be as it Ise' And he was , duly married to his first choice. The Wire's Obligation to " Ohey."—The French Legislature is contemplating a revision of the civil marriage service, so as to free the wife from the legal I obligation to obey. There is no vow of obedience in the Roman Catholic rite, and it is only at the civil taarriage service that the French bride has to make such a premiers. It is often made with a smile, an a whispered reservation, and it is a little inconsistent that this formality should be necessary, before the Mayor, whilst it is ignored in the church. The Anglican Church preserves the Roman rite, almost intact, but added the word which enjoins obedience ou the part of the wife. It is not to he supposed that it will make much difference, if the French Government does abolish the legal obligation to obey, but it is a little signiflcent of the trend of modern ideas, on the question of equality, or inequality of the sexes. ,Ilard Worked Mothers.—The Bishop of Manchester sympathetically remarked last week, that no Bishop worked as hard as did a mother in a poor home ' • not only or them bad duties so exacting, so varied, and demanding so much patience. The Bishop might have added,that there were many mothers in his own diocesel who not only discharge these exacting duties, but also go Out to work in order to help in maintaining the borne. We bear a good deal in these days and rightly so, of schemes for providing poor children with holidays, , but there must be thOusands of cases where the mother is even more in need of a rest. The Women Graduates appeal to the House of Lords. The Scottish Women Graduates' Appeal to the House of Lords which was expected to he heard this week, raises the interesting point that the statutory law has never deprived woman of her-vote. In 190 G, the women graduates of Edinburgh University claimed the right to vote for University lember of Parliament, but the I Court of Session decided against them. Hence the' Appeal to the House of Lords, in which Miss F. 11. Simsen, and Mies C. 'Macmillan, both graduates of Edinhergh University, appear in person to support • their claim for the franchise. The names et the women graduates of each Scot tish University are on the Statu- tory Parliamentary Voting Register, and in the oct enfranchising Scottish graduate:,the word" person I " is used, not " man." tis therefore crsetende.d that it isjudge-made law, cemetery to statutory law, that has refasen women gradnates the right to work for the University Member, and it will be extremely interesting to see whether the House of Lords supports or reverses the deeision,of the Court of Session. " "Stock Size."—The perfect woman, as far as the ' posesseion of an ideal figure goes, varies as we know. according to the fashions. At one season hips are , prominent, at another they disappear, and the waist line I differs from time to time by several inches, Loth in iTleastireruent end position. But it seems that naturally the difference between tall and abort women is never snore than two inches from heck to waist, however many filches they may vary from waist, to floor. Another ()Olt elicited in a dispute over "stock-size" goods was, that the so-called perfect woman wears a forty-font inch skirt. Bat skirts, .1 louses, and in fact everythitig in reedy-to-near clothing, is now so standardised that few women of average height and figure have any didiculty in obtaining a fit. This often means a saving of money, and gi‘ es a wide choice to the man who thinks to marry a woman of "steck-size because she comes cheaper. Very Tight Sleeves.—The home worker, when buying dress lengths, this winter, will do well to include another yard or two, beyond the customary "over-piece" for repairs, in slew of the possible change when spring comes round from the very tight sleeves to more roomy varieties. Just now slaves may Le said to have reached their tightest, red, swollen hands frequently resulting. This mode is A huge mistake. unfashionable '" baggy "sleeves being by far the lesser evil. An Uncomfortable Idea. Many of the new evening wraps havano sleeves, only openings through which the arm is thrust. It is true the folds of the wraps are ample and that they can he drawn over the arms, still this is not the same as a sleeve, and schen the folds are permitted to hang straight, the occasional vision of a bare arm, is not aleays entrancing. Moreover the idea can scarcely be termed a comfortable or safe one, with winter at hand. Little Gish: F.'ashions —lt, would seem as thouglel the Kate Greenaway style will lead this winter, long- I</p>
        <p>4</p>
        <p>-4v 4 - -444</p>
      </div>
