        <head>EMPHATIC EARLESTOWN STATEMENT.</head>
        <p>EMPHATIC EARLESTOWN STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>Do you know of any Lind of statement that is more ernpbstio than a rersecal experience ? - Don't you kr.ow that first is Letter than second hand evidence? _ _</p>
        <p>Here is emphatic, fist hand evidence of great value to many. Ni. D. floyes, 20, Sheffield-row, Vulcan, Earlestown, says :—" I can give you high praise for Doan' s pills. There is no doubt there is something about these Pills that relieves an aching back. There were pains across my kidneys and a sort of stiffness; I found it very difficult to get up after Lending. At one time I was. od work for three months with backache. "Dose's pills, however, did me good and Cased the backache ; I am pleased to recommend them. I noticed this medicine cleared the kidney searetions which used to be dark and gravelly. (•tiigned) DAVID IIOYES." " Keep your kidneys well, and they will keep you well," is the best or all rules of health. When the kidneys are well they can carry out their work of filtering out the kidney poisons. When they are ill,you are ill, end you will sager horn Rheumatism, Dropsy, Bsclotche, headache, Urinary Troubles, Nervousness, Pains in the Limbs and Joints. an Broken Donn's Pills cure all Kidney-and Urinary Disoreers, because they act solely on the k:dneys and bladder. They make you well and keep you well. Dortn's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings aml ninepence). Of all chemists and stores, or poet free, direct from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Mr. Boyes was cured by Doan's ; be sure that :you get" DOAN'S"</p>
        <p>Warrington Wounding Case. At the Li'verpool Quarter SeSsions, on Tuesday, Joseph Marratt, of 17, Foundry-street, Warrington, sarrendered to his bail on a charge of unlawfully wounding Isabella Mannion, with a knife on the Srd of August at Warrington. - Mr. W. Mudden (instructed. by Messrs. Jenkins and Co.), appeared for the prose- cution, and Mr. IL L. Riley (instructed by-Mr. T. S. Steel) for the prisoner. The Grand Jury returned a true bill against the prisoner.— Jr. Madden stated that the prosecutrix was unable to appear, she baying been re-admitted to the Warrington Infirmary. . Her condition was serious, and the senior house surgeon was present to testify to that effect.- Sir W. D. Fcrwood, who presided, said that in the eironnstances he thought the C 390 ought to be adjourned until the intermediate sessicms to be held on the 15th Decem- ber. The case was accordingly 'acijoucned, the prisoner being allowed bail, which was enlarged, . himself in £BO and one surety of .tlO.</p>
