      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>NURSE'S VIOLENT HEADACHE.</head>
        <p>A NURSE'S VIOLENT HEADACHE.</p>
        <p>Etriking Testimony for Cil2S. For:la's Bile</p>
        <p>Beans.</p>
        <p>Bi'lateness, Indigestion, and Debility Cured.</p>
        <p>, Mrs. Clara Stuart, a tvell-know nine, of 180, Grosvenor Buildings, Dianesty-street, Poplar, London, E. suffered all the miseries of a chronic victim of billioustess and disot dered liver for years. _</p>
        <p>To-day a course of CHAS. FORM'S Bite Beans have alnce completely restored her health. Nurse Stuart said to London reporter :—'l must have been ailing for fife or six years. I begamfirst With violent head- aches, accompanied by wind on the stomach. Then host my appetite, and turned so bilious that the very sight of food made me sick. Chronic indigestion followed, with extreme lassitude; in fact, I was so weak and ill that it was a trouble for me to even dress myself. .</p>
        <p>I felt that I wanted to be lying about the whole day long.- I was naturally very low-spirited; for the doctor I *at —under, seemed unable to make any improvement in my condition. At last my husband, who is a seaman, persuaded me to try CHAS.' Foam's Bile Beans, which had greatly benefited a mate of his. I immediately got a box of CMS. FORDE'S Bile Beans, and before I was half through it I felt• relief. My headache disappeared ; I felt less languid, and my appetite returned. So much better did I feel when the first hex had been used 'that I eagerly continued the treatment. After the third box of Cats. FORM'S Bile Beans I was entirely relieved of the bilious feelings and the indigestion. My liver was put right and I felt quite strong again. I found pleasure in do'ng work that had before been impos- sible, and my whole life seemed changed. I ant perfectly cured - it is fear years since I had a bilious attack. We are never without a box of CHAS, FOE6E'?• Bile Beans. Beware of worthless " cheap " substi- tutes. The "genuine " that cure are sold only in. sealed wooden boxes ; Is. 11. and 2s. H., of all chemists. 'l'he genuine box has " CIRRUS FORDE'S " on the label.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>- -; 1,</p>
        <p>PHOTOGRAPHIC NOTES.</p>
        <p>By Vei'onica.</p>
        <p>AN EFITONE OF PlCrortin</p>
        <p>Every year a large number of representative pictures, both of these which are not, are submitted to the exhibitions, and of others which are not, are collected and reproduced in the pnges of Photograms of the year. This volume for 1908, like its thirteen predecessors, has a scope much wider than that of any of the National or International Exhibitions, and in addition to some of the best representative work from the principal shows, it hns several pieces from well.known workers who have withdrawn from the annual competitions, and who still produce prints that are as interesting as ever, and who allow the editor of this volume to reproduce some of them. From foreign countries and the Colonies many interesting things are collected, and the editor writes.a frank illuminative critipe of his reasons for selecting certain pictures, and also of the faults which are to be found in many of them, ulongside virtues sufficient to cause their acceptance. Anyone who wishes to learn what is good and bad from the pictorial point of -view, especially if he is a wotker anxious to do pictorial photogrnphy himself, should carefully compare the pictures with the critique in this volume.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>GASLIGHT PHOTOGRAPHY.</head>
        <p>GASLIGHT PHOTOGRAPHY.</p>
        <p>_ Now that the long evenings are upon us, mane a tmera that wculd be otherwise laid aside may well be employed in making gaslight portraits, and as a rough guide to exposures, &amp;c. I may quote from an article by Nurse F. C. Davis in one of the issues of "The Photographic Monthly " for lan winter. With two ordinary bats-wing burners, the, sitters Bft. from the light, a plate of 300 11 St 1) sensitiveness, and a lens aperture of FS, 2t., minutes gave ample exposure. Fob another portrait, a large head, the monree of light was a Welsbach Incandescent C mantle, with large .shade of opal glass, and was used nearer to the sitter, with a e bite reflector for softening the shadows with the same plate speed and shuttle aperture )2 minutes was given. FLOWERS AND STILL LIFE BY GASLIGHT. Many inierestiug arrangements of flowers, dried grasses, pottery, objects of art, Ste., twy well be photo- graphed by ordinary gaslight or by the light of one or two paraffin lamps. In these cases, the camera will be used ap a longer extension than for portraits (hecauae it is desirable to get the objects large on the plate) and a smaller stop will generally have to he used. As a rough approximation of the exposnre, one may suggest that with the subjects within three feet or four feet of an incandescent gas burner, and with a stop of FIG, an , exposure of frora 5,t0 10 minutes should be given to special rapid plates. INTERIORS BY GASLIGHT: •</p>
        <p>It is quite possible to make good soft negatives of the interiors of ordinary rooms by means of their ordinitry lighting with gas or oil lamp, and very often this evening lighting is more harmonious and gives a more cosy-looking result tha.l is obtained in the same room by daylight. For such subjects it is generally possille to use an aperture as large as Fll, and if the actual som ce of light he excluded from the picture, it will be possible to give long enough exposure to give detail well into the chadows without over-exposing the 'higher lights. With a rapid or extra-rapid plate, and with the whole of the usual lighting of the room. exposures of from 10 to 61 minutes may be given' the difference depending largely, upon the colour of the paper and furokhings, and the diffusion of the light..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>LONG EI`;.PpSURES FOR PORTRAITS.</head>
        <p>LONG EI`;.PpSURES FOR PORTRAITS.</p>
        <p>Ono of pA trs'oro es.rs'ap"hin. '' sitters by gaslight i. that thy are not to look very rigid and staring if they have to sit still through an exposure of from two to five minutes. Miss Annie S. Carter, who haidone a good deal of this Work, 'gets orcr the di:ll- culty by arranging for tht sitter's heed to rest comfort- ably against some object, and by breshing the exposure into three or more parts. At each interval the sitter is allowed to close the eyes, moisten the lips, and gener- ally relax all the muscles ,of the face, and the rest for the head ensures the position being the same when the exposure is resumed. '</p>
        <p>NEXT YEAR'S Z."IOTOGRAPIIIC CONVENTION. The fellovting programme has been Arranged by the Council for the tmenty-fcurth annual Convention, to be held at Cantertury in July next, nticlerthe Presi- dency of IL Snowden Ward Esq., P.R.P.S. Retiring President, Sir Cecil llertslet. :Monday, July sth. morning and aftoronn, in and nround Canterbury. Evening, Re-union, Presidentinl Address and annual meeting. • Tuesday, July CH), a driving tour though the villages of Bridge, Patrixbourne, Belres- bourne, Lit tleboune, Wingharn, Ickbarn,lTichbam, Ford- wick f.nd SU:try. Evening, rapers, etc., Wednesday, July 7th. Mern:ng, visit to the Cathedral. Afternoon garden party, and evening the annual dinner and concert. Thursday,-.Toly Bh,Antursion to Rochester. Evening, papers, etc. Friday, July 00, excursion to Rye and Winchester. Evening, papers. etc. Saturday, July 30th, exchrsion to Maidstone. The holdcriarters or the Convention during the week will boat St. 00.,rge's , Hall. further particulars :nay be obtained of Mr. F. A. Bridge, hon. sec., East Lodge, Dalstan-lane, London, N.B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>TINTER IN THE DARK ROOT.</head>
        <p>TINTER IN THE DARK ROOT.</p>
        <p>The Psyche knot seems to accord best with the classic.mode in dress.. Hair is no longer dressed high but between the nape of the neck and the. crown. ' It is made into soft irregular waves, which seem most natural. Very wide hair - dressing above the ears is the thing. Long coils of hair are to be twisted round this Psyche knot. The large hats set well over them cover them, but they -are so big, says the , Queen,: that soon it will be no longer possible to wear them at the theatre, because there would not be roam to be seated side by side; - and bunches of plumes/ with every stem of a different colour, are among ,the newest things, sashes being knotted about all hats now. TO-DAY'S RECIPE. SHROPSEIRE PlE.—First make a good 'puff- paste crust, then cut a couple of rabbits to pieces, with two pounds of fat pork, cut in little pieces; season both with pepper and salt. Line the pie-dish with crust, lay an the rabbits mixed with the pork. Take the livers of the rabbits, parboil them, beat them in a mortar, with as much fat bacon, a few sweet herbs, some oysters if you .have them; season with pepper, salt, and nutmeg. mix it 'up with yolk of egg, make it , into lolls lay them .here. and A s artichoke thereoon itoms the Me. r"-r7 . 6, FARRINGDON' AVENUE, nice addition -ts ome t eat 4.11... n into dice, and cocks' combs. Grate some nut- . LONDON, B.C. :nog over the.:mcat,:pour in half a pint of red wino, half a pint of Water. Cover the pie with a. crust lid, bako it for one hour and a-half -ia a quick oven, but not too &amp;ma,</p>
        <p>IS A COLLECTION OF NEARLY 200 CHARMING PICTURES, REPRESENT- ING THE BEST PICTORIAL PHOTO- GRAPHY OF THE YEAR. —STIFF PAPER COVERS; 2s. NET. CLOTH BOUND, 3s. NET. POSTA.GE SD. • DAWBARN Sz: WARD, LTD.,</p>
        <p>Beginners- -i-ri-pln-toi:r.rtpV3liir-n-Wr-3; apt to think that they have made mistakes by tinder-exposure, or that their plates are much less sensitive than those they have been accustomed to using. Simply because the lowered temperature of the dark room and of the water supply with which they make up their solutions, causes development and ell other chemical operations to become much slower. It is at...thiA time of the year that the thermometer becomes most veinal:le. Every dark room should have one, rad it should be constantly in use. Generally speaking, the best results will be obtained by keeping all photogr.phic solutions at about 63 degrees, E. Tha one with which there is likely to be the most trouble is hypo, and in the winter at any rate, photographers are advised to keep a stock of this solution and not to make It freshly each time it is wanted. The dissolving of the crystals reduces the temperature cf the water very greatly, end the use of a chilled dsnse fixing lath after n relatively warm developer or toning is very liable to cause blisters. 3fr. .3. H. Crabtree pointed out some time ago that the dark room lrmp is a convenient source of heat for small quantities of solution, end advised that a skeleton stage of wood or wire should be built above the lamp so that a bottle or dish containiug solution could easily he stood to receive the heat.</p>
        <p>AN IDEAL CHU BOW:. PHOTOG RAMS OF THE YEAR, DK</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>;HOLIDAY IMPRESSIONS OF AMERICA.!</head>
        <p>;HOLIDAY IMPRESSIONS OF AMERICA.!</p>
        <p>Rev. C. Harvey Cook's Intansting</p>
        <p>Lecture.</p>
        <p>The itev. C. Harvey Cook delivered an interesting lecture entitled "Holiday Impressions of America" before the members of the Warrington Society at the Old Academy on Monday evening. Mr. 0. Lofthouse presided over a good attendance. 'Mr. Cook left Liver- pool by the "Carmania " on Auguet 27th. arriving at New York on September 3rd. After a visit to many of the chief places of interest he arrived home on October 100.</p>
        <p>I--The trip which he bad the delight of making, said Mr. COOK, was in connection with a series of Inter- -1 national meetings at Boston of friends of the particular household of faith to which he belonged. It would not have been possible for him to have attended had not his generous congrega- tion given him a cheque of £5O towards the expenses, an extended holiday, and a resident student preacher in charge daring his absence Mr. Cook gave a graphic description of the voyage and touched very entertain- ingly upon the hot debate on Free Trade and Tariff Reform, games and various occupations which served to while away the time. Of all one's impressions of America, the !mutest and the deepest (said Mr. Cook) was one of rawness, of uncouthness. He did not mean of manners—for he found acme very suave and urbane Americans—but he meant of its life, its institutions, its architecture, its cities, its railways, the whole order of its collective being. America was young, it was lhsty, it was as a Goliath, but no ruddy English David had wit enough to sling a smooth stone straight enough to lay it low. To treat America as Philistine was cheap. It was a surface criticism,an explaining of tire obvious. But America was not to be treated as Philistine. Her , extravagances, her swaggerings, were but the entlausi- I astic exuberations of her youthful vigour. Her heart Iwas sound. She was "given to hospitality." New York—well, it was New York. If there be a madder place on God's earth, God help the poor 1 'lunatics. If there be a more insane asylum than Wall- Street, God have mercy upon it. Fever heat was as zero, and yet was immensely exciting. He felt its - clutches getting hold of him, and he believed that if he had stayed in the Exchange a few minutes longer he would have joined the frenzied mob. It was a great scene. "Bedlam let loose" wasn't a patch on it. To speak of her, condition as electric was to use a mild metaphor. ... . . . • . . . .</p>
        <p>What America needed was "Peace," vtbich would help her to possess her soul in patience, " peace " which could help her calm and quiet, "peace " which made her gentle. One could not view with unconcern the growth of that other " Great Empire by the sea." Then one remembered that America was receiving on an average 1,000,000,000 new souls into her embrace every year, not like Canada more European than Eastern, but Western and Eastern, one could not but look with something akin to alarm and ask what sort of social amalgam she was going to make of it? To weld Asiatics. Japanese, Chinese, men and women horn every tribe under the great sun' into one organic social whole was an ideal which might well fire the imagination of a President and kindle a people to heroic, deeds. This was the most pressing problem America had on her hands. America was not riding to a fall ; her bead was screwed on tightly, and she kept it clear from fuddledom, by which he meant that however keen and razor-edged her commercial ways, she was sober. He never saw less drinking in his various travels than among those who were in. business in America.</p>
        <p>At Philadelphia he found something nearer culture in the atmosphere, and the rush was slackened ; while at Washington, the one city of America which appealed to him' masners were gentler. Washington was planned ' almost as a big garden city, and here America had I lavished treasure. One wished that America would I develop ell architecture of her own. Washington's 1 buildings were wanting in that they contained nothing distinctively architecturally national. No only so, but the mixture of 5t31e5 in any one building was so fre- quent that one got no idea of symmetry or finish. Certain indelible impressions of his trip were prophetic. Surfaci critics had asserted, until theasser- tion bad become nauseating, that in America the dollar was almighty, that the American worshipped his dollar, put his faith in it. To those assertions ho gave the lie direct. America was learning as England was, that the manufacturj) of millionaires was a social menace to the nation. Again and again he talked with men,not parsons nor pious in the church or chapel sense, but men of the world who avowed their conviction that the best Americans were seeing that Rockfellers, Vanderbilts, Pierpont Morgans were a reproach and not a blessing to civilization, that the power of the money-bag was sapping the best life of her peopre, that its ramifica- tions were such that be could defeat the ends of justice and stood condemned by all sane men before the bar of sound judgment. ' . . . . .</p>
        <p>The domestic side of American life strlick him as being very congenial. Clubs abounded, but there was a distinct home instinct. That the American was not another form of Englishman every observer could not fail to note. The Canadian was much more an English- man than the American. America was no part of Greater Britain. She was different in many subtle, very difficult to &amp;fine and yet vety real ways. She was American speaking Anglo-Saxon and the language was the one thing only in which we were one people. Her ideals differed from ours. Iler mental machinery worked with different material ; her genies, her spirit, her tempera- ment, and her character were all in the make. He took it that her difference ley in the fact of her sub- conscious, untrained, undisciplined idea of-her vastness. We were insular despite the greatness, of our Empire. We had been born and bred in a lend whence we could go from one end to the other in 'a matter of practically 124 hours. It was the unrealized magnitude of their Continent which made the two Americas and the real !North American no other than English. The South America was much more English than his brother of the States and ho (Mr. Cook) took it to be due to the fact that whilst both had common English ancestry, North America—the United States—bad given room to ! the wanderer from every part of the earth and by ! coming into contact with him had been and was being subtly changed—conditions which the South American had never yet met.</p>
        <p>Of the nasal twang and the metallic voice, his impres- sion was that both, found their explanation in atmos- pheric conditions. America bred few priguadonnns ; her climate acted too drastically upon the sensitive chords of' tho throat and chest. There was a kind of chronic catarrh, so that one did not hear good singing, even on the boards. What he saw of the theatres, whioh was very little, told him The same tale of the effect of sudden changes from beat to cold on the-Fgan of speech. Arucrica had a native literature and poetry. It had art, not as yet quite native like its literature and poetry. Its art was too lipped in luxury ; it was not yet instructed by Ziecipline. Of music, except the banjo which was negro,it had no national instrument, nor nation4l score. This be took it was accounted for by the fact that the musical sonl of a people could only be expressed in its music after the ptopte Lad been upon the land for am, caught the particular whisper of the wind in its disnict so that to a Norwegian the wind spoke another tune than to him who dwelt in the prairie. What that music would be with which presently America would charm the world, one could only faintly guess from trying to imagine what should be a nation's music which had all the variety of coast and inland, sea and lake, mountain end prairie, heat and cold of America, w hen caught up by the imagination and by the ear and genius of some great American Mendelesohn, whose musical pen should set it within an immortal scote.</p>
        <p>,dis;</p>
        <p>Of the condition—s of labour on BMW plainly enough that it was no Eldorado. That labour is well paid on tSe whole he thought was clear. but Tammany was so powerful-that freedom was not more known, perhaps not so much known, as among our own industrial elJasee. The wages were larger, but the expenses of liylo. greater and the pressure at least two-fold, if not three-told greater.</p>
        <p>In morals e not think we could afford to throw --- - stones. In philantbrophy we 'might be a notch or two ahead because of our longer history. But in education, it seemed to him we might well learn of America. What struck him most was the unstinted expenditure of money to make the educational machinery as perfectas possible. Ile found everywhere in America that the schoolmaster and the teacher were looked up to. and treated as being as worthy of respect as the medical profession. Of the Church life, be had only one thing to note. The spirit there was in the same throes as in all other realtne. The Churches on the whole seemed ostentatious, the atmosphere of the peacher promoting at lest the idea in his mind : " Well, God, here we are, n smart lot of Americans, mho know just the value of things to the bottom dollar. You'd better take us, or so much tho worse for Your Peshaps it was all his own fault, due to his own eccentricity. He liked cheerfulness in public service, but he also liked solemnity. An interesting discussion followed and Mr. Cook was cordially thanked for his lecture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>Wycliffe Eeciety.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Eeciety.</p>
        <p>There was a very enjoyable meeting of this Society .on Sunday, presided over by the Secretary. Mr. Wardman gave tbe address upon "The Optimism and pessimism of Elijah." In the lives of all, he said, these two features were manifest : the brighter and the darker §icle of things. It was better at all times to keep a steady outlook upon the brighter side. Mr. C. Cotton and Ur. G. Smiley also took part in the meet- ing. Mr. Humphreys sang '' For ever with the Lord' and "Arm, arm ye brave" in gond style find both solos were greatly appreciated. Mr. W. 11. Payton, Mug. 131 C , ptesided at the organ. One new member received a hearty welcome. Mrs. Parry returned thanks for herself and family in the sympathy shown In their bereavement. A letter was read from the Rev. John Yonge thanking the brethren for the con- gratulations sent him last week. As it was Federation Sunday sp,eial hymns had been appointed to be sungffy all P.S.A.'sin the Federation. and they were heartily Jotne,tl in. The Wycliffe P.S.A is steadily improving in numbers. ;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>CHRONIC ASTHMA with I-hemorrhage of Lungs.</p>
