      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>DECLARED HOPELESS Extraordinary Results from VENO'S LIGTNINO COUGH CURE.</head>
        <p>DECLARED HOPELESS Extraordinary Results from VENO'S LIGTNINO COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Beard, of 40, Hueltnall Road, Carriag- , ton, Nottingham, writes :—" I suffered fur five years / from chronic aqhma and shortness of breath, with periodical and 'dangerous attacks of bleeding of the- ! lungs. Doctors declared me incurable, and I gave- up all hope of ever being better. II One day, is a last resource, I began to take Veno's Lightning Cough Cure, and it at once checked- ! the bleeding and eased the spasms, when doctors' / medicine and all else had failed. I Mn quite sure 'that your wonderful remedy has prolonged my life. I "1 was very bad when I began taking Veno's- Lightning Cough Cure, but now lam able to follow my employment. lam never without a bottle of your valuable specific." Mr. Herbert C. Lees, Chemist, of *Market Place, Cariington, Nottingham, verifies the above a 3 follows : —" I can personally vouch for the value of I Yeno's Lightning Cough Cure in the case of Mr. Beard being indisputably and substantially correct." (Signed) H. C. LEES. Veno's Lightning Cough Cure is free from all' dangerous drugs, and is the safest, purest, and most i effective remedy extant for asthma, coughs, colds, bronchitis, catarrh, hoarseness, sore throat, whooping, cough, and all chest and lung troubles. Price - Is. id. and 2s. 9d. of all chemists and stores.</p>
        <p>DIAMOND JUBILEE OF THE MUSEUM. Sunday. was the Goth anniversary of the opening of the Warrington Museum and Library. Alluding id the occasion the "Manchester Guardian' of Saturday said: "The celebration this week of the 'diamond jubilee of the Warrington Free Library raises once again a question of some nicety between the free libraries of Warrington and of Salford. When the Library AESOCia- tiorr held its meeting in Manchester ten years ego Mr. , Ben Mullen made this question the subject of an interest:ng paper, and he seemed to convince manY people that the distinction of being the pioneer library was Salford's and not Warrington's. But Warrington I apparently is unconvinced, and this week's doings have been well advertised as the celebrations of "the first rate-supported free library." This it certainly is not. IBristol had a free library nearly $OO years ago that was partly supported by. a levy on the community. But that Bristol institution became extinct In the latter part of the eighteenth century, and its successor to-daYt- , the city's municipal free library, can claim no relation- ship with it. Free libraries there are, of course, of greater antiquity than either Salford's or Warrington's, but these two were unquestionably the first ....municipal free libraries. As is usual in controversies of this kind, there is something to be said for both sides. The Warrington library, originally a proprietary library, I was surrendered to form a public library in 1848. The library in Peel Park, Salford, was established in June, 1840. That seems to he the strong point of the Warrington claim. But in its beginning the Warring- ton institution was not wholly "free ' in the sense- , that the term is understood to-day and in the sense that the Salford library has been always free. At the start the Warrington Corporation imposed, for revenue per- poses, a subscription, and the use of the library Was restricted to subscribers on Mondays, Tuesdays- , Thursdays, and Fridays. In hls paper before the I Library • Association Mr. Mullen argued that this extreme prohibition of public free admission precluded Warrington from the distinction of having been the first town to found a free nubile' library. But he granted to Warrington the credit of being the first town `to make a suggestive movement in that direction.' It w 5 the Salford, and not the Warrington, example that other municipalities quickly copied. Warrington,oddly enough, did not give unrestricted admission until 1837 and continued nntil 1891 to charge a fee of one penol on every book borrowed by a resident in the town."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>JUMPED ON THE LINES.</head>
        <p>JUMPED ON THE LINES.</p>
        <p>Warrlngton ryinns Depression and Attempted Suicide.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednes- day. William Clair, Knutsford-road, IVarrington, ssllg charged with attempting to commit suicide by throu- ing himself in front of a passenger train at Earlestownr on August 27th. It was stated by prisoner's brother-in-law, MO' Shaffer°, house furnisher, Market-street, Earlestounr that he was in company with the prisoner in Earlestown Station, waiting for a train to Warrington. Without any warning prisoner jumped across the metals in front of a train, and was caught by the wheels. He 5y5,5 taken to the Warrington Infirmary, and afterwards removed to tho iVorkbouse Ekspital, where he remained as an inmate.</p>
        <p>Dr. Corker,. assistant medical officer at the W 0 house, Enid prisor.er was very- depressed, and on t,v° • occasions he had expressed a desire to commit The prisoner's relatives declined to take— responsibility of his custody, and the maglstrste3- remanded priioner to the workhouse for —anOtilt,r. fOrtriiht.</p>
