      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>WORKMEN'S RECREATION. MESSRS. RYLAN-D'S' AMBITIOUS SCHEME.</p>
        <p>SOCIAL REFOR :U.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. J. BLECKLY said he wish ad to say how much interested be was in what he had seen and how he admired the method by which the men had worked to bring the scheme to such a successful issue. Mr. Nield and his co-adjutors who had managed the business had done a very great deal for the advancement of social reform and he thought their efforts in that direction would compare very favourably with a great many sermons and teetotal lectures, though he did not for a moment wish to under-value the effect of those. Be the orators as eloquent as they might be, he thought the substantial good that was done by clubs of that hind would be very difficult for them to outweigh. He was exceedingly glad to see Mr. Ilarmood Banner and Mr. Crosfleld present,ns he thought tho history of those institutions and the evidence they saw before them should he taken to a higher tribunal. What they wanted was not the right to work, but the opportunity of working, and when they had worked they wanted the opportunity of recreation and enjoyment, and that wee afforded in both its phases at that club. He wished every -success to the undertaking. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>HARMOOD BANNER -AND THE SCENES IN PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. .T. S. lIARMOOD BANNER, M.P., said that ss the Chairman of the Pearson and Knowles Company, Tfe felt 'somewhat ashamed that the parent concern had not done , as much as Messrs. Itylands s in regard to a recreation scheme. Mr. Crosfleld and himself were on opposite ' sides in politics, but he believed they could shake hands over everything that concernod the welfare of 1 Warrington. (Applause.) They were feeling rather proud to be there in safety. (Laughter). He saw , some ladies amongst those who were listening to his ; speech and be was exceedingly glad to know that neither Mr. Crosfield nor himself was a Minister in Ithe present Administration—(laughter)—because ladies did not seem quite willing to let Ministers speak. (Laughter.) However, they were onlyhumble members of Parliament and he thrnked the ladies for having allowed him to speak so much without intervention. There was one thing in Parliament they were very much struck about and that was the attention now being paid to the health and recreation of the people. There was no doubt uhAtever that whilst we were all being stimulated to work very hard we had also pot to assert ourselves in order that we might play very bard, because the future of EnFland depended entirely upon the energy and strength of her sons and daughters and'unless they tried to improve their bodies, to live up to the Latin phrnse merge sotto in corpore nano, they would not he fit to do the work which the future would call them to do. (Hear, hear). Mr. IT. MADDOCK proposed a vote of thanks to Councillor Nield for presiding. S PEECH TW THE 130ROLTGIII MEMBER.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. CROSFILLD, M.P. who bad every cordial greeting, paid he was sorry he had not a little MOTO voiee with which to second the vote of thanks•and he would like at once therefore, in reference to the humorous and very appropriate remarks that had fallen</p>
