        <p>THE RESULTS.</p>
        <p>At eight o'clock the ballot boxes were conveyed to the Town Hall, where, in the Council Chsruber, the work ofceunting the votes was proceeded with in the Prnenceof the Nfayor (Alderman Smethurst), the Pre- siding Odicers (Alderman Porshaw for Orford Ward and Alderman Tianjin for St. John's Ward), the Town Clerk, the Daputy T..rvn Clerk, and others. The result WAS made known shortly before nine o'clock as follows :</p>
        <p>ST. JOIN'S Air. F. T. Las'itlrd (0.) 733 Mr. J. Ball (Labour)</p>
        <p>I\fasjurity ORFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>Tr. P. Palcoo% ' 555 Mr. J. A. Rushmorth (C.)</p>
        <p>Arr. J. [lan:lpso° (f.nb;:n.)</p>
        <p>Majority.. . 83</p>
        <p>There were mare spoiled votes than usual, several r"rsons hlving written their names opposite that or the candidate.</p>
        <p>VOTES OE THANIcS. A MESSAGE TO TGE BRENER'S.</p>
        <p>Councillor Edisnrorin proposed a vote of than!rs to Alderman Tinnion and the oincials for their services. Mr. BALI, seconded, and said the officials bad served them faithfully, honestly did courteously. They always fdund that the presiding officers, poll clerks and returning officers endeavoured to carry the campaign through with the greatest amount of utbanity and fairness to both sides, and that had been the case that day. With regard to Councillor Lashford and himself, he wished to say that they had been on the most friendly terms, and there had been no personal bitterness or hostility between thorn. The cam- paign in St. John's Ward had been conducted with great fairness on both sides. (Applause.) 'rho resolution having being carried, Alderman TiNivrox said he could corroborate what bad been said about the happy and filendly way in which the contest had been fought. Ile congratulated the winner and sympathized with the loser. Councillor PEACOCK, in proposing a vote of thanks to Alderman Forshaw and the officials, said they had had a hard fight but he believed work would win. ,He hoped the result of the election would give notice to the brewers of Warrington that they had got enough of them in the Cmincil, and that was the only reason he had been fighting. Ho believed right was on his side, and he was pleased he had won. He was sorry his opponent could not win as well. Mr.itUsitwortrit seconded,and said he could not com- mend his opponent, for the simple reason that be had I made it an unpleasant duty. There should be an end of !controversy in that room. He thanked the officials very much for the impartial way in which the election had been conducted. , (Hear, hear). Mr. HAMPSON kinpp orterl and said he was nat at all satisfied with the result of the elootion, as be would rather have got in. Bat he was only is pung man yet, and if the party to which he-belonged had confidence in hinrhe was willing to try'2ngain. Ile bad noticed that the officials and others connected with the election had done their utmost to make all of them work together as amicably as possible, and be did not think there was any reason to complain.</p>
