        <head>New Pavillon Opened.</head>
        <p>New Pavillon Opened.</p>
        <p>.. 11 Saturday was a memorable day for the members of ' assts. Rylands' Bros. Workmen's Recreation Club, . /or • , . • Il it witnessed the formal opening by Mi.' W. Peter ylande of the handsome new peril' in which has been , •efected on the Gorsey lane recreation grounche The ':,.,nh is heartily to be congratulated on acquiring such a e and well appointed build:mg and it is question- 'loe whether a better one of its kind is to be found in . lacction with any other works in the country. The , ai3.olaint(lt•s two 'workmen's recreation scheme was started years ago, when the men hell a mass !nesting, and decided to pay a small sum per week to 11g ale it, and also 0 control and support it rnselves. A suitable piece of land of fifteen acres t 17. as secured from Colonel Ireland Blackburne, through , els an.p ;., el ~nts, on very reasonable terms, situated between . 12,nsey-lane and Orford-road, Warrington, and - arsween the two C.L.C. railway embankments. There - . I, "nm on the, ground two football pitches, four sowliars greens, and a large cricket ground with n•cnially laid turf. What with the fine cinder paths s T-„lvith orders of pretty flowers, and hundreds of trees ad shrubs plaided, the whole forms an attractive llark which in the near future will undoubtedly be the beauty spots of Warrington. The pavilion was opened on Satnrdav has been erected' -': ;1." aa approximate cost of L,3,000. The general acal with the stage and proscenium is . capable of (vetting three hundred people. There , are also billiard rooms containing two full size tables, ~ 3.7,114, a itmior room with two semi-billiard tables The ..' -Sass'.room is well furnished, and is very pleasant "•, With its oak fittings. There is also a reading-room -- aPstairs, upholstered in leather, and it opens out on • t, which ;) a 'Deleonoverlooks the whole grounds, and y '-' iraal winch a fine view of the surrounding district i' ' „can be obtained. Special attention has been pail to ' ' lle la;rato.ries, which are furnished throughout in the ' latet designs and include four hot and cold water - "abower baths, which are greatly appreciated by the :Islayers after a hard game of foritball. The pavilion . aas be,en furnished throuoliont in oak by Messrs. J. M. Greenhalgh and Sons, Bridge street, and is heated by .. - r_adiators on the low-pressure hot-water system. The forms of recreation already adopted by the members , Tiraterreierrical7t, Association football (three teams), bowie, swimming, gymnastics,and fishing, to which !low the pavilion is open may be added billiards, g n. 1 ats, chess, amateur tbeatrials, and vocal . adad instrumental music. Though so much has been ane, the council are still ambitions, and hops in the , snaan future to erect a building fora boys' gymnasium, •, 'l7adquarters for a boys' brigade, also provide tennis .. npurts, hoske ygrounds, and rifle ranges. , Gr ' • s.i eat credit is du ato the architect (Mr. Percy ecck. F.R.1.8.A.), and the builder (Mr. Frank ..,_ arbarton, of Stockton Heath) for the excellent way . %which the building has been designed and erected. , 13,,,,1•n,a, ofilcials of the drab are :—Mr. W. Peter Rylands, * Lel.'ent ; Messrs. FL H. Field, J. J. Nield and I'. ' 114.1,. vice-presidents. Mr. IV. Webb, hon. treasurar ; ' tari '`lessrs. W. H. Evans and H. Haddock, hon. scare- c''• The Works' Council or Executive Committee ~ ,v nsists of 30 members, nominated and elected by the 1 erc'erknum in the prqportion of one representative to no members or thereabouts. AI'S netter day for the opening ceremony could not vis:e been chosen. The weather was ideal—in fact „. so'h slich brilliant, sunshine and balmy air it Was bard llor!ialies that it was the last day of October. The ti Ilion was tastefully decorated with a profusion of IRR an nondi d bunting. There was a very large attendance, . „rind rig many prominent town:Tee-Tie and several .` r,...1 red worknenple and their wives and friends. . ~ aZineillor J. J. Nield presided over the proceedinvt , r,:t amongst others present were :—Mr. and Mrs. \V. ,r R: ar Rylancls, Miss G. ftylands, Mr. J. Paul Rylands, •- Tr Gilbert and Lady Greenali, Mr. J. S. llaratood, , l'- 7 ilr on sr :fleer Id,' LP- and Mrs. Harrnood-Banner, Mr. A. H. M.P., Mr. J. J. Bleckly, Mr. and Mrs. Jelin .• asgej, iun.. mr. and Mrs. Arthur S. Bleakly, Mr. and ,and 'AV. Ti. Pield,Mr. and Mrs. Henry Greenall,the Mayor - Pc, VaToresr, t?(”7. Canon Willis, Alderman and Mrs. Councillor and Mrs. James Locker, Mr.and Mrs. Cr. Aldermen end Mrs.Arthar Bennett,Captain G. .orrCrorifield, Mr. Holman Kingden, Mr. J. H. Watts . ' mingham), Mr. and Mrs. John White, Councillor • 'kV Mrs. Fulton-Smith Mr. and Mrs. Walter Milner, Mrs. Henry Itribert; Councillor and Mrs. J. G. ." iiralk. Alderman and Mrs. Tin4ion, Mr. and Mrs. . tilnehinn. Ir. and Mrs. Thomas Leslie, Mr. and Mrs. !ank Warburton, Mr. and Mrs. S. P. Silcock, Mr. and 4rs. Sloane, Mr. J. Willett, Mr. A. C. Binks, Mr. A. ' .slaage, Mr. and Mrs. W. Webster, Mr. and Mrs. IT. ,j.:°„Pe, Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Greenbalgh, Mr. and Mrs. w v. it oe 4. Gireenhalgh, Messrs. IV. 11. Evans and IL Maddock e Th• sec 3.) and others. . f,, CIIAIRMAN announced the receipt of apologies ._ Trllna on-attendanee from Mrs. Peter Rylasis, Colonel tr''ra.arld Tilackburne, Mr. and Mrs. W.H. Blecltly, Mr. and it r5° 'alli 4* J. Crosfield, Mr. and Mrs. Dodds, Dr. , llrs. Markel. Mrs. Burton, Mr. and Mrs. lii:r‘e,T. Al r. 11. •tven, Mr. and Mrs. Herbert , Mr. and Mrs. C. E. Bunch, Mr. ,17. W. Mr. Tatham' Mr. Isherwood, Mr. rt Pierpoint, Mr. and Mrs. Carson and MT. 1.11,:u:, Bleelcly. Continuing, Councillor Nield said ' s.. ,;nos,nfit was a great day for them. It was. one of the 13;1,ibcif the times that working Ten had ,dtsco c! it 0. fcr th a by co-operation to provide healthy recreation .ta th elbsel yes. Universal educatioh had demonstrated 4nrl ter3 the power of the almighty penny. A penny eel, 'Nuance a week subscribed by the working men fa,t ited the back