      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>WOUND 3 AND SORES COVERED BY FRESH SKIN.</head>
        <p>WOUND 3 AND SORES COVERED BY FRESH SKIN.</p>
        <p>A Remark]Ye. Home Remedy.</p>
        <p>• The home method of effectively healing wounds and srwes by driving out poisonous germs and gr owi og new, healthy skin dates from the 'discovery of Z .in- buk, the now well-kno,vn skin-cure and first-aid. A box of this wondethal preparatiou alway. 3 kept handy saves doctors' bills and d.iino,tic worry. The story of bow the proprietors were led to seek for and obtain such a perfect healing balm is very interesting. In the first place, it was rec•agnised that modern ointments, • salves, and liniments lose in efficiency because of the rancid animal fats and minerals poisons entering into their composition. Such ingredients are too coats° ta do the average skin any good at all, and their makers do not dare to recommend them for actual skin disease, while some are entirely unsnitable for use on the human skin. From the outset, there- fore chear, doubtful, or unnatural ingredients were carefully avoided ia the search after the ideal preparation. The splendid athletes Ancient Greece and the gladiators of Rome regularly uso. healing oils and balms which were prepared f rota pure nvdi cinal herbs, and this indicated the direction in which the ideal skin- cure was, likely to be found. Certain rare herbs and plants were therefore taken, and from these were extracted pure gums and juices, possessing the various medicinal properties necessary to make up a perfect skin-healer. 'The correct com- bination' and concentration of • these essences were only secured after lengthy experiment, but when, the substance now called Zatn-buk was finally perfected and put upon-the market, a hearty reception every- where greeted the new and wonderful bahn. Zam-buk is accurately described as the last word of science in Ow treatment of shin diseases and wounds. It displaces once ,and for all the coarse and nasty ointment's that have sufficed in the past, but which to-day have lost favour with the intelligent public Zam-buk is an entirely new idea —a scientific dis- covery, and a boon to husband,wife, or mother —and is absolutely free from the rancid animal fats and mineral irritants of cheap salves. Zam-buk, by reason of its purity awl refinement, gets to the root of eczema, rinumrni, and obeinwe sores. On being smeared on an affected place, it gets absorbed through the pores or minute "breathing holes " in the skin, and promotes the soundness of the anderlying tissues. The ,regalar use of Zam-buk at this season is the genuine cure for chaps and cold- sores, as well as for piles, bad legs, festering wounds, eczema, &amp;c. Every chemist keeps Zam bah in Is. I Id., 2s. ill ,or 4s. Gd. boxes (4s, eal. size contains twice the 2s. Pd.)</p>
        <p>Licensing Transfer. At the Borough Police Court, on Monday, before the Mayor (presiding) and other magistrates, the transfer of the licence of the Old Ball Inn, Winwick-road, from Fanny White to Henry Teece was granted.</p>
        <p>THE WAR ON BATS. In connection with the deputation which waited upon Lord Carrington, President of the Board of Agriculture ond Fisheries on October 2DJI, it may be of interest to mention that the French Government voted the sum of .C 12,009 for tlio extermination of rats and mice in agricultural districts in France. Danysz virus, prepared at the Pasteur Institute, Paris, was the means officially employed, and thanks to the timely measures taken, the farmers obtained complete and speedy relief from tho troublesome pest, by which their crops had been pre- viously devastated. Danysr virus, it may be added, bets been awarded a silver medal at the Fracco-British</p>
      </div>
