      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>ST. PETER'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. PETER'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Annual Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>1 St. Peter's schoolroom, John-street, presented an attractive scene on Monday afternoon when the annual ti sale of work in aid of parochial organizations was held. 11 The stalls, which were heavily laden with all manner , of useful and fancy goods, were tastefully arranged. - They were in charge of the following :—Vicarage stalls : ' Mesdames Percival, Taylor, Garnett, Heacock ; and the ' Mies Lily Percival and Horton. Refreshment stall : ; Mesdames Barnforth, Yeomans, Webster; and Miss Yeomans. Miscellaneous stall : Mesdames Steel and . Cowden. Flower stall : Mrs. Clayton and the Misses Young. Sweet stall : Miss Percival and Miss Hughes ; and bran tub, Miss Dolly Percival. - The sale was opened on Monday by Mrs. Robert ' rierpoint, who was accompanied by %Ir. Pierpoint, and , amongst others present were :—The Mayoress, the Rev. B. Percival (vicar of the parish), the Rev. J. EL Garnett (curate), the Revs. IL V. Pigott, B. C. R. Carleton, If. B. Firth, and J. H. G. Hollingworth, Mr. John IVhite, Mr. J. Ranaforth (vicar's warden), and Councillor Steel (people's warden. Cmncill or PuLTov-Sxt urn who presided, and explained that the ohjont, of the s tlo was to augment the funds of the church. At a tuns like the pres ent one could not help thinking how important it Was that their church organization should have the hearty support of the people. They welts going through a crisis vzhich was threatening the very strongholds of Christianity itself. They were threatened with A departure under a fancied name called `• Socialism," which, when the mask was taken off, was nothing more nor less than anarchy, and the upsetting of all law and order ; and, unfortunately, the disregard cf religion They could not afford to do without religion, and therefore, the strong efforts which had been made to banish it out of their schools imposed a double dray upon Churchmen to uphold the principle that their children should he brought up in the faith of their fathers. ( toplause.) The Rev. B. PERCIVAL thanked afl who were giving their serviees to promote the success of the sale of work, an eanhined that it stun of a little over ma WEIS siren last 3 esr as the result of the sale to various organiz dious in the parish. Their needs were not less than they had been in the past. The religious battle with regard to the schools was not yet over, and it never could he over so far SA the Church was concerned and they meant to fight fot their religion in their day Ischools, and also for their dear old Church. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>TUESDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>The bazaar was re-opened on Tuesday afternoon by Mrs. James Pail-dough, and among others prrsent, were- the Rev. B. Percival, the Rev. .1. H. Garnett, the Rev. I W. Bracecamp, and Mr. Baniforth (vicar's warden). I The Rev. B. PERCIVAL announced that the sales or the previous day, including a donation from Lady. Greenall, amounted to .C.SS 10s. The Wallop of Man- chester, said the Vicar, had sr,id that "Sin was n moan— !thin which barred the path to the Promised Land." They had to battle with that difficulty in that parish, ! and during the winter, they had organized cottage ' meetings. fie believed that if they got two or three people together in a cottage they did a great deal towards reforming them, because they got them to I listen to the Wotd of God. They wanted to keep the people right in regard to character, and nith regard tcs industrial affairs they would all come round to their position by degrees. Mrs. FAtitcLouc emphmised the r ecessity of the sale in a poor parish like St. Peter's, and wished it every possible success. (Applause). , On the motion of Mr. BAMFORTH, seconded by the ' Rev. J.. H. GARNETT, Mrs. Fairdough was warmly : thanked for her services.</p>
