        <p>AMAN'd VALUH is not always to be estimated by the position he holds. We know this, and we want to hear from men who are, and con convince us that they are, capable of occupying Higher-Grade and more Responsible Positions than they now have. We have hundred* of such positions open—positions carrying eateries in accordance with Ability, Perhaps you can fill one of them. Write us to-day.—HAPGOODB, L 1 D., (Registered in Guernsey) 24A. Barton Arcade, Deansgate, Manchester.</p>
        <p>It is probable that not more MR. TAFT'S than one . Englishman in VICTORY. ten will ever bring himself</p>
        <p>to understand the intricaci is of an election to the American presidency. But every Englishman should know enough of American politics to be able to appreciate the certainty with which the "machine " that we affect to despise brings the good men to the top. So much of the process of electing the next American President as has been carried out so far leaves the ultimate result no longer in doubt. Mr. TAFT is as certain to succeed Mr. ROOSEVELT if he lives into the Spring of next year as the coming comet is to swing round the sun in due season, and even his political opponents will not allege that his election will be otherwise than honourable to the public spirit, and to the dis- cernment of the United Slates. Mr. TAFT is a man of high character, of great ability, and of unquestioned public spirit. But so, also, is his opponent, who will probably now finally disappear from the front rank of American politicians. Whether the popular verdict had gone in favour of Mr. BRYAN or of Mr. TAFT there would have been nothing to be ashamed of. The American political machine is not entitled to any extravagant. compliments,but it works perfectly in one respect. Out of all the eighty millions of American citizens it selects a President who seems to fit the position exactly. Though Presidential elections culminate in a perfect whirlwind of speech-making, it is not by oratorical talent that the contest is decided Mr. BRYAN is admittedly a much finer orator than Mr. TAFT. We saw something of him in England recently, and it was impossible to listen to him without being convinced not only of his high talent, but of his lofty character, and his intimate grasp of the higher issues of politics. But when the oratory was left out he had no superiority in other respects over Mr. TAFT. DANIEL WEB TER was the greatest orator that America has ever produced, but he tried twice for the Presidency and failed. What we insist on, however, is that the people of the United States always elect a President who is not only of unpeachable public character,but who is capable of maintaining that character throughout his whole period of office. There have been only two doubt- ful men among the American Presidents. Neither of them was ever clearly convicted of corruption, and each of them advanced from the Vice- Presidency on the death of his chief, and was never, therefore, aubjected to the ordeal of a popular election. The great conflict between Mr. TAFT and Mr. BRYAN illustrates two things very vividly. One is the rapid growth of the electorate of the United States. President GARFIELD had only what is called on the other side of the Atlantic a " plurality" of &lt;59 votes in the Electoral College, but Mr. TAFT, who in this respect will be far below President ROOSEVELT, will have 145. The plurality "in that stage of Presidental election which was competed on Wednesday, is now counted in millions, where, at times easily within memory, it was counted in thousznds. The other notable circumstance is that though there may be no great questions thr own into the political cauldron, and, in fact, not one great difference of principle, as in this instance, the country is divided in opinion by what is almost like a colour line. Even the difference on fiscal questions between Datnocrats and Republicans has almost disappeared, but "the solid South," nevertheless, remains "the solid South," and this in spite of the importation of a vast new popula- tion.</p>
        <p>Warrington people know MR. CROSFIELD AND quite well that Mr. A. H. THE BOWLERS. Crosfield, M.P., is an</p>
        <p>enthusiastic sportsman and he is never happier than when encouraging some branch of wholesome recreation. The bowlers of Lancashire and Cheshire have shown their admira tion for him by making him their President and on Saturday evening he again presided over the annual dinner of the association in Manchester. Attended by " trundlers " from a large area— several of them came from Worcestershire and Staffordshire—the gathering was a complete success and afforded unmistakable evidence of the fast spreading popularity of the ancient game. Mr. Crosfield had to make many presentations, not the least gratifying being to hand the Crosfield cup, won by the Warrington Bull's Head team in the Lancashire and Cheshire Club Charepionship tourney, to the proud victors. We have before congratulated our townsmen on their notable victory and the satisfaction felt at the achieve- ment must have been enhanced when they were able to receiue the trophy at the hands of their Member who has always taken the liveliest interest in the fortunes of the bowling clubs of Warrington. Another very noteworthy feature of the gathering was the handsome recognition of the services of Mr.J.W.G. Coombs, vice-president, who has worked unre- mittingly for the association for twenty-one years. During the evening Mr. Crosfield delivered several happy speeches in which he referred to the striking advance of bowls in England, and particularly in the Northern counties, and we heartily agree with him that this development of outdoor recreation will be approved by all right-thinking 'men. In these days of competition and high pressure in commerce and industry, it is more than ever necessary that the health and physique of the nation should be maintained at its best, and the game of bowls, which affords such pleasant exercise tin the fresh air, must do untold good in that direction. Mr. Crosfield also put in a word for the Daylight Saving Bill which would undoubtedly, if passed into law, be a boon to bowlers and all summer sportsmen in placing more time Ifor recreation at their disposal when the days are long and bright,</p>
        <p>The Licensing Bill has made PUBLib-Ronan AND steady and valuable pro- ELECTIONS. gress in Committee this week. Clauses reducing Sunday hours from six to three, and eonferring power on the magistrates to enforce entire Sunday Closing on renewal in places where public-houses are redundant, have been passed by big majorities. The barmaids have been reprieved, and we think Mr. Asquith was right in making a graceful con- cession on their behalf. There is, possibly, a stronger public opinion in favour of, than against, the employment of women in public bars, and there is certainly much to be said on both sides. But probably the most interesting feature of the week's legislation is the promise by the Premier to wake the closing of all public-houses on Parliamentary Election days compulsory by statute, instead of leaving the matter to the discretion of the magistrates. This is a very valuable and necessary reform, which was concurred in by Mr. Balfour who deplored "any extra cause of excitement being added to tbat necessarily engendered by the state of public opinion at such times." We thank the Ex-Premier for such an honest expression of opinion, particu- larly as, in the main, it will be against the interests of the party he represents now that it has become so closely allied with the Brewing Trade. There are few elections in big working-class constitu- encies which are not influenced to some extent by beer. Votes can be, and have been, bought by beer—and in ninety-nine CaE.OB out of a hundred the votes have been cast for the Tory nominee. Moreover, it is the usual thing now-a-days to consider every public-house a committee-room for the Tory candidate, so that Mr. Balfour was giving away something in the nature of a party asset when he blessed the statutory closing of public-houses on Parliamentary election days. Colonel Hall Walker made it quite clear that he, at any rate, was of a different opinion from his leader. He feared the closing of public-houses would make elections unpopular, and added with nnconscious humour that "he wanted to encourage voters to go to the poll." They have been encouraged too often in the past to go to tha poll via the public-house. In future we shall have sober electors, which will be an immense gain to the comniucity. Our only regret is that the statute is not made to apply to municipal as well as Parliamentary elections.</p>
        <p>Liberals and Conservatives I THE MUNICIPAL share the honours of the ELECTIOWS. two contests fought so I vigorously in Warrington, inasmuch as each side has won a seat. The sufferers in both cases are the L sbour and Socialist party, and the result deprives Labour of any representation on the Council, which, we think, is to be deplored. In some degree we feel regret that the Liberal Party has had a share in bringing about this result, but the circumstances in Orford Ward were such as to merit their inter- vention. It was generally aimited that the Labour nominee, Mr. Hampson, had no chance of beating Mr. Rush worth, the publican, and stepping into ex-Councillor Stott's vacant seat. The Liberals were obliged to intervene in order to prevent the Tories and the Trade securing another representative, and thanks to the magnificent fight made by Mr.—now Councillor—Peter Peacock, they succeeded. It was no small feat to win this three-cornered contest, but Councillor Peacock went about it in a way which appealed forcibly to the electorate. He said plainly what he meant and he meant what he said, even for instance when he stated in the counting room and on the Town Hall steps that "the result of the election would give notice to the brewers of Warrington that they had got enough of them in the Town Council." Mr. W. Peter Rylands described the result as galling, and expressed his surprise that the electors had not voted for Mr. Rushworth as against Mr. Paacock. There are none so blind as those who will not see! We are proud to congratulate Councillor Peacock on having defied the Trade and beaten it flat to the ground. In thus associating Liberalism with Temperance he has shown the way to curb the power of the drink traffic in Warrington. We cannot credit St. John's Ward with having chosen either wisely or well. In rejecting Mr. James Ball they have unseated one of th 3 most intelligent, sincere, and progressive members of the Council. Mr. Ball did remarkably well —considering the organised political opposition —in polling fill votes, but it seems to us that having been so near winning he ought to have won. It was not to Mr. Ball's advantage that he stood as a Socialist—the label may be an asset someday—and he was further handicapped by the lack of organisation amongst his supporters. Individual effort, however determined and sus- tained, can hardly hope to prevail against massed battalions such as the Tory party brought to the support of Mr. Lashford, the young butcher of Chorley-street. By the way, there is another trade which may now be considered quite adequately represented on the Town Council—and that is the butchering and meat-selling trade. It is not advisable that the Town Council should be composed of little cliques of individuals repre- senting certain trades, because the suspicion would be unavoidable that they were there more in the interests of their trade than of the ratepayers generally. Civic government can only inspire confidence when the administrative body is com- posed of men whose bona fides areself-evident and whose public probity is unquestioned. The War- rington Town Council will only step from old troubles into new ones if any further extension of the baneful principle of trade or sectional repre- sentation is tolerated.</p>
      </div>
