      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Those people who have not attended at least two sales of work this week are fortunate in their day and generation. .</p>
        <p>* * Many of the people who called Mr. Ball an Atheist and shouted so loudly about Religious Liberty had probably never been inside a place of worship since the day they were married.</p>
        <p>* * Councillor Fulton-Smith not unnaturally sympa- thises with mine host of the" Horse and Jockey " on his defeat. At the next election he says they must " return a stout Conservative." We would suggest " extra-stout "—if not bitter ! * * * It is always difficult for the municipal candidate tp gauge exactly- the kind of appeal which will move the elector. Said Mr.Septimus Smith,a Blackpool candidate addressing an election meeting : " I have never drunk nor smoked, and have never walked out with a woman in my life." He received twenty-three votes. Mr. Smith was evidently too unique for Blackpool.</p>
        <p>* * The Fire Fiend was an unwelcome participant in the Fifth of November revels in Warrington. He applied his torch to no less than three places of business on Thursday evening and beat all competitors in making bonfire night thoroughly realistic. The firemen had a particularly anxious and unpleasant evening. * * * Mr. Victor Grayson's latest charge against the members of the House of Commons, made at Tower Hill the other morning, is one of the most surprising, and, at the same time, one of the most serious of the many that have come from him. He said :—" They wear white shirts to conceal the fact that they have nothing behind their foreheads."—Mr. Grayson has talked about shirts before, but he has not hitherto disclosed such intimate acquaintance with their manifold uses. * * *</p>
        <p>Mr. Asquith has been more fortunate in his Rectorial contest than Mr. Lloyd George and Mr. Churchill, for on Saturday the Prime Minister was elected Lord Rector of the University *of Aberdeen. His opponent was Sir Edward Carson, and he had to face a Suffragette element in the woman students. Even so, Mr. Asquith wins with a majority in three out of the four nations, and a majority of 64 on a poll of 804 (the total number of electors is 891). Mr. Asquith was defeated when he stood in 1902, and his victory now is, personally and politically, a notable mark of appreciation. * *</p>
        <p>Quite a feature of the thyor's work has bcen the assiduity with which be has attended to public engagements. In fact nothing but ill-health and the difficulty of being in two places at once lus eTer</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>caused him to refuse a reasonable invitation. During his two years of office which close this week, I Alderman Smethurst has made 679 attendances at lTown Council and Committee meetings; 241 attend- ances as Chief Magistrate at the Police Court • and 210 public engagements; grand total 1130. This is a record of work which any future mayor will find a difficulty in surpassing. * * The truth of the following incident at a Warrington elementary school is vouched for. The other day the teacher of an infant class was giving a reading lesson, and for the benefit of one small boy she was explain- ing how necessary it was that he should be able to read newspapers and books when he grew up. A little girl of six chimed in with the clinching argu- ment that unless he could read the papers when he was a man "he would not know which horse to put hi money on." As a sample of juvenile depravity this would be hard to beat. And what a commentary, too, on the influence of a certain class of newspapers! * * *</p>
        <p>To what depths or Conservative friends will sink in order to gain votes was illustrated in the closing stages of Monday's municipal contests. It is playing the game of politics decidedly low down when lurry loads of children have to be carted about in the sacred name of " Religious Liberty." In both St. John's and Orford Wards lurries laden with young children were to be seen bearing the fatuous appeal " Daddy, vote for me, I want to be taught the same religion as you !" When one reflects upon the history of the Tory party in respect to the liberties of the people one is reminded of the illustration which depicts Satan reproving Sin. * * * _ . . .</p>
        <p>It is not customary, in- modern times, for the military to take part in the enthronement of a bishop of the Church. Dr. 145aclagan, who has just resigned the Archbishopric of York, in his early days served in the army, and the circumstance was not forgotten when he was translated from Litchfield to York in 1891. Detachments of the troops quartered at York, which included the Ist Royal Dragoons and the Royal Scots, took a prominent part in the procession through the picturesque streets which accompanied the new Archbishop to the Minister, and a row of scarlet- coated officers in the choir stalls relieved the masses of white and black in those parts of the building occupied by the dergy. An elderly gentleman in the orthodox frock coat and silk hat was provided with a seat by one of the many stewards. Few knew that it was the Duke of Cambridge, then the head of the British army, who was on a visit at the time to Sir George Wombwell.</p>
        <p>* * * Mr. Joynson-Hicks, M.P. indulged in the luxury of disagreeing with Mr. Balfour in the House of Commons on Wednesday when the provisions of the Licensing Bill lespecting clubs were being considered. He supported the Government vigorously, and desired the law respecting clubs to be strengthened. To under- stand what happened it is necessary to remember that Mr. Joynson-Hicks for a good many years was chiefly known as an enthusiast in Temperance Reform. When the Church of England Temperance Society wanted a spokesman before a Royal Commission, Mr. Joynson-Hicks was their man, hotly in favour of a time limit of (if we are not mistaken) as little as five years. Then came the time when Mr. Joynson-Hicks found himself a Conservative candidate at a by-election in Manchester, with one of the issues the present Licensing Bill. Mr. Joynson-Hicks did not hesitate to denounce the Bill in a manner that made him wholly acceptable to the Trade, and he defeated Mr. Churchill largely as the result of Trade activity. But, of course, the publicans do not like the clubs, and in that simple fact Mr. Joynson-Hicks saw his chance of reviving his past. On Wednesday he appeared as a Temperance Reformer (his original ' part). He "did not agree " with Mr. Balfour, who I perhaps will not wholly misunderstand the position, since the Opposition Leader's knowledge of Man- cheater is extensive and peculiar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>[FROSI OUR LONDON CORRZSPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>Quiet but substantial progress with the Licensing Bill has been the chief feature of the week in dome stic politics. Sunday, November Ist, was a notable day, since it was exactly fifty years since the Proclamation by the Queen in Council to the Princes, Chiefs and People of India, was published,setting forth the ideals cherished in this country concerning the government of India, when its adminstration was transferred from the East India Company to the Crown. King Edward, as Emperor of India, marked the occasion by a message to the people of India, praising the loyalty of the Princes and chiefs find the valour and fidelity of the native troops, while it announced a remission of sentences and liberal measures of reform. The remarkable " interview " ' with the Kaiser, which appeared last week in the " Daily Telegraph," con- tine to excite widespread discussion and surmise in Continental politics.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>SUNDAY CLOSING.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY CLOSING.</p>
        <p>Last Friday the clause in the Licensing Bill which reduces the time for the sale of intoxicating liquour on Sunday in England from six to three hours was carried by 237 votes to 55, the Government intimat- ing that on the Report stage the clause would be applied to London either as it stood or with some modification of a minor sort. It was, however, made perfectly clear by Mr. Asquith on Monday that the legitimate conduct upon its present lines of the business of hotel and restaurant keepers would not be affected. Where a person enters a hotel or restaurant for the purpose of being supplied with a meal, the present law will remain in force and no additional restrictions will be imposed. One of the outstanding features of Friday's debate was the hearty welcome given by the Labour members to the limita- tion of the hours of Sunday opening. Mr. W. Johnson, for example, who proclaimed himself to be neither "an abstainer" nor "one of the 'Temperance wolves,' ' declared that, if the Bill were passed with this limitation in it, it would still be quite possible for the working man to obtain liquor on Sundays, and one hour in the middle of the day and two at night would be ample for the working classes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Iltii. BALFOUR'S ATTITUDE.</head>
        <p>Iltii. BALFOUR'S ATTITUDE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Balfour, although he adopted a critical attitude towards the Government proposal, admitted that in Scotland great value was attached to Sunday closing, which had existed in the country for more than fifty years. He thought it very likely that the great majority of the population in Wales took the same view, and recalled that, when he was Chief iecretary for Ireland, Sunday closing was supported by every class in Ireland. More important still was his concession that he would approve of licensed premises being closed during Parliamentary elections—a concession of which Mr. Asquith promptly and adroitly availed himself. The Lord Advocate justly ridiculed the contention put forward by some of the opponents of a restriction of the hours on Sunday during which public-houses might be open that this was, as he phrased it, " a provision for compulsory gulping.' "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>EMPLOYMENT OF BARMAIDS.</head>
        <p>EMPLOYMENT OF BARMAIDS.</p>
        <p>It is obviously not in the interests of any social reform that legislation towards it should be pressed in advance of the main body of public opinion. Con- sequently, although the employment of women in the sale of intoxicating liquor cannot be regarded as an ideal state of things, the decision of the Government on Monday to abandon the proposal that the licensing justices should have power to make it a condition of the renewal of an off-licence that women should not be employed on the premises as barmaids, was prudent and well-advised in the interests of a great measure of reform, which it is desirable not to over- load with controversial matter. At the same time Mr. Asquith pointed out that more elasticity was needed in the working of the licensing law, and that, till the Act of 1904, justices had the power to impose any conditions they chose on the granting of new i licenees ; and even under that it s competent for licensing justices to make the condition for a new licence, as opposed to a renewal, that barmaids should not be employed.</p>
        <p>- - THE CASE OF CHILDREN. The proviso in regard to children was on a different footing. It was not because public opinion is not rips for such a restriction, but because sufficient powers are already existent, enabling local authorities to make by-laws under the Employment of Children Act, 1903, prohibiting their employment in public-houses under fourteen, and in view of the stringent provision for- bidding the presence of children under this age in the bars of public-houses, that the provision was deleted in as far as it concerned children. In other words, it was only a convenience of drafting which was here consulted, as it was unnecessary to give powers to Justices to make a prohibition under this head when they could do the same thing in a more effectual way. FURTHER EXTENSION OF SUNDAY CLOSING.</p>
        <p>Another condition of renewal that justices are empowered to make under Clause 20 is prohibition of</p>
        <p>sale for consumption on the premises during the whole or any part of Sunday. The Committee had already agreed that a considerable restriction of the hours during which public-houses should be open oil Sundays ought to be made the general law of the land. As there is no necessity, in certain localities both urban and rural, where there is a redundant number of licensed houses, that all should be open during the whole or any of the hours which the Bill permits, the licensing justices may fairly be allowed to say that the restrictions imposed by the general law should be still further extended.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>SALUTARY REFORM</head>
        <p>A SALUTARY REFORM</p>
        <p>A change of salutary effect in the direction not only of temperance, but of bringing a better tone into political contests, undoubtedly consists in the closing of public houses on polling days for Parliamentary elections, as a matter of compulsory enactment. Mr. Asquith caught eagerly at the admission by the Leader of the Opposition, which was made on Friday, that he would not oppose compulsory closing at elections. The result was that, instead of the ques- tion whether public-houses should be closed or not being left to the discretion of the licensing justices, it will now be a statutory necessity. Mr. Balfour gave an unqualified approval to a step which, as he said, would prevent any extra cause of excitement being added to that necessarily engendered by the state of public opinion at such times. MONOPOLY VALUE.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday an amendment was moved by the Solicitor-General defining the monopoly value end providing that it should be assessed by the Com- missioners of Inland Revenue, and not by the licensing justices. The amount to be paid as monopoly value is fixed on precisely the same principle as the amount to be paid as compensation. Mr. Younger, who of course speaks on behalf of "the Trade," was prepared to acknowledge that "the Government had made la not altogether unsuccessful effort to define what monopoly ought to be." He could scarcely be expected to make an explicit avowal that the basis of compensa- tion, which had been so fiercely attacked by the friends of the Trade," was equitable, yet it is difficult, if not possible, for the plain man, unbiassed , eitner for or against the vested interests affected, to understand how the basis of monopoly value can be fair, as Mr. Younger granted, while the basis of compensation—determined on precisely the same principles —could be unfair, as Mr. Younger claimed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events. At Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church to-morrow (Sunday) special services in connection with the Wesley Guild will be held. The Rev. W.R. Fitzgerald will preach. On Monday evening the Circuit Guild rally will take place. The Rev. T. Travers Herford, of Stand, will preach at Cairo-street Chapel to-morrow (Sunday).:Subjects : Morning, " Religion and Social Service" ; evening, "Faith and Fear."</p>
        <p>_ Band of Hope Union. Public meeting in Parr Hall to-morrow (Sunday) evening. Scottish concert at the Parr Hall on Monday evening. Free Church Council annual public meetings ; Golborne-street Church and Bold-street School ots Thursday after noon and evening. Musical Society concert at the Parr Hall ott Thursday evening. Rev. F. Ballard will lecture on " Christianity and Socialism" at Bold-street Churclt on Monday November 23rd. 1 A Pair of " DOULTON ORNAMENTS make a choice _</p>
        <p>and acceptable present. Stock ranges from Sc. to £5 per pair.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Cuerden Hall Sold. Cuerden Hall, Thelwall, was put up for sale by auction at the Lion Hotel,:on Wednesday. The highest bid was £l3lO, but we understand that the Hall was afterwards sold privately to Mr. George Simcock, of the "Dog and Dart" Inn, Grappenhall, for £l9OO. A Thelwall Bankrupt. Before Mr. Registrar Ridgway at the Warrington County Court yesterday a Receiving Order was made on the application of Mr. T. S. Steel, solicitor, against James Clayton, Commission agent, of "Laurel Bank Weaste-lane, Tkelwall, on the debtor's petition. He was also adjudicated Bankrupt. Increasing Veterans' Pensions. Following the suggestion of Lord Roberts that local efforts should be made to increase the pensions of Crimean and Indian Mutiny Veterans to 13s. per week a local committee took the project in hand and succeeded in raising £6B. At a meeting held on Wednesday it was agreed to increase the pensions of local veterans to 13s. per week and also to endeavour to induce them to agree to the payment of the pensions in weekly amounts by the Committee instead of receiving them quarterly from the Post Office. There are about seven local veterans to whom the increase will apply. Presentation to Mr. H. A. Thompson. Mr. Thompson who for 30 years was the Midland Railway Company representative in Warrington was the recipient on sth inst., of a silver mounted ebony walking stick with inscription, from the Warrington Railway Agents.—Mr. Townley, the London and North-Western Agent, in making the presentation ott behalf of the agents, spoke of the very high regard ill which Mr. Thompson was held not only by the rail- way Agents, but by the traders ;generally, and trusted he would be long spared to enjoy a life of leisure. Mr. Thompson feelingly responded.</p>
        <p>SALE of Surplus Stook of Boots and Shoes now on.—PUBLIC BENEFIT BOOT Co.—Advt. Domestic Servant's Claim.</p>
        <p>_________ At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour, Judge Shiress Will, K.C., Clara Knight, domestic servant, sued Samuel Wilson, 27, Golborne-street, for £1 2s. arrears of wages and board and lodging alleged to be due to her.—Mr. Steel appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. Browne for the defendant —P laintiff stated that on September 24th there was some unpleasantness between Mrs. Wilson and herself and she gave a week's notice. At night the defendant came home and when informed of what had occurred he told plaintiff she had better clear out. _ She went the next day for her wages and he refused to pay her. She denied having made use of bad language.—Defendant's case was that one of the lodgers had complained about her bed not having been made for three days and Mrs. Wilson spoke to the plaintiff who came out with a foul expression and said the complaint ought to have been made to the servant and not to the mistress. She said she would leave at once. Although she was told that unless she served a week's notice she would not get any wages, plaintiff persisted in leaving. Defendant did not turn her out. —His Honour gave judgment for the defendant with costs. CHESHIRE FED BA.CON from sd. lb.at ASHLEY'S.—Advt. Ellesmere-street Bible Class. The members of the Ellesmere-street Wesleyan Bible Class held a social gathering at Bold-street schoolroom on Tuesday, evening. After tea a splendid programme was submitted. Chairman Rev. T. Miller, accompanist, Mr. W. Barnes.—Violin solo (a); " Melody " (Brewer), (c) " Shepherd's Dance ', (German), Mr. R. H. Pearson ; songs, " Margarita;, and " Only," Mr. Joseph Hannan ; songs, " Smiles and "Shepherd's Cradle Song," Miss Avice Beswick ; pianoforte solo, " Sonato ' (Beethoven), Ur. J. A- Pownall ; song, " Nivana," Mr. E. Roberts ; song, 1" Devout Lovers" Mr. W. Dutton.—The artistes were I well received, both the singing and the instrumental part of the concert belug of a first-class order. During the evening an interesting ceremony took place in the nature of a presentation to Mr. John Barlow (teacher of the Young Men's Class) of an inscribed ipla pendant. in appreciation of his regular and valued service. The presentation was made by Mr. W' Wareham, who in a few choice remarks asked Mr. Barlow to accept the pendant from the members of the class ; Mr. F. Cos, Mr. J. Taylor, Mr. Levi Richardson supporting, who also added testimony to the value 01 Mr. Barlow as a leader.—Mr. Barlow feelingly replied' He said he was in rather an awkward position, as this presentation had taken him completely by surprise. How ever, he would treasure the gift as one from a 'body of young men of whom he was proud to have the honour of being their teacher. • The Rev. T. Miller added a few words, and a pleasant evening closed et 9.45 with the Doxology. OUGHT ITo USE GLASS ES .—ln order to answer this question it is necessary to test the sight. This is doll. and all informati on given willingly.—Joarlsn- WATSON, EYESIGHT TESTING ROOMS, 84, Bridge-stree,..." Warrington. Priem' including testing : steel spectsclr. from 2s. 6d., Rolled Gold from is. 6., Solid Oold frO 218.—A dvt,</p>
        <p>1 i Warringtan Free Church Council. The annual meetings of the Free Church Council Will , 11 be held on Thursday next. Dr. J. T. Marshall, M.A., President Elect of the Baptist Union of Great Britain and Ireland, will preach in the afternoon at - four o'clock in the Baptist Church, Golborne-street. All Free Church people should make an effort to hear Dr. Marshall. After the sermon there will be a public - tea in Bold-street Wesleyan schoolroom, to be followed -- at 7.30 by a Great Public Meeting at which addresses - will be delivered by Rev. James Watkin of Liverpool, Hey. R. Rainey Workman of Manchester, and Rev. R. E. Letheren, Warrington. The President (C. E. Parker, Esq., J.P:) will preside. PUBLIC BENEFIT BOOT CO.—Sale of Surplas Stock now OR. UNDER THE GLOM—Advt. , 'Suspicious Behaviour. At the Warrington Police Court on Thursday - William Kelly, who stated he was a baker from •, Manchester, was charged with being a suspecte . Person found loitering in Sankey-street with intent to ' commit a felony. It was stated by the Chief Constable , that shortly before mid-night on Saturday last Police Constable Wood was examining the property in ' 'Sankey-street. When near the entry adjoining the - Shop of Mr. Baker, jeweller, he saw prisoner with his band on the window at the back of the premises. In reply to the officer's questions, prisoner replied that he was going to break into the shop. He was ' slightly under the; influence of drink. The magistrates committed him to gaol for two months. Warrington Women's Circle. Mrs. Joseph Harrison took the choir at the Circle meeting on Sunday, she was assisted by Mrs. Broad- hurst and Miss Lizzie Heywood. The address was given by Miss Bessie Dutton, who, after expressing her interest in the new movement and her pleasure at being a vice-president of the Circle touched on the fact that a Circle had a surrounding as well as a lecture. Jesus Christ in the centre but also uncom- passing round about. She told lan Maclaren's little sketch 'His Mother's Sermon ' to illustrate the power . , of the simple gospel. The art of painting helped to ..: illustrate filling in the blank canvas of life. Every • .struggle gives more strength for the next attack, the - difficulties tune and tone us to go forward. With the account of her recent boating accident, some spiritual , lessons were interwoven. It was a great disappoint• • ment to the meeting that Miss Bridge was absent -through Hitless, but Miss Cissie Hankey took her - place and sang. " Far from my heavenly home " and " The Promised Land." BRASS AND COPPER HEARTH SIEITES, see window display for new and artistic patterns.—J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge-street, Warriugton.—Advt. improvements in Bridge-street. The first shop in the remodelled Bridgostreet is the architecturally fine building which has been planned bY Messrs William and Segar Owen for the business use and occupation of Mr. T. J. Lee who, for some . time past carrying on his trade temporarily at tho ; Horsemarket-street corner of Market-gate, will open the new premises on Saturday next as per advertise- ment on the front page of this issue. The new building besides presenting a handsome exterior to Bridge-street, is internally fitted up with the very best " and up-to-date essentials for the transaction of a r Prosperous and growing business, the ground-floor sPace being particular light and airy, while the windows and display portion are replete with the most raodern contrivances. Above the shop and approached , bY a separate and commodious staircase are rooms of : a most attractive character suitable for offices. The Whole building is well-worthy of association with the TerY superior class of architecture which has arisen within the past decade between Rylands-street and Sankey-street. - CRESHIRE FED BACON from sd. lb.at 4.SHLEY'S.—Advt. Scottish Concert. The forthcoming Scottish Concert announced for Monday promises to be a great success both musically , and financially. The programme is a splendid one , and represents the very best artistes that are to be found in Scotland. Miss Jessie Mc'Lachlan, who tops the bill, is in the very first flight of sopranos, and her gaelic contributions are sure to be a source of great , , pleasure to her highland compatriots. Madame Marian Christie is a most accomplished contralto, and . comes with a fine repution. Mr. Robert Burnett is a Most finished artiste, and is known in London and the provinces to all the musical world, as a baritone of great promise and attainment. This artist has been ' engaged for one of the forthcoming Musical Society's concerts. Mr. George Neil is the possessor of a • magnificent tenor voice, and has just returned from a snccessful tour in the Straits and Canada. Mr. Theo. Crozier, the solo violinist, is a young player who is , rapidly gaining distinction in his profession, and was recently the subject of flattering criticism in the London press. Mr. Craighall Sherry, who will supply the humorous element, is a most refined artiste, who is certain to capture his audience by his quaint, pawky humour, and his musical abilities which are of a very high order. The proceeds of the concert are in aid of the Warrington Infirmary and the Associa- tion's benevolent fund, and a good house is confidently anticipated. SALE isieW ON. UNDER THE CLOCK. Surplus . Stock of Boots and Shoes.—Advt. Important Compensation Point. At the Warrington County Court on Thursday before his Honour Judge Shiress Will, K.C., there • 'was down for hearing an application for arbitration 4 proceedings under the Workmen's Compensation Act, 000. The applicant was George Cole, 1, Garner- , - street, Warrington (for whom Messrs. Riley, Kelly and Picton, St. Helens, acted), and the respondents, , the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., Who were represented by Mr. F. G. Taylor (Messrs. Ilenry Greenall and Co., Warrington). The applies- was in respect of an accident which happened to • toe applicant whilst he was engaged as a labourer at he Dallam-lane works. He was breaking up old en when a sledge hammer fell upon his right thumb. he company opposed the application on the ground that Cole was a member of the Pearson and Knowles • .-1„ron and Engineering Department Mutual Accident insurance Society, whereby he had contracted out . cf the Act of 1906 under a scheme duly registered Mader the Act with the Registrar of Friendly Societies. ----Mr. Taylor stated that Cole's case had already been arbitrated upon by the committee of the above society - . Which was solely composed of workmen in the employ , 91 the company and under the scheme the only cases •In which an application could possibly be made to ~ tile County Court were those of a fatal accident or _ Permanent incapacity. The company denied that Cole .a.as permanently incapacitated. Cole had now signed . an acknowledgment admitting that he was a member of . the society and as such had contracted out of the •Act and he accepted the decision of the committee arrived at in May 1908 to the effect that he was not entitled -. to further compensation, and he abandoned the pro- , ceedings against the company in the County Court. The company had, however,given Cole the sum of £2O out of which he had to bear his own costs. The company did not ask for costs against Cole. Rams ARE CEIEAPER 6d per lb. at A SHLET'S.—Advt. , Hard Swearing. • His Honour Judge Shiress Will, K.C., made some i strong remarks in a case at the Warrington County .. Court on Thursday in which William Kinder, Higher - Whitley, sued John Duffern, Comberbach, for £2 10s., 00e quarters' rent for Grove Cottage, Higher Whitley, , and S-4 16s. damages alleged to have been done to a • stable adjoining. There was a counter-claim by the , defendant f,r work done and expenses of sending out , Washing.—Mr. A. J. Willett appeared for the plaintiff ' and Mr: Browne for the defendant.—The case for the , plaintiff was that he let the house to Duffern for £lO • a Year. When defendant had paid a first quarter's rent in January last lie ailed for a washhouse to be built. Plaintiff said he had not money .to do it St the time but as the defendant continued to Press him he gave him notice in March. . . 1311 leaving, the defeadant only paid one quarters' Tent instead of two.—Plaintiff admitted that the rent Of the house prior to defendent going into possession ' was £8 a year.—Defendant said the reason the house t ,Ti'as raised £2 a year was that plaintiff promised to . build a washhouse and actually pointed out the site -. to defendant before he took possession. Owing to plaint i ff's subsequent refusal to build the waslohouse th'e washing had to be sent out. Plaintiff also , promised to pay for the labour of a man in making a ' °,?tPath and on that assurance he parted with Cs. 3d. •.- Zuich had never been repaid. -His Honour said there , ad been much cross-swearing in the ease and it was rrfectly evident that one side had committed perjury. • ;,._, .1.! Was utterly impossible to reconcile the two stories. 'e was influenced by the fact that the rent of the ,• Z'ttage for the past 35 years was £B, and he believed L'' was raised because a promise was given that a . lalslohouse would be provided. He gave judgment for -, Iltendant on the claim and counter-claim, allowing ta,!na £2 gs. for the expenses incurred in sending out " washing and 6s. 2d. for laying the path. ~.. For influenza and Bronchial Cougbs,Woods' Great 1. ePlaernaint Cure. Is. 11d., 2. 91—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>WIDNES ELECNONS.</p>
        <p>Polling took place in two wards in Widnes, on Monday, but by far the greater interest was centred in the fight in Victoria Ward, where Mr. Dan Garghan, the nominee of the Amalgamated Union of Labour, opposed the sitting member, Councillor Peters. The results were declared as follow : Farnworth—E. Wood 433, T. A. Bullough 227 majority 256. Victoria.—G. Garghan 372, T, G. Peters 251—majority 118.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>COLONEL HALL WALKER ON ELECTION DAY</head>
        <p>COLONEL HALL WALKER ON ELECTION DAY</p>
        <p>CLOSING.</p>
        <p>In committee on the Licensing Bill in the House of Committee on Monday Mr. Bottomley moved to delete the first sub-section, dealing with the employment of women and children in public-houses, bona-fide travellers, and closing on election days. He contended that the powers placed in the hands of the magistrates should only be exercised by Parliament, and he com- plained that by the barmaid clause 100,000 women would be thrown out of employment. Mr. Asquith said that up to 1901 the justices had always.had power to impose restrictions, and even that Act gave them power over new licences—a power which the Essex justices exercised to forbid barmaids. Having regard to the expressions of opinion that had reached him from all parts of the House, the Government would not press the provisions as to barmaids. As to closing on election days, that would be taken out of the power of the magistrates, and made a statutory provision. Mr. Balfour said the Prime Minister had shown a wise and statesmanlike discretion. Several members welcomed the announcement that closing on election days would be done by general law, and not locally by ' magistrates and Mr. A. Henderson pleaded that public- houses shotild not be opened so early in the morning, as it did immense harm near large works. Colonel Hall Walker said he thought he might say, as one who had experience of the trade, that this was not an unwelcome section. All they desired as traders was that they should be treated alike, and therefore be asked that the proposals should be embodied in the compulsory clauses. All they wanted wes that every licensc 3 should be treatedalike, and not be at the whims of some local beech of magistrates. As regarded the question of closing houses on polling day, his personal exeerience of figures taken in Liverpool was that there are absolutely no increase of takings on an election day. He stated that as a fact. (Hear, hear, and Ministerial laughter.) If the Government were going to close all public-houses on an election day, they were making that district a verj unpopular place. Did the Government wish to make Parliamentary elections las unpopular as parochial elections. (Ministerial I laughter) ? Did the number of electors voting in a county council election represent the feeling of that community? He did not think so. He wanted to encourage voters to go to the poll. (Loud laughter.) If the Government were to punish the whole of a cora- munity from getting refreshment in the legitimate manner, then elections would be unpopular. (Laughter.) He also protested against licensees being at the whim of magistrates—of cranks and fanatics—so far as the alteration of premises was concerned. After further discussion the amendment was defeated by 267 to 83.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED PERJURY AT ST. HELENS. LICENSING PROSECUTION SEQUEL</p>
        <p>A sequel to the prosecution of the licensee of the Engine Inn, Newton-road, Parr, St. Helens, wal provided at the St. Helens police court on Tuesday, when James Northey, a young man residing in Newton- road, one of the witnesses, was placed in the dock on charge of perjury.—The Chief Constable said that when giving evidence for the defence in the cese tried the previous day, the prisoner swore that at nine o'clock, when the constable said he saw two men named Higgins and Harkins leaving the premises, he was standing out- side with a certain young lady, and saw the men run away. Iramediately the statement was made, and after askilg prisoner two or three times, if it was true, he sent up to the young lady, the girl referred to, and he found that Northey's statement was absolutely untrue, and that she was not there. He asked for a remand until Wednesday.—The Chief Constable added that if bail was granted it would be prejudicial to the interests of justice. After some discussien the bench granted bail in £2OO and two sureties of £lOO each and Mr. Sharpies, the licensee of the Engine Inr and the youth's father immediately entered into recognisances. ST. HELENS MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The excitement and interest in the St. Helens municipal contests on Monday was equal almost to the stir that took place at the last general election. Seven wards,a larger number than usual, were being contested between the Labour and Co rservati ve p%rties.The Labour men had four seats to defend, and they were hopeful of increasing their representation by victories in Parr and Sutton Wards. On the other side the Conservatives had been exceptionally active, and in the campaign they had the help of Mr. Rigby Swift, the prospective candidate for the borough. On Monday the workers in the large outlying wards complained of a shortage of conveyances especially in West Sutton. The results were declared soon after nine o'clock. All the Labour candidates save one were defeated, and the party lose three seats as the net result of the polling. Messrs. Hamblett, Rudd, and Pugh, who have been very promi- nent in the Council, were unseated, and one Conserva- tive—Mr. ft. Woodward—was defeated. There are now only four Labour members in the Council. Results : North Eccleston.—W. J. Ellison (C), 922; T. Hamblett (Lab), 85 t ; majority. 63. Conservative gain. South Eccleston.—W. Woodcock (C), 9.7.8 ; A. Graham (Lab), 650 ; majority, 308. Conservative gain. West Sutton.—J. H. Bottomley (C), 674 ; A. Rudd (Lab), 523 ; majority, 151. Conservative gaiu. East Sutton.—Dr. Bates (C), 920; Dr. O'Keefe (Lab) 749 ; majority, 171. No change. Parr.—R. Wareing (Lab), 751 ; R. Woodward (C),739; majority, 15. Labour gain. Hardshaw.—R. B. Davidson (C), 817; P. Pugh (Lab) 702 ; majority, 115. Conservative gain. South Windle.—J. Haslam Fox (C), 775 ; R. W. Thomson (Lab), 411 ; majority, 364. No change.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH OF A CLERGYMAN.</p>
        <p>Rev. T. B. Waleot, curate of St. Peter's, Newton-le- Willows, died on Tuesday evening after a brief illness. He was busy in the parish on Thursday last, but suc- cumbed to an attack of influenza and pneumonia. In 1904 he was appointed as warden of St. Matthew's House, Cambridge, and occupied this position until a few months ago, when ho removed to Newton-le- Willows, where in a short time he had gained the affection and goodwill of the parishioners.</p>
        <p>WIGAN BRAKESIIAN KILLED. While engaged in shunting operations at the London and North-Western Railway goods yard at Earlestown, on Tuesday afternoon, Joseph Bibby, a Wigan brakes- man, was trapped between a waggon and the loading stage of the goods warehouse, and was carried round several- times before the engine could be stopped. He was terribly crushed about the chest, whilst his right arm was broken. He was removed to the Royal Infirmary, Liverpool, where he died shortly after admis- sion to the institution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>TEACHERS AT PLAY.</head>
        <p>TEACHERS AT PLAY.</p>
        <p>The members of the Eirlestown and District Teachers' Association held a dance and whist drive in the Town Hall. Newton, on Saturday, when about 50 members and friends attended. During the intervals of dancing, songs and recitations were contributed by several of the party. The M.C.'s were Miss Baybut and Mr. Gee, Pad Mr. Norman played for dancing. The proceeds are devoted to the Benevolent and Orphans' Fund of the National Union of Teachers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>CONGREGATIONALISTS NEW BANNER.</head>
        <p>CONGREGATIONALISTS NEW BANNER.</p>
        <p>There were interesting ceremonies connected with the unfurling of the new banner of the Newton Congre- gational Sunday school en Saturday, The day's pro- ceedings commenced with a public tea, when over 200 sat down to a well served repast. This number was further augmented during the ensuing evening,and a long entertainment was very much appreciated.—Previous to the concert the Rev. J. Davies said it was a red-letter day in the experience of some of them. Some bad been dreaming, talking or working for this evening for a long time, but now they saw their dreams realized, and their expectations fulfilled. (Applause.) It was only this year that definite steps were taken to procure this banner, and he hoped, and the teachers hoped, that they would start out with it fully free from debt. Mr. John Owen was to unfurl the banner, for since the inception of the idea he had put his heart and soul into their work. (Applause.) Mr. Owen, who on rising was heartily applauded, said he well remembered some years ago when he first followed the old banner, more years than he cared to recall. The question of superseding it with a new one had been in the minds of the teachers for a long time back. Many years ago contributions were given for this fund, but owing to the increasing wants of the school the money found its way into other charities. Last Easter the teachers formed a committee with the firm intention of getting one for the following Walking Di.y. —Mr. G. Phail said he wished in the name of teachers, to express their gratitude to all for the generous response they had made to the call. When Mr. Owen unfurled the banner all the people stood up and impressively sang " God bless our Sunday School." Several songs were subsequently given. Miss Minnie 'Barr proving an able accompanist. The banner will 'cost about £3O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>CRICKET AND BOWLING CLUB DINNER.</head>
        <p>CRICKET AND BOWLING CLUB DINNER.</p>
        <p>There WM a very enjoyable, gathering at the Pied Bull liotel, Newton, on Saturday when the members</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Examiner Saturday, November 7, 1908. TECHNICAL INSTITUTE. PRESENTATION TO PRIZE DISTRIBUTION LAST EVENING. 1 EX-SUPERINTENDENT MONAGHAN.</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, November 7, 1908. TECHNICAL INSTITUTE. PRESENTATION TO PRIZE DISTRIBUTION LAST EVENING. 1 EX-SUPERINTENDENT MONAGHAN.</p>
        <p>of the above club held their annual dinner. There was an attendance of about 70, under the presidency of Mr. A. Smith, who congratulated the team on landing the Liverpool cup and gold medals, and spoke of the successful season they had had. He hoped, with the young members who had been enrolled, that they would be more successful in the Lancashire and Cheshire League. During the evening the captain—Mr. Wadley —arrived from Manchester, and brought two Cogue medals which had been won by two of their members, viz., Mr. McCormick and Mr. Talbot. The President's prize, a gold albert, was won by Mr. J. Raynor. A cup which the club had won in previous years, and which had been put uo for the club handicap, was won by Mr. Taibof. who has won it twice in succession. A gold medal goes with it but Mr. Talbot has so many medals that a brooch was given to him for his wife. The folloa:ng prizes were also awarded :-Ist prize, pair of vases, given by Colonel Woods, won by C. Hindley; 2, silver tea pot, J. Tunscall; , 3, cruet, NV. Fairclough ; 4, biscuit barrel, T. Crampton; 5, biscuit barrel, J. Sweetlove ; 6, cruet, ft. Bent ; 7, cruet, A. Lennox ; 8, ham, H. Eloldgate ; 9, jam dish, T. Swift ; 10, cale of carvers, NV. Sutton ; 11, gent's companion case, P. Naylor ; 12, clock, D. ltylands ; 13, load of potatoes, A. Downham ; 14, clock, C. 'Mallard ; 15, silver salts, D. Davies ; 16, set of carvers, J. McCormick ; 17, set of carvers, F. Beresford ; 18, dozen of spoons, F. Adams ; 19, photo frame, NV. Sutton ; 20, oak tea tray, J. Jones. After Mr. Smith had presented the prizes a smoking concert was held, when artistes from Liverpool and the local district supplied the evening's entertainment.</p>
        <p>The annual prize distribution in connection with the Warrington School of Art, Technical Institute and Branch Technical Schools, took place at the Parr Hall last evening. There was a large attendance, Councillor Fulton-Smith, chairman of the Education Committee, presiding. Alderman AV. Scott Barrett, chairman of the Lancashire County Council, distributed the prizes. Before the meeting begae, Mr. W. H. Payton, Miss. Bac. gave an enjoyable organ recital. The CHAIRMAN emphasised the importance of con- tinuing the education received in the elementary schools which really oely awakened the intellect. At present there was a kind of internecine war between the parent, the teachers, and the employers of labour, with the result that boys were handicapped in their after life. The period between the ages of fourteen and eighteen ought to be bridged over by continuatioa schools, but he believed that only one in three children who had passed through the elementary schools took advantage of them. However repulsive compulsion might be to the average Englishman, he believed it would have to be applied and that employers of labour should only be permitted to engage young persons for a very few hours of the day, the remainder of the time to be de- voted to continuing education in the advanced schools. The DIRECTOR OF EDUCATION (rr. J. Moore Murray) submitted the annual report giving statistical information as to the successes obtained at the various schools. He pointed out that the Technical Institute now afforded facilities to young clerks to obtain a general training which qualified theta better to study the details of their business which were also dealt with at some of the classes and it was pleasing to know that so many were prepared to take advantage of them. The work of last session fully justified the Committee in providing for and requiring students to attend courses of instruction rather than single subjects. The classes were less disturbed by the indifferent attendance of those students who joined for something to do and soon tired when they were asked to work. There was a mare serious air about the class and the schools and it was no longer necessary for the teachers to pander to the students in order to maintain their classes. Alderman SCOTT BARRETT in the course of his address said the gratifying feature of the Director's report was that although the number of students in 1907-8 was less than in 1903-7 the passes had been more and the failures less in percentage, so that the schools must be doing very good work. For a long time we in England lagged behind in technical ecbica- tion. He believed the reason was that the English nation was conservatively careful in undertaking a new thing until it had had some experience of it. Conse- quently other nations were allowed to commence and carry out technical instruction long before we realized its vital importance. In poor families it was a very desirable thing that children should begin to earn money as soon as they could, but it was not everything, in fact it was very little compared with the future of the children. It should be the endeavour of educationists, to induce parents, instead of taking their children from school at a tender age and getting them to earn a few shillings a week at some- thing which would be unproductive to them in after life, to let them continue their education at the Secondary and Technical schools. At present many were sent to be shop boys or golf caddies, and when they reached the age of seventeen or eighteen they had nothing at their (Inger ends, and went to swell the ranks of the unemployed. Technical schools were intended to do away with that condition of affairs. Prizes and examinations were very good things in their way, but they were only a means to an end. Education was the principal thing. It would be an excellent thing if employers could be induced to allow their young employees to become day students. The vast expendi- ture incurred in the schools would then be much more effective. Education, concluded Alderman Scott Barrett, was not merely the three R's but the consistent development of the whole male and female character. (Applause). ALFRED SCOTT BARRETT then presented the prizes and certificates and a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to him on the proposition of the Rev. Canon STEVENSON (chairman of the Governing Body), seconded by the Rev. JOHN YONGE (member of the Governing Body). The Chairman was thanked on the proposition of Mr. HENRY ROBERTS seconded by Mr. E.J. QUEST.</p>
        <p>To show the respect in which Mr.Monaghan is held in Warrington, he was on Thursday the recipient of a number of presents from the townspeople of the place wherein he has resided for a great number of years To:mark the occasion, supper was partaken of at the Lion Hotel, and was attended by about 70 of Mr. Nionaghan's friends. He was presented with an illuminated address which is as follows : To Superintendent Monaghan. We the inhabitants of the County Borough of ‘Varring- ton have pleasure in presenting you on the occasion of your retire-a ent from the County Borough Police Force this address with a gold watch and chain, gold pendant, gold ring and other articles in recognition of the elficient and.] impartial manner in which you have discharged your duties. For 23 years, 1880 to 1903, you have been a member of the Force, and as one sligh token :of the esteem in which you are held as ta citizen,we assure you of our best wishes for your future happiness and prosperity and earnestly hope that you will be spared for many years to enjoy the rest which you have justly earned. On behalf of the subscribers. .</p>
        <p>(Signed) IL SINGLETON, Chairman of Committee. A. Brtowsz, Treasurer. 3. W. SULLEN, lion. Secretary. Councillor Singleton presided, and had on his left the Mayor, Mr. A. Browne, Mr. H. F. Greenough, Coun- cillor W. B. Roberts. On the right of the Chairman was the guest of the evening, Mr. J. Lister (Post- master), Mr. H. Isherwood, Mr. J. Lee, and Councillor Rogers. Songs were contributed by Messrs. Nield, Lea, S nith and Owen, and were very much enjoyed. The Maxon, prior to presenting the articles in ques- tion, said he felt highly honoured by being asked on a second occasion to present to Mr. Monaghan useful presents. Ile had known him for about 30 years, and during that time he had never been in his clutches. (Laughter.) He had proved himself a most expert detective, and he was one of the finest in Lancashire to-day. (Hear, hear).—Turning to Mr. Monaghan the Mayor said; —lt must be very gratifying to him to be there that night to receive those presents. It was to show the esteem in which the inhabitants of Warrington held him. The puhlic had responded in a way which he had never known before. He trusted Mr. Monaghan would live long to make use of the articles. Mr. A. Brto wea: referred to the absence of the Chief Constable who had been called away to Liverpool. It seemed to him that the years went by quickly. It did not seem 30 years since be had the privilege of declaring Mr. Monaghan a constable of Warrington. He had always had the fullest confidence in him. (Applause). Mr. MONAGIIA.N responding said he was very Pleased to receive the valuable presents. He could scarcely find words sufficient to thank them for their kindness. When he first came he liked Warrington, and the longer he lived the better he liked it. (Applause). Councillor IV. B. ROM:1;1'i proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor for SO kindly being present that evening. Mr. J. LisTnit seconded. The motion was accorded with musical honours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>YOUNG lIOUSEBREAKE?</head>
        <p>A YOUNG lIOUSEBREAKE?</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool County Quarter Sessions, on Tues- day, William Cowell (19), joiner, pleaded guilty to four charges, one of breaking into the school horse of the Liverpool Juvenile Reformatory Association at Nes. Won- le-Willows, and stealing a bicycle, and three charges of house-breaking, one at Childwall, and the others at Little Woolton. Cowell is a native of the Isle of 'Alan, and the police reported that he had been for some years at the reformatory, where he was industrious and exemelary. When he left he was presented with a set of joinery tools as a reward for his good conduct. In April last he was before the Court and discharged. An effort had been made to get him away to Australia or Canada, but the laws of the colonies presented diffi- culties. He had returned to the reformatory and worked there as a paid apprentice. I att;rly he had not attended to his duties, and the offenr 39 had been committed while he was at the reformatory. The Superintendent of the reformatory stated that the youth's behaviour was inexplicable. He had everything in his favour, and he was clever at his trade. The Chairman (Sir W. B. Forwood) said the Bench had decided to send Cowell to two year's imprisonment under the Borstal system. If it was found that the Borstal system was not applicable the sentence would be eight months' imprisonment with hard labor-.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>FOOTBALL FIXTURES AND RESULTS.</head>
