      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ST. JAMES' CHURCH, LATCHFORD. Successful Bazaar.</head>
        <p>ST. JAMES' CHURCH, LATCHFORD. Successful Bazaar.</p>
        <p>The congregation of St. James' Church, Latchford, have been making a special effort by means of a bazaar held in the schoolroom on Wednesday, Thursday and Friday to raise funds for variou3 parochial purposes. The rooms presented a beantiful appearance, flags and bunting being freely utilized for decorative purposes, and the stalls were temptingly arranged. The following is a list of the stallholders CoNOWEGATIONAL STALL.—Mrs. Foster, Miss Parry, Miss Peers, Miss Carruthers, Miss Gibson, Mrs. M. Acton, Miss Smith, Mrs. Harris, Miss Bowcock, Miss Barnham, Airs. Guest and Miss Johnson. MISSION CHURCH STALL.—Miss Burgess, Miss Ker- mode, Miss Rhodes, Misses Isherwood, Mrs. Hampson and Mrs. Drummond.</p>
        <p>TEACHERS' STALL—Mrs. George, Miss Hughes. Miss Gibbs,Miss Houghton,MissThrossel the Misses Miller ; Messrs. Livingstone, Minards, Todd, and Jones. SWEET STALL.—Mrs. Collins, Mrs. Swift, and Miss Chester. . • wt.: YOUNG MEN'S STALL—Messrs. Grounds, Foster, Leigh, Astley and Parr. FLOWER STALL.—Mt-S. and Miss Bolton.</p>
        <p>ICE CREAM STALL.—.)IiSSOS Foster, Warburton and Farghar. REFRESITMENTS.—Mrs. Hallwood, Miss Hallwood, Master Hallwood, Mrs. Farghar, Mrs. Grant, Mrs. Davies, Mrs. Lea, Mrs. Marsh, Miss Heath, Mrs. Newton, Mrs. Lingard, Mrs. Booth, Mrs. Jefferson, Miss Bailey, Miss Eckersley, Miss Ellison and Miss Sampson. The Entertainment Committee consisted of Messrs. Warburton, Brayshaw and Rev. E. M. swift. On Wednesday the opening ceremony was performed by Mrs. R. PIERPOINT accompanied by Mr. R. Pierpoint. There were also preset-A the Vicar, (Rev. H. B. Firth), Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, Mrs. Carleton, Rev. H. 11. Cotsbie, Mrs. Foster, Mr. Eckersley, Mr. J. Farghar and other members of the congrggation. The VICAR in asking Mrs. Pierpoint to open the bazaar said that he had to thank all the ladies and gentlemen who had taken part in getting the sale together. He explained that the primary object of the bazaar was•to raise funds for the erection of a new Mission Church. St. James' parish had grown very largely in recent years, especially in that part of Latch- ford which lay close to Black Bear Bridge on tha linutsford-road. The Mission Church,which is situated in the midst of a large population was doing very good service but regarded from a structural point of view it is unsatisfactory. It was sadly dilapidated and so they were hoping to build a new mission church which would be substantial and permanent. Half the money made by the bazaar would be placed to the credit of " St. Hilda's Mission Church Building Fund." With regard to the other purposes of the sale, during the past few years some improvements had been made in St. James' Church, but there wore several others they wished to see affected. They were proposing to construct above the heating chamber a new vestry for the choir ; to fill up the archways at the west end of the church with screens of wood and glass, with a door in the lower portions o'f each so as to exclude the draught which in cold weather was a source of serious inconvenience to the congregation. They had lately introduced incan- descent lights into the church and everyone was loud in their praise but they had not yet been paid for. Towards the expense of these alterations the second half of the bazaar proceeds would be applied. Mrs. PlErtrotxT, who was received with applause, , said she was very pleased that she was asked to open the bazaar, and she hoped the efforts to raise funds for , the erection of a new mission church would be largely I helped by that bazaar. She had every sympathy with the objects and wished the bazaar every success. (Applause.) On the motion of Mr. FOSTER seconded by Mr. ECKERSLEY, a hearty vote of thanks was recorded to Mrs. Pierpoint. In responding on behalf of his wife Mr. ROBERT FIERPOINT gave some or his recollections of electioneer- ing in Latchford and pointed out that it was there he received his first " election kiss." (Laughter.) The bazaar was re-opened on Thursday by the Rev. F. Clifton Smiit- Beech House, Helsby, and yesterday afternoon there was a pleasing opening ceremony in which the children took part. An admirable series of entertainments has been pro- vided. The sketch " The Crystal Gazer " was given by the Misses S. and H. Broadbent on Wednesday after- noon while the evening attractions were a musical entertainment by Miss Adams and party, concerts by members of the Warrington Male Voice Choir and others. On Thursday there were special concerts and illustrated readings, those taking part including Mr. J. F. Swift, and last night "Professor Marvello "gave con- juring entertainments and many other attractions and competitions engaged attention. The total proceeds amounted to about £3S).</p>
        <p>" BON-FIRE " NIGHT FIRES. The Warrington Fire Brigade had a busy time on the merry fifth of November. The first outbreak occurred while the festivities were in full swing, the scene of the fire being the premises of Mr. G. Wallington, builder, Firth-place. The stables and a timber store- room were found by the brigade to be well alight, but after about an hour's work the fire was subdued. After extinguishing the outbreak in Firth-place the brigade went to a fire which broke out at the brushworks of of Messrs. Rushwortb, in Clegg-street, but it was only a slight outbreak. Abaut midnight a fire was discovered by Police-constable Fish on the premises of Messrs. Pierpoint, seed merchants, Horse- market-street. The brigatie had a difficult task in I• copmg with the outbreak, as it was confined to the back of the premises. During the fire several minor accidents happened to the firemen. Sergeant Cook, in making an entrance to the upper floor, was seriously cut about the knee, and bad to be taken to the Infirmary. Two other firemen, owing to the intense heat and smoke fell a considerable distance into the basement. After several hours hard work the firemen got the upper hand, and the flames were extinguished. A portion of the adjoin- ing premises of Mr. J. B. Isaac was damsgei by water.</p>
      </div>
