      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "With Efg7il Tools."</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "With Efg7il Tools."</p>
        <p>We have only to say that Mr. Henry Seton Merriman's fine novel loses nothing in the dramatisation to give the stage-production of " With Edged Tools" the highest possible recommendation. Much, of course, also depends on the company entrusted with the play and we can add, with honest conviction, that Messrs. Armitage and Arthur Leigh's company does full justice to the work. The Ferfornaances at the Royal Court Theatre this week have been really high-class, and it is a long time siace we witnessed a dramatic play which aff ordel us more satisfaction. " With Edged Tools" is of absorbing interest from first to last, whether the scene be pitched in the west-end drawing-room of Lady Cantourne or in Maurice Gordon's bungalow at Leango on the West Coast of Africa. The story is of two intrepid Englishmen who, guided by Victor Durnovo, a half-caste adven- turer, penetrate the West African jungle in order to secure a rare and valuable plant with wonderful medicinal properties. Before leaving England they both plight their troth to the same girl, the flirtatious Millicent Chyne, and both set oat with the honourable and manly intention of making a fortune worthy of the lady's beauty and position. There is something tragic about these two men, sworn friends, risking life itself on the spur of what each conceives to be a good woman's love. They confide in one another without discloslng the identity of their respective flanc4es. While at Loango another girl enters into the life of one of the men, Jack Meredith, and wins a deeper love than ever he felt for the absent Millicent. But Meredith is loyal and returns to England to fulfil his contract with Millicent, even after Jocelyn Gordon has nursed him through the African malaria,and given away the secret of her affection. The other man, Guy Oscard, also returns, reaching L'andon only a few hours before the time fixed for the wedding of Meredith and Millicent. Naturally the denottcatent is interest- ing It is also satisfactory—at least for Meredith and Jocelyn. Tills is the groundwork of the story, but Durnovo an -I the life in Africa fill in much of the detail. The half-caste is an unscrupulous wretch, and he comes to a horrible end, which is depicted in the fourth act in a scene which surpasses anything we have witnessed for a long time in weird realism. The mystery and horror of "darkest Africa " brood over the whole scene, which keeps one breathless with suspense. A 9 Victor Durnovo, Mr. Campbell Goldsmid supplies a wonderful study of Mr. Merriman's uncouth villain. His acting was full of power and " grip" from first to last. The hero's part, that of Jack Meredith, is played in very finished and forceful style by Ur. William F. Grant, who adds considerably to the laurels he has gained in " Leah fileschna " and "Lucky Durham." Mr. Charles Hartopp is equally good in the smaller part of Guy Oscard, while Mr. J. A. Rosier depicts the elderly diplomatist, Sir John Meredith, with consummate skill. As Jocelyn Gordon, the heroine of Loango, Miss Phyllis Rel ph acts most charmingly. She can be winsome to a degree, and as pathetic as the occasion demands in the fourth act when she and Meredith part under heartbreaking circumstances. We might offer almost as high praise to Miss Marie Leoahard, did the part of Millicent afford the same scope. She certainly acted the frivolous London girl with grace and skill. Miss Isabel Grey makes a most natural and motherly Lady Cantourna, .and no fault can be found with Miss Frances Wolviston as the negro servant, Marie. The play is exceedingly well-dressed and staged, the scenes at Loango, iii particular,being a triumph of scene-painting and stagecraft.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRA:IIIIE.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRA:IIIIE.</p>
        <p>The Royal Court Theatre is now uader new management, Jr. Joseph Beacham, the new proprietor, having taken possession this week. The theatre will be under the control of Mr. A U. Loader, who is also the manager of the St. Helens Opera House, with Ur. S. Smiley as assistant manager. Next week that popular drama" The Breed of the Treshams" which has always proved a great attraction to Warrington playgoers, will again be presented. Mr. Sidney Bland, who scored such a signal 81.1303£44 as " Reresby, the Rat" on the occasion of the last visit, will again take the leading part and the production promises to be thoroughly high-class.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>CMS-COUNTRY RUNNING. -- SATURDAY RUNS.</head>
        <p>CMS-COUNTRY RUNNING. -- SATURDAY RUNS.</p>
        <p>SEFTON HARRIERS were visited by Wallasey A.C. ; a six mile run was would up with a mile break :—result : J. T. Rimmerl, D. J. Hunter, J. Rice 3, R. M. Caughey 4, C. H. Spence 5, W. H. Hocken 6—all seftonians except Spence. SUTTON HARRIERS won another St. Helens League fixture, their latest victims being St. Helens A.C., who made the best show of any of the local clubs who have so far tackled the doughty Suttonites ; a seven mile run resulted :—Sutton : Straw 1, Whittle 2, Welding 3, Joe Bailey 5, Devlin 6, .T. W. Bailey 9; total 26. St. Helens A.C. A. Howden 4, Tiffney 7, R. Elowden 8, Thomas 13, Garnett 14, Crooks 16 ; total 62. BROUGHTON HARRIERS had three packs out for an enjoyable run, over about six miles, in the neighbour- hood of Worsley ; a subsequent two miles burst on the I road, provided some of the older runners with food for I refection—the new material is very promising. CREWE HARRIERS had two packs running over a seven mile course, H. Bennion, T. Millington, E. Aspinall and FL Shaw of the fast pack arrived some time after W. Edwards, J. Wilson and T. Aspintll of the slow pack who had the liberal concession of ten minutes—the novices are doing well. WARRINGTON A.C. formed two packs. A grand run over about seven miles resulted in J. George 1, A. Siddall 2, J. Litherland 3, A. Millington 4, S. Hewitt 5, S. Holbrook 6, being most promieont of the slow pack. H. Holbrook 1, A. Parkinson 2, W. Antrobus 3, G. Watmough 4, If. K. Jepson 5, were the leaders of the fast pack who finished nearly three minutes behind the slows who had made good use of a ten minutes allowance Twenty turned mit on Tuesday evening for a three miles run over Cheshire roads. At a subsequent com- mittee meeting it was decided to hold the annual dance early in December. To-day's run will be over the Christmas handicap course and two packs will be formed at 3.15. Tuesday evening's run will start at 8.15 prompt. All are invited to attend. For all Chronic Chest Complaints, take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, is, lid., 2s. Bd.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>A Bill for a National Minimum Wage.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL CRITIC.</p>
        <p>Oa Thursday evening the legislators assembled in our local Parliament, had before them a Bill with the ambitious object of establishing a national industrial organization which shall at all times provide work for all requiring it and a national minimum wage. Mr. J. MORTON (President of the Local Government Board) introduced the measure which, he claimed, approached the serious question of unemployment on non-party lines. The chief provisions of the Bill are : To establish agricultural, manufacturing or other industries, as well as retail stores, to meet the needs of the unemployed. To set to work those applying for employment ; to collect the produce of their labour and distrib ate the same through the retail stores amongst the producers. Payment for labour t 3 be made in paper money, the amounts ranging from sixpence upwards. The wage when this Act comes into operation shall be 12s. 6d. per week, to be increased only when the value of the total production of the State Industries exceeds an average of 12s. 6cl. per head of those employed. No work shall be undertaken at the State Industries other than that required to meet the demands of the retail stores. The machinery to administer the Act to consist of a Central Council of fifteen members appointed by the secretary of the Local Government Board, and a Local Managing Committee for each centre of industry ; the capital required to be raised by loan for which the interest shall be guaranteed by the State, the interest and capital to be repaid from the State industries in thirty years. Mr. E. ENGLAND (Chancellor of the. Exchequer) seconded. Mr. E. GORDON BURGESS (Oxford Universiby) moved that the Bill be read six months hence. In the course of a breezy speech he said that the Bill from first to last contained all the principles which were enunciated in State Socialism. (" No, no.") It certainly contained that bureaucratic system which was involved in State Socialism. He objected to industry being ruled by these official systems. It was confusing and unbusinosslike, As one remedy for the present state of affairs, he sug- gested that in many industries hours of labour could be considerably curtailed. It was, however, only natural that there should be unemployment when by our present system we were bound to tax things we could not produce and let in free those things which directly competed with British labour. The speaker caused some amusement when referring to the maintenance of those employed under the new scheme, by stating that men like Mr. Grayson would demand far more ' sumptuous things than others. Mr. NIGHTINGALE : Hear, hoar, so should I. (Laughter.) Mr. Butte ess maintained that the President for the Local Government Board had approached the subject from the wrong standpoint and the di.advantages of his system would far outweigh any advantages which might accrue. Mr. W. 11. CHANDLER (Salisbury) in a maiden speech said it was passing strange that in the twentieth century they should have to bring in a Bill to grant men food and shelter. To defer the matter for another six months would be an insult to the British public. The scheme might be Socialistic but it touched the roots of the problem. The most vigorons criticism of the measure was forthcoming from Mr. R. G. PIERPOINT, the Liberal member for E. Manchester. lie said it was with mixed feelings that he rose to address the House because he found himself opposed to his own Government. When he read the Bill, it made him wonder what tren had framed it. Were they men of sound business common- sense dealing with the matter in a serious manner, or were they simply playing with it? It was more in pity than in actual condemnation that he was going to try and put the Government through their facings. Critising the measure in detail, the hon. member said it would be impossible to run the factories at full time, and only supply their own workers. The matter had not been properly thought out. The Government were either tired of being in office and wanted to have something easier to do on the other side of the House or else—well, he I would not say what he thought about them. (Laughter.) The financial side of the measure was the bigeest mess of the whole lot. With regard to the paper money, the minimum order was to be worth sixpence. What about the man who bought a twopenny glass of beer and wanted fourpence change ? How would he get it? Was he to drink two more glasses ? (Great laughter.) Why did the Government suggest paper money ? He thought it was because they could not trust the people. Had not the men who would work in the Government factories as much right to be paid in current coin as the workeis in private industry ? (Applause.) ,He claimed the votes of the Socialists against the Bill, because the Gocernment had to guarantee the interest on the capital, and one of the fundamental bases of the Socialist party was that interest was robbery. Further, he main- tained that it was impracticable to run State industry side by side with private industry ; they must have either one or the other. "This Bill " concluded Mr. Pierpoint "is in my opinion a waste of paper. It would , have been much better employed to light the bonfires for the sth of November. (Laughter.) I second the amendment that it be read this day six months, but I would like to make it 16,000 months." (Laughter and applause). Mr. M. G. HEARNE (Kilkenny S.) poked fun at the " masterly intricacies " of the Bill, and said it was remarkable for what it left out rather than for What it had in it. (Laughter.) Mr. NIGHTINGALE (Normanton), in his usual earrest style, said the Bill was not what the Socialists wanted, but it was something on the way. It was ridiculous to talk of over-production, when vast numbers were starving for the necessities of life. Socialists went in for paper m mey. What did it matter whether it was paper or a sovereign so long as they could obtain what they required? I Mr. E. WYNNE JONES (Foreign Secretary) said he thought the member for East Manchester Saw that the lOpposition were rather weak and stepped into the 'breach to help them. ("No, no.") Mr. PIETtPOINT (Warmly) : I object to that remark. , I was absolutely sincere in what I said. I maintain I the Government Bill is rubbish. I Mr. WwYNE JONES claimed that the Bill was an honest attempt to deal with the problem and would not interfere with private industry. Tariff reform was no remedy, as was evident from the fact that there were more unemployed in Berlin to-day than in London. There would be no difficulty with the paper money, for those who did not purchase to the whole value of their order would have it endorsed to that effect and it could', be used again. Mr. G. WILSON (flythe) also took part in the debate which was adjourned until next Thursday evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>TRAP ACCIDENT AT RISLEY.—As Mrs. Sherlock, the wife of Mr. Sherlock, butcher, Padgate and Fearnhead, and a boy named Leslie were driving in a trap last Saturday through Risley, one of the wheels of the trap unfortunately collided with a large stone at the side of the road with the result that both were thrown violently out of the trap, on to the road. Mrs. Sherlock was much shaken and reseived injuries to her back and on being taken home had to obtain medical aid, whilst the boy was bruised about the face. From the latest accounts both are making progress to convalescence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>WESLEYAN CHURCH SALE OF WORK'.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN CHURCH SALE OF WORK'.</p>
        <p>This sale which is in aid of various church improve- ments and other worthy objects, was held on Thursday, in the schoolroom adjoining the church. The room though small, presented a striking appearance, being beautifully decorated with all kinds of autumn flowers. The stalls were beautifully laid out and much credit is due to the following : Fancy Stall.—Mrs. F. Wright Itfrs. W. Newton, Mrs. Bennett, Miss Richardson, Miss Protitt and Miss Ward.</p>
        <p>• Plain Sall.—Mrs, W. Leigh, Mrs. Duckworth and Mrs. Dutton.</p>
        <p>Flower StalL—Mrs. M. Wright, Miss Duckworth, Miss Dutton and Miss Gladding. Refreshment Stall.—Mrs. Barnett, Mrs. Cladding, Mrs. Plinston, Misses Mason, Mrs. Gandy, Miss E. Bennett, Mrs. Andrews and Miss Bridge. The opening ceremony was performed by Mrs. Charles E. Parker, the Rev. William Mellor presiding. There were also present :—The Rev. T. Gladding, Rev. J. Gunnell, Dr. Bennett, Mr. Leonard Bennett, Mr. Wright, and other members of the congregation. The Rev. William Mellor, in opening the proceedings said he hoped the bazaar would be carried to a success- ful issue. He had been informed that they wanted £2O towards the refurnishing of the minister's house. They also wanted to recarpet the chapel and recushion the pews and do other general repairs to the chapel. That would need another £3O, so that meant £5O in all. He remarked that the carpet in the church bad been on the floor since the chapel was built in 1877. They also wanted a larger schoolroom, and all the surplus cash would go to that object. They much needed a fairly large room in which to hold cone )rts and other enter- tainments during the winter months. Mrs. Parker was presented with a beautiful bouquet of chrysanthemums and lilies, by Miss Gwennie Bennett, Mr. Mellor was also presented with an orchid. Mrs. Parker, who was received with applause, said it was the first time she had been asked to undertake such an important function in Padgate, but she hoped it would not be the last. She hoped the time would come when women would be able to occupy official positions in the church. In declaring the sale open Mrs. Parker wished it every success. (Applause.) Dr. Bennett proposed and Mr. Wright seconded a vote of thanks to Mrs. Parker which was heartily passed. The proceeds of the sale amounted to £lO2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
