      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Sweet music has been discoursed at the Palace this week by the Austral Trio, whose refined act entitled "A Musical Evening" is a distinctly pleasing feature of a bright programme. The lady of the trio excels as a harpist, and supplies an effective obligato to songs by one of the gentlemen who possesses a tenor voice of great purity. But the resources of the Austral Trio are not solely musical, one of the mem- bers bringing down the house with an exceptionally smart dancing turn. The Jackley Trio submit a drawing-room entertainment which is quite out of the ordinary. They are all erperts at tumbling and similar accomplishments, but the life and soul of the show is the famous whistling comedian George. He does not say much but whistles a great deal—in fact he carries on a whistling conversation—if the description be admis- sible -which is perfectly intelligible. Moreover his facial contortions are enough to make anyone laugh heartily. Albert Letine's female impersonations are remarkably good. As the aged spinster or the gay comedienne his "make up" is a triumph,and were it not for an occasional very masculine cough the deception would be complete. Geoff reys and Benson provide a pleasant ten minutes in one of their comedy sketches. A husband of a theatrical turn is endeavouring to train his better half to play Juliet but gives up the attempt in disgust. To show that she is something of an actress the wife impersonates an Irish slavey and does it so well that her husband cannot see through the little ruse, and lively scenes ensue. It is a clover turn and gives immense satis- faction to the audience. Flo Barnett is an accomplished 1 vocalist with a rich voice and her songs are always popular. Irma Orbasany's cackatoos display an intelligence almost human, and go through a series of interesting tricks as to the manner born. Jack Rae, novelty gymnast, is a man of muscle, and we cannot speak too highly of his feats on the rings. He carries through a difficult performance with a dexterity and coolness which suggest that it is almost second nature with him. The Prinellas submit a farm yard scene, in which a few of the familiar denizens of the farin: yard wander about at leisure while the artistes pass the time in song. The biograph and the band selections are well up to the usual high standard. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. _</p>
        <p>Next week Mr. Harry Roxbury presents the famous inimitable comedian Tubby Edlin in the grand new production " The Cowboy Millionaire—" a merry mixture of mirth and melody. There will be a com- pany of first-class artistes and full comic opera chorus of servants, guests, officers, policemen, etc. The pro- gramme will also be contributedto by :—Morny Cash, the popular comedian ; Xfaston, Yorkshire mimic ; Stidder and Dunbar, musical comedy artistes ; Elmo and Rego, comedy acrobats ; Burnetti, original char- acter musician ; Maggie Bowman and Tom J. Morton in the comedy duologue " She loved a policeman."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>WARRINGTON BAND OF HOPE UNION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BAND OF HOPE UNION.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Council of the Warrington Band of Hope Union was held on Thursday evening in Gregory's Caf4. Mrs. James Dodds (president), presid- ing over a very large attendance, supported by Revs. J. S. Hughes and T. Miller (vice-presidents), Mr. R. Pierce Williams (treasurer., Mr. F. Gandy (hon. secre- tary) and Messrs. W. Wooley and J. Muir (assistant secretaries), Rev. J. S. Hughes opened the proceedings with prayer, after which Mr. H. Wooley read the annual report of the Executive Committee which showed that and large amount of educational and progressive work had been done.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Pierce Williams presented the financial state- ment and Mr. C. S. Fellows gave the auditor's report, all of which were approved and considered very satis- factory. Mr. Fkr.Lows gave a report of the meetings held in Manchester on October 6th in connection with the great Forward Movement of the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope Union. The election of officers for the coming year also took place, Mrs. Dodds was unanimously re-elected Presi- dent for another year. In thanking the meeting for again electing her to that honourable position, Mrs. DODDS expressed her great love for the work and also emphasised the great need in the to% n for Band of Hope propaganda. A number of ladies and gentlemen were elected vice-presidents as follows :—lles-s. J. S. , Hughes, J. Yonge, H. Wall, F. Vaughan, J. F. Matthews, Thomas Miller, J.B. Buglass, 11. E.Letheren, E. B. Keeling, Mrs. Simpson, Miss B. Dutton, Miss Naylor, Mrs. E. Walker, Mr. A. Mot:infield, Mr. IL Thornton, Mr. W. H. Sherburn, Mr. W. Clatworthy, Mr J. J. Firth, Mr. Ben Few, Mr. E. J. Guest and Mr. Moss (Lymm). Mr. R. Pierce Williams was re-elected treasurer, Mr. F. Gandy was unanimously re-elected bon, secretary, expression being given to the hope that Mrs. Gandy's health would soon be restored. Mr. Muir intimated his desire to resign as one of the assistant secretaries and the council received the resignation with regret. Mr. H. Wooley and Mr. C. S. Fellows being elected hon, assistant secretaries for the year. Other appointments were :—Press secretary, Ikfli G. Whitfield; auditors, Messrs. S. Fernley and S. Gallop; stocktakers, Mrs. Whitmore, and Messrs. S. Fernley and S. Gallop ; Representatives to the Lancashire and , Cheshire Band of Hope Union, Mr • F. Gandy and Mr. B. Warburton ; organist and accompanist, Hr. A. Clatworthy ; conductor, Mr. A. Spruce; Organising and Visiting Committee, Mrs. Whitmore, Miss Bacon, Messrs. J. Hamblett, J. Owen, W. H. Pearson, B. Warburton, Adams and Ingham. The meeting was full of enthusiasm and speaks well for the work of the coming winter amongst the young. The Union are holding a great public meeting in Parr Hall to-morrow (Sunday) evening, which is Temperance Sunday, chair to be taken by Mrs. Dodds and addresses given by A, Thompson, Esq., of Manchester, and Mr. Boreland of Liverpool. The Band of Hope Union Choir will sing a choice selection of choruses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>DROWNED IN THE FOG. Warrington Girl's Sad End.</head>
        <p>DROWNED IN THE FOG. Warrington Girl's Sad End.</p>
        <p>A sad story of a young woman's death in the fog was told in the Manchester Coroner's Court yesterday afternoon when the jury inquired into the death of Mabel Everett, 22 years, a cook at the Mitre Wine Bar, Stretford-road, Hill[me, and whose body was found in the Mersey at Northenden. She was the daughter of Mr. Frank Everett, of 121, Cartwright-street, Warring- ton, who gave evidence of identification. A man named Alfred Scargill, living at Withington said that at ten minutes past seven on Tuesday night he was on the bridge at Northenden when he heard a splash. He thought he heard sotheone cry, "Oh, God, help me." He pulled a young man off his bicycle, and both, listened, when they heard the cry again. They followed it all the way down the bank, shouting, but they could not tell what it said.</p>
        <p>- P.C. J. R. Burgess said that when he was summoned to the river it was so dark and foggy that it was impossible to do anything then. Two days after he recovered the body from the rive- near Stanton- avenue, three-quarters of a mile away from the bridge. William Revell, deceased's sweetheart, also gave evidence. He knew of no trouble which deceased had. She should have met him that night, but did not do so. ' The jury returned a verdict of ' Found drowned?'</p>
        <p>' GOOD SUBSTITUTE FOR PUTTY. A cheap and effective substitute for putty, }.4 stop the cracks in floors and other woodwork, is matte by soaking newspapers in a paste made by boiling a pound of flow in three quarts of water and adding a teaspoonful of alum. This mixture should be about the consistency of putty, and should be forced into the cracks with a blunt knife. It will harden as it dries, and then may be painted or stained to match the boards. If the eraeßs are neatly stopped, they gill, after painting; be hardly perceptible.</p>
        <p>HOUSE-CLEANING HINTS. Here are some good hints on sweeping and dusting: Never set a broom against the wall or stand it in the corner—it spoils the shape of the broom. Put a cord in the handle and hang the broom up. A broom is made to last longer if, when now, it is steeped a few moments in warm water. Never put a broom away dusty. Never scrub with the house broom.</p>
        <p>Learn to handle the broom so as to sweep at, little dust as possible into the air; keep the dust on the floor, where it can be taken up in the dustpan. Cover furniture too heavy to move. Drag the remaining furniture from the walls and cover it If the curtains reach the floor, pin them up out of the way. Carry out the rugs, brush and beat them, and lay them away out of the dust. Open the windows so that the air may pass through the room..</p>
        <p>In dusting down a room take are not to brush the paper. A paper that has been brushed off can never be cleaned.</p>
        <p>USEFUL SUGGESTIONS.</p>
        <p>Avoid too hot irons for delicately-tinted wash goods; this often does as much to fade them as the washing. If the ceiling becomes smoked from a lamp, wash off the blackened surface with a little weak soda water. • . _</p>
        <p>Lumps of "g1;71 camphor Pet in cupboards that are infested with mice will soon drive awaz these little pests. I In laundering coloured shirt waists or nepligle ahirts use lukewarm water with plenty of salt in it, and a moderate application of some pure white soap. Dry wrong side out in the shade. and remove from tbe line as soon as dried.</p>
      </div>
