      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>NEXT CONCERT OF THE</head>
        <p>THE NEXT CONCERT OF THE</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Barnett's "ANCIENT MARINER."</p>
        <p>The inception of " The Ancient Mariner" in the form of a cantata takes one back to the very earliest! evidences of John Francis Barnett's gifts as a com- poser, for this was not only his first wOrk of its class, hut it was closely allied with the initial effort which brought him i.to pohlic notice. From the chatty and interesting " Reminiscences and Impressions" (London: Hodder and Stoughton) in which the composer of "The Ancient Mariner" has, lin the mellowness of his old age, taken the world into Ihis confidence, we learn that it was as far back as the year 1863, when the Musical Society of London had announced its intention of giving trials of new orchestral compositions, that Barnett was first moved to bestir himself, and that he then sent inn symphony on which he ha 1/been engaged. This work was, happily,selected for performance at one of these trials, and subsequently, in June of the following year,it had the good fortune to be included in the programme of tha London Musical Society's Concerts. The favourable manner in which that symphony was received prompted the Committee of the Birmingham Musical Festival to ask Barnett to write a cantata for the Festival of 1867. " Curiously enough " says he in his bt ok, "I had, about two years prior to this event, seen a copy of Sir Noel Paton's illustrations to Coleridge's Ancient Mariner' though I had never read this particular poem. But the drawings made a great impresssion upon me by their weird character and thus gave me an idea that The Rime of the Ancient Mariner ' would be a splendid subject for a cantata." Small wonder then that when the Birmingham invitation was received Barnett's ambition was at once fried. But the exigencies of time and opportunity .were pressing. It was then November 1866 and the chorus ptrts of the new work would be wanted by the following, March °lily four months. "At first" continues The Reminiscences, "I thought three years would not have been too long to have allowed myself." But eventually Barnett got to work, though at first he thought the poem too gloomy for effective treatment ; and that same evening found him seated at the piano with the volume open before him. Looking the difficulties of the poem in the face Barnett rightly arrived at the conclusion that it was quite reasonable he should regard the chorus and solo voices in the light of recite's of the story, in other words, says he, " I proposed to write a work that would be a musical rauling of Coleridg !'s verses." The selection and arrangement of the %you de were, Barnett continues, "in themselves rather difficult tasks. The whole, poem it would have been impossible to take, not only on account of its length, but likewise vowing to much of it being unsuitable for music. The phrase with which "Ile Ancient Mariner' opens at the entry of basses and tenors :</p>
        <p>It is an Ancient Mariner, And he atoppeth one of three' came to me quite easily, and I was delighted to find that the words went well to music, and their metre suitable to any kind of musical rhythtn. Having once formed an opening phrase I discovered that the verses assisted my ideas so well that before I gave up for the evening I had completed not only the preliminary recitatives but the sketch of the first ch qns The ship was cheered ' which contains the initial melody of the cantata. Never before, says the now enthusiastic worker, had _ I composed with such</p>
        <p>facility. The sketch thenceforward -developed rapidly and my doubts about finishing the work in time for the Festival Fere to a great extent relieved. The portions of the pcem where the individuality of the Matiner is most prominent I allotted to the baritone. In this manner I was able to avail myself of certain oppor- ' tunities of a dramatic character, as, for example, at the point where the Wedding Guest interrupts the old sailor with the words : God save thee ancient Mariner ! From the fiends that plague thee thus ! Why look'st thou so And he replies : '—With my crossbow I shot the Albatross.' I have given the Ivor&amp; of the Wedding Guest to the tenor and those of the Mariner, as just stated, to the baritone. Such episodes of a dramatic character I found very useful in giving relief to the generally descriptive character of The poem. The modern idea as to the method of writing such works as cantatas and oratorios is that they should be composed of scenes in which the music runs on without coming to</p>
        <p>any finish until the end of the seene is reached. That this system in a dramatic work is a great gain is undeniable ; but its advantage in a descriptive cantata are not so obvious. One reason why I found "Ile Rime of the Ancient Mariner' so adapted for separate numbers was that the poet generally describes one subjeet at a time without introducing desultory mattar. Thus , - -</p>
        <p>'About, about in reel bid rout The death-fires danced at night' pictures a weird scene and gives scope for the corn- poser writing in a given style - in this case somewhat in the style of iucantation music. Although says Barnett, I cannot say that in writing "The Ancient Mariner ' I have used leitmotiven in the Wagnerian sense, I do not lose sight of them altogether; for when- ever the poetic theme admits some musieal phrase is :allied to it. 'Thus there are about seven leitmotiyen of this kind which form connecting links with different portions of the cantata." It is futthermore interesting 0 learn that during the compositicn of the greater part of the music Barnett's ideas came to him as easily as they did in the opening chorus, but theme were some exceptions. Thus the beautiful quartet" Around, around flew each sweet sound " gave him a great deal of trouble. Indeed he wrote first one quattet, then another and fully worked them cut, both of which he ultimately rejected. His third attempt was more fortunate and this is the one included in the finished' work. In , spite of these delays Barnett was able to send in the ; choral parts for rehearsal in the March preceding the Festival, and in a few weeks later he was asked to himself conduct a rehearsal, it being later arranged that the public performance of the wok should also be conducted by the composer. According to The Times Barnett's cantata met with a success which must have been most gratifying to its young and clever composer. Commenting upon the work itself The Times critic says "In selecting Coleridge's metrical romance The Rime of the Ancient Mariner' for the theme of his cantata 11r. J. F. Barnett may not have acted with sound judgment; but his treatment of it being • exeln- sively from the point of view of realism, he eludes the charge of having over-estimated the reach and quality of his own powers. 'The Ancient Mariner' has an inner as well as an outer signification, and, in reject- ing the inner and devoting his wine energy to the illustration and development of the outer, Mr. Barnett has exhibited equal goad sense and modesty. Mr. Barnett's melody is of that ad captandum kind rrutst easily caught and retained by the ear, and, for this cause alone, his music is listened th with a pleasure too spontaneous for reflection and enquiry. We like it, why we neither know nor care; enough that we like it. It goes along fluently without let or hindrance' and we go along with it as Rossini (according to Richard Wagner) goes along 'in the melodious coach,' heedless of where it. may carry tri. If never stet thing it is never dull,and the existence cf thia last desideratum is an inestimable boon in a ' secul ir cantata.' I G 'The Ancient Mariner ' is a work without labour as it is work without ambition." Speaking of one of the most striking numbers in the cantata— the chorus in B minor 'About, about in reel and rout"— The Times premeds " Here, while gazing at Mendelssohn from all sides, it will be seen that Barnett has ingeniously managed to create some- thing which, though unquestionably incantation music, is in spirit as unlikeanything in the First Walcurgis Night and Lorelei ' as one thing can well be unlike another." THE RIME OF THE ANCIENT MARINER.</p>
        <p>—.— -- Of the peculiar characteristics of the gifted author of a poem so vy eirdly.pieturesque yet so full of an ietagery of a distinctly spiritual order, the following brief epitome gleaned from "The Treasury of Biography" may be read With interest. SAMUEL TAYLOR COLERIDGE, Born 1772- Died 1834.</p>
        <p>As a philosopher and theologian the influence of Coleridge has been very great and probably is so still notwithstanding the apparent predominance of a less spiritual philosophy than his. Although he did not live to complete the grand system of religious philosophy which be appears to have projected, the "massive fragments " he has left, us suffice to show more than the outlines of the vast whole. His writings are pervaded by a spirit not of this world and for every earnest student they are rich in treasures !.tf truth, wisdom and faith. Not a few have found in them the special help, guidance and defence which the critical doubts and discnasLms of the age make so needful. H.B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Yong P23?1..1'S 33:Iety. The' weekly meting of this ussful organiziti3n was held on Nfonlay.in els 113 n3rill 818 ling% Ilr. T. E3cles in the chur, when sli3rt papsrs were gives by the„lfisses Corney and fi3lg3on, and %assn. I'. F. Wallington, C. Fliwthlrn an lA. G. Wallinztos. An interesting discassion easnel.„ flickliy-3in3t P. 3 A.</p>
        <p>An 02;1 )a:ing of er) B tztkl97-stra3t S3oilty Vrai held on Si vra3n W ))Ilay pra3i I)I ov3r filrly atten-lincl.tt iatareiti3 1.1re43 Wai deliveral by Arr. B. Riehtrl3 )1, a) I Itr. v read Vla lee3nni. Tin an,l3-aptaist otA.4 Plinston.</p>
        <p>Eva:l,Ulistic S3PIIC3. An evangelistic service was 'co:Ili:gel ia tileld- street Wesleyan Sa'l,3olr..m:n, at the Cl3i) of the ordinal y service o S laity eveaiag. I llA.Lsaoli preside,l, and an ad {rats was. give .I by Alt% lEile, of Latchford. The Rev. T. Miller ma le a few encouraging remarks.</p>
        <p>FARM Fin BACON f tom Arley district at ASIILEY.S. Advt. The Bagliag ili!sance. " The begging nuis tuna in the tomn i 3 becooling yery serious, and 1.1.11,3iS something is cloae the pablic will havo no peace at all." This was the,Cnief Con- stable's remark when three offsniers were broaght up on Monday morning.—John Ntichell, of Bank-street, was fined 54. and costs for begging in Bridgs-street on Saturday night.—Thoznas Brainey, of Ship yard, was fined 14s. for bogging in lioraemarket-street, and Michael Modogue was fined 103. and coats for begging in Bridge street on Saturday night. Panay Popular -Concerts. . _</p>
        <p>A° Crowded ,audience and a high c'.a-;s programme were the outstanding features of Stturdaky evening's concert in the Bold-street Wesleyan Schoolron n. The Rev. T. , Millsr presided. Songs by MIS 3 ithrie flongliton, Iliss Lily Locke, Mrs. Sepliton, Mr. Dickenson and Mr. Entwisle were much enjoyed, as was a duet by Hessrs. D:ckenson and Entiviste. Mr. H. A. Roberts p-ovide I the butuour and kept the audience in rare good humour. Encores were frequent and the concert was one of the most successful yet held. Youth's Alleged Thefts. _ _ _</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Thursday, Albert Williams or Davies, a youth residing at Streiton, near Warrington, was remanded, on a charge of stealing two watches. On Monday last prisoner went to the shop of Mr. Danaville, pawnbroker, Kuntsferd-road, and asked to be shown some watches with a view to purchasing one of them. Whilst the Manager had his back turned he stole one of the watches. • Later in the moming he offered the watch to a watchmaker at Bridge Foot, and whilst there he took another watch. Information was giveu to the police, who effected his arrest. The magistrates remanded the case until to-day. Wilms To.tsTrso CFIE3SE CU. per lb. at Asimmt's.--Alvt The Field Club.</p>
        <p>At the entomological meeting, hell in the Museum Lecture Room, ere was, as usual, a 1 trge and beautiful display of butterflies and moths as well as of many other groups of insects, including beetles and dragon-flies. The earlier part of the evening was devoted o the inspecti m of these, and much and just admiration was expressed f)r the neatness and skill manifested in the setting of the specimens as well as wonder at Such a wealth of beauty in colour being found amongst insects occurring in the midst of a manufacturing district. Mr. F. Womershly opened the second part of th evening with notes on his season's captures, and an interesting discussion followed in which Ur. Mounfiell, Mr. Armstrong, Mr. M. Womersley, Mr. G. A. Dunlop, and Mr. Robinson took part.</p>
        <p>• The Late Mrs. J. C. Thompson. " "Holy Trinity Parish Magazine" for this month ontains tlicaollowing: —Our friends will be aware of the loss the church has sustained during the past month in the death of Mrs:Christmas Thompson, who not only on the Sunday, but also on tho week-day, was ,to be found at the usual services of the church. Acting under her instructions, her daughter, Mrs. Charles F. Pierpoint, has handed a cheque for £3O for the benefit of the Curacy Fund of the parish, at the discretion of the Vicar. The money has been . .</p>
        <p>placed to the credit of the other Curacy Fund of which the Vicar is for the precsnt treasurer, and there are good hopes that this fund also will in two or three years right itself with the bank. A few years ago the overdraft was as much as £93, now it is down to £lO. Table, Floor, and Hanging Lam PS. Latest designs now showing. See windows.—J. S. GARLAND, 51- 55, Bridge-street; Warrington.—Advt. Latchford (Without) Parish Council. The quarterly meeting of the above Council was held on Friday evening in the Baptist schoolroom. Mr. It. T. Fairclough presiding The other members present were Messrs. H. L. Taylor, F. A. Jenkins, Gorton and G. Pilling (clerk). A letter of apology</p>
        <p>9 for absence was received from Mr. W. D. 4Jeans. Saverel accounts were passed A communication was read from the county police offices, Chester, acknowledging a resolution passed at a previous meeting asking for better police supervision for Latchford Without with the result that additional police have been appointed. Further communications were read from the Gas Committee agreeing to con- vert the present lights throughout the parish into incandescent lights.</p>
        <p>A*Hr.ey's for PRovrsloNs, 4, BurTEnt inKer-sT.--Advt. Expensise Workhouse Maintenance. _ . .</p>
        <p>Daniel Ince, last from Prescot, was charged at the Borough Police Court on Mmday with deserting his three children, wheieby they' became chargeable to the funds of the Warrington Union. Mr. Shearer prosecuted for the \ Guardians. He stated that defendant had been off work for some time. When he went hack another man had token his place. He was given other woik, but this did not suit him so he left. He then went to Prescot leaving his children in the charge of a Mrs. Smith who lived in the same street as defendant bad formerly done. Aka. Smith removed the children to the workhouse on October 6th. They had been in the institution for three weeks the cost to the ratepayers being .v.. 2 15s. Mr. S. Wright : Do you mean to say it costs 15a. to keep a child in the vrorkhorve fcr a week?—Mr. Shearer: Yes, the amount is based on art average. —Mr. S. Wright : It seems that it costs more to keep a child in the Workhouse than out.—Mr. Shearer : Young: children are a great deal more trouble than otheis.—Mr. S. Wright :. It is excessive.--Defendant said that his son, who was 21 years of age, promised faithfully to look after the children. He had got work and was going on very well. He would pay all that was owing if they would give him a chance. He had had a lot of trouble lately; he had been in bed for 13 weeks through illness. He bad lost his wife and his eldest daughter. He Would try and get his children nut of the workhouse the following week.—Mr. S. Wright : Don't you know if yonkeepyour children in the Workhouse any longer you will to pay 15a. a week each until they are taken out ?—Defendant ia reply said he had no clothes or shoes for them. —The magistrates decided to sdloArn the case for a month the defendant promising to pay the amount by instal-. ments and to take the children out of the Workhouse ART CHINA TEA SERVICES, 40 pieces 12s. Gd.— J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street,Warrington. Batch of Warrington Cases at the Sessions. At the Liverpool County Quarter Sessions, on Tuesday, before Sir W. Forwood and other justices, Peter Grady (30), labourer' and Petrick M'Cresse (41), labourer, were charged with breaking into the Mersey Golf Club pavilion at Penketli, between the 22nd and 21th August, and stealing nine pairs of boots, 12 jackets' four caps, and a number of golf balls, the pro arty of William Stanley Knowles and others. —Prisoners pleaded guilty.—The pavilon was entered by forcing the catch of a window, and the clothinw and boots were taken from lockers. Grad; was sent to prison for three months with hard labour' and M'Creese, whom the Chairman 'described as nothing better than a professional thief was sentenced to 12 months imprisonment with hard labour.—William Tompkinson (31), clerk, was indicted for breaking into the shop of Messrs. Eekersley, chemists. Prisoner, who admitted the charge, said he did not know why he should act as he did, as he was in regular work at the time The police said they had no record against the prisoner. They had ascertained, however, that he was given to gambling. He had gained an entrance to the shop by means of a skeleton key which he had evidently made himself. A number of other keys were ton in his possession. On promising to give up gambling the justices decided to give prisoner another chance, an 1 bound him over.—Frank Henderson (31),labourer, was sentenced to six month's imprisonment for steal- ing five boxes of cigarettes, the property of John Monks. —James Higgins (29), labourer, for shop- breaking was sent to prison for four months' hard labour ; and James Ready (33) and Elizabeth Ready (38), for receiving the stolen property, for two months each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>OUR LOIL CIURCIIES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOIL CIURCIIES.</p>
        <p>Tä3 131313,1 PP3131'.1 LIP tä3 Dail</p>
        <p>A vary large congregation assembled at St. Paul's C'anrch on Sunday evening when the Bishop of Liver- pool (Dr. Calvaese) ocUptel the pulpit. His Lordship took far tett, " An I JOian sail Unto hhn, Verily I say into thee, to-day suit Cul bs with fa in Paradise (St. Luke the coarse of a po-verful sermon , the Bishop said there wts no period of sleen, of uncon- sziouslece bet yam death end being with Cadet. The idea of pirg Ito ry mag, that when the soal left tho holy I it Wan net sill :is ttly team nil clatill I to stud at once ii the preseiu of G al; an', therefore it ratst pass through long 4;93 Qr cleaanitag fire ; bit if there was ono person in the W 0714 night to hue gone to purgatory it was the dying robber, who had never been hiptisei, who hai never partaken of the Lard's S tom-, who hal no time to make smartie to society against whom he hat done such grievous wrong, hat (within a few hours of his 1-31)33ttaue he ptsaal fro-n this world to the net. And yat Teens said: " withont any purgatorial lire) shalt that be with Nie in Paradise." Mere was no single proof in the Holy Scriptures that the soul hal to pats thro tg'a purgatory before it want to bo with Cadet. Death cull not destroy life, personality, or lore ; it only perfected them. Para gal true Chrir.,iva live Nan as inn rtal 11.4 G 1 Himself, and their dear deal noes loved then with a parer an I better love in the I'M idii3 of Girl than they (the living) loved them oa earth. Tea C varch of Raglan cat oat prayers for the dead from her service in 1552, and had never bronght them back. Tao blessed dead analinl not Valk prayers. A colleot;o a was r-a'cei at th 3 CI 111 in nil of St. Pall's endowment fral and the building fund of the new church at Orford.</p>
