      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Tll3 Maim ani Bible Manes.</head>
        <p>Tll3 Maim ani Bible Manes.</p>
        <p>Trio Lord Bishop of Livarpool addressed a united service in connection with the Church of England Bible Classes of Warrington at Holy Trinity Church on Sanday afternoon. Vilma w.ts a very large congrep- tion. His Lordship bised an earnest and impressivø discours4 on tits wJrds : " 133 not slothful, but</p>
        <p>followers of then yelp throagh faith and piiienoe inherit the pr 'raises " (Hebrews vi. 12). That day, he ssid, was All SAints' aty. In the Cnunh of England . . . .</p>
        <p>V 0 neither worshipped saints nor invoked them: We neither prayed to them nor for them, and we did not ask them to pray for 113, hut we praised God for them tnd we sought to follow in their footsteps. He rejoiced to heve the privilege of speaking to them on such a dog Is that. He rejoiced to see so many of them tthered together in that church ant to know that they were members of Bible 'classes. Chirty-eight years age he began his life as a minister of Jesus Christ in Preuton, and he had never ceased to thank God that his flrs.t curacy was in Lancashire, for there he learat a lesson which he had never forgotten, namely, the power and the usefulness of Bible classes. Wherever, under the providence of God, he had had to work since he left Pr:ston, in North London and in Otford, he tried to carry oat the lesson he learnt by starting a Bible class for men, and he could truly say that some of the happiest and most fruitful hours of his ministerial life had been those whi(At he spent on Sunlay afternoons with the men of those classes, and to-day some of his best and most valued friends were men whom he learnt to know as they studied the Word of god together. The Bishop then spoke upon three footprints of the Saints of God into which we must put our feet, if we were to follow them i—ftrstly honouring the Holy Spirit, secondly, holding fast to Jesus Christ, and thirdly, seeking to live in the presence of God. He thought it was failure to grasp the truth that Jesus Christ was not only God bet truly man and able therefore to feel with us in all that belonged to our human nature, which so often sent men to pray to the Virgin Mother of our Lord and to saints. They deemed that our Lord was so great and glorious, so beyond their reach that they must have mediators betwixt them and Him, whereas His heart was so infinitely tender that it heard the faintest cry of every man and woman who turned to Him. Dwelling upon the importance of realizing the Divine presence, his lordship slid there would be no scamps(' work in bingland if every man worked as though he lived before God. The more the clergy realized they were in God's presence, the more pains they would take in preparing their se-mons. The more the laity lived in God's presence the more thoroughly would their work be done. They conld not scamp their work if they really believed that God was stunting by llnd looking on. Several hymns were heartily sung, Mr. %V. 13. Made!' presiding at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>P.S.A. MOVEMENT.</head>
        <p>P.S.A. MOVEMENT.</p>
        <p>Federation Meetings at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Lancashire and neighbour- ing counties Federation of P.S.A. Societies was held at Wycliffe Congregational Church, Warrington, on Saturday afternoon. Tn the absence, through Indis- position, of the President of the Federation, Mr. J. Ifcintosh of Oldham, a:vice-president, took the chair. After singing and prayer, the Rev. John Yonge rose to give the Fedration a hearty welcome to Warrington. lie said there Were two things that accounted for the small attendance of delegates. One was e4itt Warring- ton was rather an out of the way place and secondly the terrible cotton strike which many of the societies were affected by. strike, was sorry to say the numbers at Wycliffe P.S.A. were not what they were 12 or 14 years ago, when they had over 1200 members, hut since then other societies had been formed, and a large number of Bible classes had been started, for which they were thankful. After the minutes of the previous meeting had been read,and the minutes of the Executive bad been submitted for information, the following officers were eleuted : Rev. Fred [Ebben, president ; Messrs. J. 'Mclntosh, IV. Porter, Whalley, Armitage, vice-presidents ; Mr. James Dinkerley, secretary ; Mr. S. F. Jarvis, treasurer • and 18 of an Executive. . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. WITA.LLEY gave a report of the Ambulance Com- petitions, and although they were over .£4 in debt, the meeting felt that the money was well spent,not only upon the members of classes, but help was given to those in severe need. At 5 o'clock the delegates adjonrned to the Memorial Buildings where tea was served• After tea the delegates thanked the local society for their kind hospitality. Then the Rev. Hennesey, of Manchester, introduced the question of the great unemployed and said that he and others koew of the very quiet suffering of large numbers in around -Manchester, and they were sorry to know this, suffering was felt all over the country. He proposed that a resolution from the Federation be sent to the Government expressing the feelings of the Federation and the strong hope that all help would he given to the unemployed. This was seconded and sup- ported with the addition that the plates should go round for a colleotiou for the Lord nayor's fond for the pom,A3f Manchester. Over 303. was collected. The delegates than adjourned to the Church, while the tables were removed. Itfr. W. El. P.tyton, gag. gave an organ recitil, until 6.33, when a public meeting presided over by His Worship,the Mayor of Warrington (Alderman Smethurst) was held. _ . • • -</p>
        <p>The Rev. Joint YONGE said that those who had been I living ender Alderman Smethurst for the last two years knew the value of his Worship the Mayor He was exceedingly sympathetic, one who was anxious to help in any way and he was perfectly certain that when he retired from o!Bge he (the Mayor) would carry with him the respect and the confidence and in a great measure the honour and the love of this town. (Applause.) He did not think he could remember any Mayor who had been so thorough as our present one. In fact he thought the town would hava been content to have gone under his supremacy again if it bad been possible. (Applause.) The Mayon said he felt that he did not deserve the great praise which had been bestowed upon him,because the work they took in hand and carried out according to their -own conscience beri.ame not work but a pleasure. He believed that every man who had time at ! his dispCsal should do something for those around him. They might have comfort to their souls and recreation for their bodies. If he understood their associa- tion it was to bring them together, both man ! and women together. inChristian service. There was nothing so uplifting and so comforting to a man and his wife as o go to those pleasant social gatherings on Sunday afternoons, instead of lolling about with neighbours, talking, sleeping, and doing no earthly good whatever either to their bodies or souls. He wanted all the Church to be united, and instead of troubling about people in good positions se much, they shoull put their shoulders to the wheel and visit the slums to be determined to make something of People in them before they gave it up. (Heat, hear.) If everyone of them would try to take hold of some one, what a great amount of good would he derived. They said in War- Jington that he was the "Children's Mayor." He hoped r God would bless them, for he loved children. If they wanted to reform the country they must be kind to the children. (Applause). Mr. DUttREFILEY called attention to the absence of the President of the Federation, the Rev. F. Hibbert, who was detained at home through illness. He told the meeting that when the delegates arrived on the scene of the conference they were met by the Lord Mayor of Newcastle, and that all the meetings held in connection with it both at Newcastle and Sunderland were pervaded by enthusiasm which would be remem- bered by all present. They all knew of the distress ! that prevailed in Senderland through the past year, when the Mayor of Sunderland did splendid work and he was then truly a servant of God. He believed that permanence of reform coup only be attained by changed character. A hymn hook would be published at the end of the year, upon the compilation of which Mr. Jeffs and Mr. Knowles had spent a vast amount of time. There was a growth of Federations. At the London Conference held in 19011 there were 9 federations and at the Liverpool one last year there were 28 ; and there was likely to be more in the autumn. At a recep- -1 tion held in their honour, tbe Mayor spoke of the great j distress there was in Sunderland, and one of the dele- gates 'reposed that a collection should be taken then.</p>
        <p>This was heartily responded to by the handsome sum of £3O. One of their meetings was addressed by a representative from Fiance, who slid tlsat not one man out of 5,000 attended Church. There only women went to Church. He ventured to say they were doing mpre than that in England. It was -decided that next year's conference be field at Cardiff and Penarth. Mr. Arthur Henderson spoke before a meeting of 3,000 people—a political meeting was not in it. Tue spiritual fervour that WAS evidenced he should never forget. Concluding, Mr. Dunkerley said that the impression of the Mayor of Sunderland was that the P.S.A.'s stood for righteousness, sobriety and good citizenship. . _</p>
        <p>The Rev. WM. HENNESEY said he was delighted to see the first citizen of this town in the chair that evening. Experience told him that they were getting more men of high character to take part in public affairs. They were giving up their own time and their own inclinations in order to do some good for the town to which they belonged. He had spoken to GOO or 700 men early on Sunday morning, to learn something that was not so easily learnt when a man took too much liberty on a Saturday night. Their P.S.A. mosement was altogether on the side of elementary morality. When he was in Sydney the Prime Minister of that time told him they had 14 local preachers in the cabinet. The speaker proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor and the artistes. I Mr. ATKISSON seconded and was supported by Mr. FARRAR, and the motion was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S UNEMPLOYED.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S UNEMPLOYED.</p>
        <p>Corporation Urged to Undertake .</p>
        <p>allief Work.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Distress Committee was held at the Town Hall on Wednesday evening, Alderinan Tinnion presided and there were alse pre- sent:—Councillors Barton, Dickenson, Wilkinson and Wilson, Miss Broadbent, Rev. W. Bracecamp and John ,Yonge' Messrs. A. J. Jolley, W. C. Brooks, T. P. Bradshaw, John Haley, A. T. Hallaway (deputy town clerk), T. H. Spinks, (assistant town clerk), J. Fair- hurst (borough treasurer) and J. Fargher (inquiry officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>DEPUTY CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>DEPUTY CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Councillor WILICIN3ON, seconded by Rev. Jolts VONOE, Mr. A. J. Jolley was elected deputy-chairman in place of ex-Councillor Stott. Mr. JOLLEY said he was sure all present, like himself, regretted the cause which had made it necessary to appoint a deputy-chairman and they all wished ex-Councillor Stott every success and prosperity in his new sphere. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>TETE GROWLEG DTSTRESS. CORPORATION LTRGED TO UNDERTAKE WORKt</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Inquiry and Labour Sub-Com- mittee held on November 4th, it was resolved that Mr. A. J. Jolley be appointed chairman and the Rev. John Yonge the deputy chairman of the committee. Accounts were allowed amounting to £l5l 10s. 91. The annual report of the inquiry officer for the year en led 31st March last was subniitted, and approved. It showed that the operations of the committee during the three years of its existencehad been instrumental, with the valuable co-operation of employe is, in obtaining situa- tions for 1,091 persons. The, need of the committee was the means of providing useful employment for the deserving cases. It had been found that the majority of the cases which had been inquired into were unknown to the Poor-Law Guardians, and be therefore believed the committee had to a large extent succeeded in getting into touch with the honest and self-respecting who were too indapenient to take what they considered to be charity in the ordinary sense of the word and were therefore desirous of earning all they got. The following report of the Chairman (Mr. Jolley) on the Conference held in London on the Unemployed Workmen Act was read ;—"As requested by the Dis- tress Committee I attended the Conference at West- minster on December 120, 1907. I !Waisted your opinion that some amendment of the existing regula- tions in the direction of lessened stringency and increased elasticity was desirable. I also conveyed your resolution that the cost of providing employment under the Act should not be dependent upon voluntary con- tributions. 1 further suggested that the Executive should use its inflw rice with the Government to ensure the continuance (under the Expiring LawiContibustion Act) of the resent Act until the report of the Royal Commission on the Poor Laws had been issued. The suggestion the Chairman thought a very wise one, thanked me for it, and promised to lay it before the I Executive."</p>
        <p>Resolutions suggesting various amendments in the Unemployed Workineu Act were read from Birmingham, Kettering and Bournemouth. An invitation from the Right to Work Committee to attend a meeting to be held in London in December next to consider " the present exceptional distress " and the line to he urge upon the Government far permanent legislation was read and the committee decided not to he represented. The Inquiry Officer submitted his report on the work of the labour bureau from Ist April to date, from which it appeared that there were on the register at the present time 370 persons out of employment. It was resolved that the Clerk be requested to obtain informa- tion as to the number of unemployed from the various trade unions in the Borough. A discrssion took place on the provision of work for unemployed and it was resolved that this committee are of opinion that owing to the growing distress in the Borough due to unem- ployment the General Parposes Committee of tho Corporation be urged to undertake such works of utility as in their opinion are necessary to relieve the same. _ .</p>
        <p>The minutes were approved on the proposition of Mr. JOLLEY seconded by Councillor WILKINSON.</p>
        <p>FINA&amp;CE AND_GENERAL PURPOSE'S,</p>
        <p>At a meeting' of the Finance and General Parplses Committee held on November 4th it was resolved that Councillor Wilkinson be appointed chairman and Mr. A. J. Jolley deputy-chairman of the committee. The estimate of £2Ol for the ensuing year's expenditure was approved. The District Auditor reported that he had concluded Lis audit of the acounts. The total receipts amounted to £2Ol 75., the expenditure to 05 155., leaving a balance in hand of, £167 7s. sd. The minutes were conflrmed on the proposition of Councillor WILKTNSON, seconded by Mr. HALEY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>CIVIG I EAGUE. OF HELP.</head>
        <p>THE CIVIG I EAGUE. OF HELP.</p>
        <p>The Rev. VV. BRACECAMP said o was very much interested in the Civic League of Help. Several pre- liminary meetings bad been held and it was suggested that they should invite the chairmen and secretaries of existing organizations to attend a meeting and discuss the formation of a League in Warrington. He proposed that the chairman of that committee and the secretary be asked to attend the meeting of which due notice wonld be given. The•CHA,trDIAN said he ha sl full sympathy with the objects of the Leripe, which would *do something to regulate the various charities in Warrington and put them on a proper basis. He would be happy to attend a preliminary meeting but his time was so fully occu- pied that be could nit promii9 to take any further part in the work of the organization.</p>
        <p>JOHN WALKER, Sr CO. LITHOGR&amp;PHERe. BGOKBINDBRS. BTATIONBRS. BERSEY STREET WARRINGTON a</p>
        <p>!1:07.</p>
        <p>Farther down •tillese columns you will find a letter frein Bet:trio-6 Arnold. The euggestion !Lade is - very ' ,?!ood, e:r.,l I effer a P.O. -ler ?s. ed. for the best received. New I intia fiiii ;or this week, Willi love, UNCLE NED. Welcome to TiTilfrcel Priest and M. E. Parker. l'itiri,ors have been sent to Minnie Groom, Wilfred Priest, and May Groom.</p>
        <p>Dear 1.7,ne1e Ned,—Do you not think an essay ,Icooribing the difference between the Chaist- Inas of a very poor child and that of a rich th.:ld would be very ar,propriate for a- Christ- mas Competit:on? It is a very, easy subject to write .about, and one all the members -should enjoy. lam so glad so much interest has been taken in the "Kindness to Animals" ,Com- petition. I am afraid I shall not be cue of the fortuna,te ones, 5...3 the: csSays which have lately been printed sect much better than Mine; but, never 'mind, I shall try Hoping th:s will pieet with.your approval, I re- ina.n, your affectionate niece, BEATRICE ARNOLD. [This is a very gcod. idea, and I will' award to the sender of the best essay a P.O. for Zs. S.I.—UNCLE' "NED.] • KINDNESS TO ANIMALS.</p>
        <p>It is very nice to see all animals, Whatever kind they .be, treated kimlly. Although they are dumb, they can feel, and they do not often very soon forget an angry blow en unkind, sharp words. Some animals are very knowing, such as the dc, which is generally found where man is to be found. Being a very faithful anhnal, if treated kindly it is very useful, especially the big St. Bernard,/ to Which many people owe their lives.'</p>
        <p>We often see boys, and sometimes men, M- using dumb animals. .Some of them we could not -do without, such as the horse, which is us,ed for drawing heavy loads. From this comes the name 'of " the beast of burden." Also the cow, from which we obtain a part of cur fool and clothing. And other animals which were all brought into existence for some good use. For this reason we owe them all the kindness we can give them. .</p>
        <p>LIZZIE STJTCLIFFi.</p>
        <p>KINDNESS TO ANIMALS.</p>
        <p>We con be kind to • animals in many dif- ferent ways. First, in winter to give .the poor birds sonie crumbs to keep them from starv- ing. t•,:eeond, to be kind to them, not to throw stones at. i4lietn, or ao them any harm. .Third, td stop tiny one else if they ere doing them her-rn. If policemen see boys or girls robbing birds' nests they are generally summoned for it. We ought always. to be kind to dumb ani- mals. such as eats, dogs, etc. Every one ought to be kind to dumb animals.</p>
