      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday November 7, 1908. SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS. POSTAL AND SAYINUS BANK 27.13LAT13 NS</p>
        <p>For further particulars see "Poet Office Guide," at any Post Odice. POSTAGE STAMPS, Books containing 12 penny and 23 halfpenny stamps are now on sale at each post office. Price 2s.</p>
        <p>THE CARAT.</p>
        <p>This tiny standard of weight, although not one person in a million ever makes practical use of it, possesses great prestige because of its connection with diamonds and other pre- cious stones. But how many readers have any clear idea of the weight represented by carat? In England it is customary to reckon 1511 carats to the ounce Troy; This makes the carat equivalent to 2053 milligrams, or 31683 grains. An attempt is being made to secure general recognition in all countries of u metric standard carat of 200 milligram!.</p>
        <p>in AND CORRESPONDENCE.. The term " Inland Correspondence" means cor- respondence posted within the United Kingdom, including the Isle of Man and the Channel Islands. "Inland Correspondence" is divided into the following classes : (a) Letters (d) Newspapers (b) Postcards (e) Parcels (c) Halfpenny packets Not only letters, but any document or article (with a few trifiing exceptions) which can be trans- mitted by post at all, may be sent by letter post, the only limitation being that of size.</p>
        <p>LETTER Pon'. The prepaid rate of postage is as follows Not exceeding 4 ozs. in weight ... ld. For every additional 2 0z5.... Id. A letter posted unpaid is chargeable on delivery with double postage; a letter insufficiently paid, with double the deficiency. No letter may exceed two feet in length, one foot in width, or one foot in depth. , POST-CARDS. The prepaid rate of postage on every post-card Is one half-penny, and on every reply post-card one penny. A post-card posted unpaid is chargeable on delivery with one penny. Cards used as post-cards must be made of ordinary card-board, not thicker than the material used for the thickest official post-card. They may not exceed 5) inches in length by 31 incites in width, or be less than 31 inches ill length by 2:1 inches in width. A gummed label (not to exceed 2 inches long and 2-inch wide) bearing tho address may now be affixed to the address side of card. If any of the rules relating to a post-card bo infringed, the card will be treated as a letter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>HI;EcNrPACKFT POST.</head>
        <p>HI;EcNrPACKFT POST.</p>
        <p>'The Halfpenny Packet Post can be Used only for packets not exceeding 2oz. in weight. Printed matter, not in the nature of a letter, may be transmitted by this method. NEWSPAPER POST. . .</p>
        <p>The prepaid raVica-p-o—s4tnie On-r-t.iegistered news- paper, including any supplement thereto, whether posted singly orwith others is id. Newspapers not registered at the General Post Gaice aro treated in the post like any other printed matter.</p>
        <p>......... _.... PARC EL POST. The rates of postage on parcels areas follows: For a parcel ... ... not exceeding 1 lb. 0 3 ff exceeding 1 lb. ~ 2 lb. 0 4 „ 11 , 9-lb • lf 3 lb. 0 5 2, ff 3 lb. „ 5 lb. 0 6 If ff 51b. 21 71b. 0 7 ft 7 lb ft • ff 8 lb. 0 8 tf ft 8 lb. „ 9 lb. 0 9 ft ~ 9 lb. - „ 10 lb. 0 10 ' ~10 lb. 11 lb. 011</p>
        <p>ltio parcel may exceed 11 lb. in„ weight. The size allowed for an inland parcel is:— Greatest length... ... ... 3 ft. 6 in. ,Greatest length and girth combined 6 ft. 0 in. For example—A parcel measuring aft. Gin. in its longest dimensions as much as 2ft. Gin. round its thickest part ; a short parcel may be thicker; thus, if it measare no more than 311. in length, it may measure as much as aft. round its thickest part. The postage on a parcel must be prepaid. Mark 'each parcel Parcel Post." A parcel must not be posted in a letter box,-13ut presented at the counter of a post office, and the sender should see that the weight, size and postagoare in order before leaving. PARCELS (FOREIGN AND COLONIAL). Parcels are accepted for parcel post to most foreign countries under conditions similar to the inland regulations, except that for customs require- ments the contents must be declared. The customs charges can be prepaid or collected on delivery. The postage varies to different countries and must be prepaid. MONEY ORDERS (INLAND.) The postal authorities cannot issue a single money order for more than £4O, and will only cash them at the money order office at which they are made payable, unless they are crossed, when they can be paid through a bank. No fractional part of a id. can be contnined in a money order. The rates of poundage are :—Not exceeding El, 21.; £3, 3d.; £lO, 4d.; £2O, 63. ; £39, Bd. ; £4O, 10d. By paying an additional 2d. mid cost of the telegram of advice, a money order can be telegraphed under certain restrictions.</p>
        <p>MONEY ORDERS (FOREIGN AND COLONIAL.) The masiraum amount for which a motley order may be issued for places abroad varies in each., particular place. The poundage rates are :—Not exceedinc £1,3.1. ; £2, 6d. ; and above that, 3d. for each additional £2our portion of it. A fee of 64. in addition to the ordinary poundage, together with cost of telegram of advice, enables money orders to 'be telegraphed to certain places abroad. - POSTAL ORDERS. _ _ -</p>
        <p>_ These are obtainable at say money order office in the United Kingdom, and possess the advantage over money orders that they can be cashed at any money order office. If crossed, they can be paid into a bank. They are issued for fixed amounts, rising by 6d., from 6d. to RI Is. The poundage fees are up to 2s. 6d.; Id. up to 'Zs.; lid. up to 21s. Before parting with a postal order, always till in the name of the person to whom the antouat is to be paid. Each order is numbered, as is also the counterfoil attached, which latter should be retained and :name of .payee filled in. The Post- master-General. will entertain no further claim after an order is once paid. Stamps to the amount of sd. can be affixed to make up odd sums. : TELEGRAMS (INLAND):</p>
        <p>.• The Postmaster-General possesses,almost entirely, the privilege of transmitting,colleeing and deliver- ing telegrams in the United ICingdom. The charge for a telegram is a minimum amount of Gd. which covers 12 words, and an additional id. lor each extra word. This charge includes delivery within the postal district if sent out for delivery from a head telegraph office, but if from a sub-office then within threo miles only, beyond" which.a charge of .Id. per mile is 'made from the office door. All charges are, as far as possible, prepared by affixing stamps to telegram form. Address of person receiv- ing a telegram has to be paid for, hut sender's name if written on back of form need not be. Five figures count as one word. The cost of a reply may be prepaid.: Telegrams can be repeated on payment of half the original charge, which extra payment is refunded if error is detected. Telegrams may be posted in wall or pillar boxes, or handed to rural postmen, and are wired as soon as received at the nearest telegraph office. Late fees secure attention, in certain cases, to telegrams handed in after the usual hours. The cost: of registering an abbrevi- ated address, which must consist of two words, is .£1 is. TELEGRAMS (Formiam AND COLONIAL) Must be written ou special forms ander() accepted in the language of any of the principal European countries. The rates vary, ad are too numerous to detail here. POST OFFICE SAVINGS BANE.</p>
        <p>These era established at all money order offices. In ordinary cases the annual deposit must not exceed .£5O, rind the total amount .C• 2120. No person can have more than one account. Amounts placyll in the bank on f)ehalf of children under seven years of age cannot be withdrawn miffl the child reaches that age, and then only under his or her own signa- ture. The smallest amount received at a time is Is., and this may take the form of 12 penny stamps affixed to a special form. Each depositor receives a bank book hi which the amount of each deposit should ho entered at the time of paying in. If the deposit is El or over, a special acknowledgment is sent from the head London °lice. Interest is allowed at the rate of 211 per cent. Any amount up to and including RI mly he withdrawn without previous notice, on presenting a bank book. The bank book should be sent each year, postage free, on the anniversary of the date when the account was opened, to the Controller of the Savings Bank Department for purposes of checking and adding interest. The withdrawal of money can bee xpedited by telegraphing, the depositor paying cost of telegrams. P.O. T ELEPTIONE AND TIMEX./ LINE CHARGES. The fees for the use of the Trunk lines are as follows: For 25 miles or under ... 7 50 PI ~ 75 fr „ 100 ;, " „ every additional 40 miles or fraction thereof... ... 04.</p>
        <p>Between' the hours of 7 p.m. and 7 ri.m.,-eix minutes conversstlon Ls allowed for the same price as three minutes betneen 7 a.m. and 7 p.m., and a reduced charge is made for three ruinu:e calls.</p>
        <p>MOTOR CAR AND CYCLE REOULATIONS.</p>
        <p>Each one must be registered with the council of a county or county borough, the fee being £1 bored motor car, and ss. for a motor cycle. A register number is assigned to each, which it is an offence not to keep adixed when driving. Each driver must p.issess a license, costing 55., to be renewed every 12 months, and obtainable from the county authorities, which may be endorsed 9r suspended for reckless driving or certain other %offences. The highest speed at which a mat may be driven on the public highways i 420 miles an hour, which is reduced to 10 miles under special local regula- tions. It is an offence under the Orator Car Act (1003), to drive a car in such a way on a public highway an to be dargerous to the public, having regard Se aII the circumstances of the case. In the event of an accident to any person due to bo presence of the motor car on the road, the river must supply his name and address, and the twner and number of the car, under heavy penal - tulle The driver mutt produceh is licence at the sequbst of a police constable.</p>
      </div>
