      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>PROCESS FOR STAINING WOOD.</head>
        <p>A PROCESS FOR STAINING WOOD.</p>
        <p>Hitherto wood has been stained by impreg- nating it while still fresh with a solution of some colouring matter. The solution TM squeezed into the wood under a high pres- sure. According to a new Swiss process, the wood is impregnated with a solution of a colouring matter in hydrocarbons such as petroleum. For this purpose the wood is placed in a cask filled with the coloured solu- tion so as tO. be, completely coyered. There it remains until it is thoroughly impregnated by the solution. The staining in the cask may be effected with or without pressure, cold or warm. In this manner it is possible to stains any wood, either fresh or dry. /</p>
        <p>PHOTOGRAPHING THE STOMACH. An apparatus has been devised by a physician in Munich, by which the inside of the stomach can be clearly photographed. The camera is actually swallowed by the patient, and when it reaches its destination the interior of the- stomach is illuminated by a small electric lamp attached to the appa- ratus. At the bottoM 'of the camera is wound a photographic film 201 n. long and iin. wide. The physician pulls a cord which runs the film past the lens, the electric- lamp is turned on, and the impression is made on the sensitive film. This procedure is re- peated until the requisite number of pictures have been taken. ,</p>
        <p>QUARTZ GLASS. Quartz is an impure form of silicie acid, and quartz glass is, therefore, a glass con- sisting chiefly of al-lido acid. whereas ordi- nary.glass contains silicic acid in combina- tion. with Jim, soda, potash, or lead. The great advantage of quartz glass as compared with ordinary -glass is that it has a melting- point 800 deg. Cent. higher, and that it is net fractured by sudden, changes of tempera- ture. A vessel of quartz glass may be heated far above the melting -point -Of glass (I,2oodeg. Cent.), and' may then be plunged into cold water, without risk of breaking. Other important properties of quartz are, that it is neither hygroscopic nor soluble in acids, and alkaline solutions affect it less than they affect ordinary glass, except at the higher temperatures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>FULL AND HALF MOON LIGHT.</head>
        <p>FULL AND HALF MOON LIGHT.</p>
        <p>Many people • may not be aware of the fact that the full moon . gives several times more than twice the light of the half moon. They may be still more surprised; to learn • that the ratio is approximately 9 to I. Professor Joel Stebbins and F. C. Brown; taking advantage of the extreme 'sensitiveness to light of a selenium cell, have measured . the amount of light • coming from the mbon at different phases, with the result above mentioned.. Tho renon for the remarkable difference sheWn is to be found, Gays Science Siftings, in the varying angles of reflection presented by. the roughened surface of our satellite to the sun. The moon is brighter between full and Jest quarter. The cause of this is evi- dent in the more highly reflective character of that part of the moon which lies west of its meridian.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>lONS IN MEDICINE:</head>
        <p>lONS IN MEDICINE:</p>
        <p>• The medical profession in general shew a praiseworthy eagerness to utilise the latest scientific discoveries ' for the alleviation or cure of human disease. This is well illus- trated by the wide adoption of radium and the X-rays-in medicine.. Another interesting ease is the medical use of ions. These were first used as a curative agent by Leduc, of Nantes, in 1903. "Ion," it may be noted, is the term employed by chemists to denote an atom or mole.enle carrying a charge of posi- tive or negative electricity. Literally an ion is a thing that goes, and the term is appro- priate, according to the theory of electra- lysis, or the, breaking up of a compound by electricity. For wiv_ln 'the eleMric current is passed, say, through acidulated water, tho hydrogen molecules, in s virtue of their eke. trio charge, go to the cue electrode, and the oxygen molecules to the other.</p>
        <p>lONS PASS INTO TISSUES.</p>
        <p>The idea of using these ions, molecules rendered active by electricity, in medicine, depends on the fact tlkat they can be Made to pass. directly into the tissues. This is illustrated by the following experiment. The positive pole 9f a battery is covered with blotting-paper soaked in potassium iodine, and the negative pole with starch paper. A piece of raw beef is placed between them, and a current- passed. Tha. starch paper turns blue, shewing that ions of iodine have passed throUgh the beef. This illustrates the way in, whicli the ions, are used in medi- cine. Supposing that' it •is required to ad- minister quinine in this way. The positive pole -of a ~battery • is covered with a pad soaked in. quinine 611lphate, nncl applied to the skin. 'ether pole cf the, battery is held In the ,hand iof • the Patient, or Is m- mersed in a 'solution of salt in which the hand is placed: . CATAPHORESIS On TOiStIC MEDICATION.</p>
        <p>What happens, says the Globe, is that the quinine ions sktng the negative pole go right through the tissuts. They enter not merely the lymph spaces but the ,cells also, becoming a part of the protoplasm. Here, then, we have the. means of bringing the remedy - into the mcet intimate contact with the tissues, and at the .same time of carrying with it an electric charge. To this .system of ionic medication the term cataphoresis, which means literally a carrying -down, has, .been applied. The medicines which have been successfully administered in this way are cocaine' zinc, copper, magnesium, chlorine, iodine, and salicylic acid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>LEAVES AS CAMERA LENSES.</head>
        <p>LEAVES AS CAMERA LENSES.</p>
        <p>A remarkable property in the, leaves of plants was !brought to the notice of the botanists \at tha Bri(esh Association by Pro- fessor Wager. He ehewed that the outer lnyer of many leaves was so constructed that the rays of light were - brought to a focus in , the interior of the leaf, and that, in fact, the cel:e behaved as convex lenses. As an _illus- tration of the lens-like property of the leaves, Professor Wager shewed a number of photo- graphs taken through these living lenses. l'heee we re • remarkably clear photographs thrown upon the screen, and .were well-de- fined productions of photographs of Huxley and Darwin, first-hand photographs of the author's niece, of landscapes, houses, trees, a tobacco - pipe, and so forth. In every case there was good definition, and the photo- graphs amply • justified the author's conten- tion ' that the plant cells acted as efficient lenses. They even gave satisfactory.„ results with singla-coleur photographs by the Lunaibre process.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>ANDROMEDA NEBULA.</head>
        <p>THE ANDROMEDA NEBULA.</p>
        <p>• One of the most astonishing objects in the heavens, especially when photographed, is the great nebula in the constellation Andre- Meda, which is visible as a misty speck to the naked eye. It has long puzzled astronomers, because while its structure—a series of vast rings surrounding a, central mars—suggests a gaseous constitution, its spectrum is continu- ous, resembling that of the sun. It has been suggested that it may be composed of stars constituting a universe external to ours. Re- cent studies of its parallax, however, indi- cate that it is nearer to us than some of the well-known stars, such kaa Capella, and Mr. Ellard---Gore, the astronomer, points out that if the Andromeda nebula were assumed to be an, external universe, ,having a diameter comparable with that of 'the Milky Way, its mass would be forty million million million ttmes tile mass of .the sun. iiiis is regarded as incredible, and so may be taken as an addi- tional argument in favour of the view that Ws nebula is a, member of titvr system. .</p>
      </div>
