      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Merthyr's First Visit to Warrington.</head>
        <p>Merthyr's First Visit to Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Merthyr Club paid their first visit to Warrington on Saturday, and a crowd of quite 5,000 assembled to give the South Wales men a cordial welcome. Treharne (late of Wigan) and George Thomas turned out for Warrington, who had not, however, their best side. Fine weather prevailed but the ground was very wet owing to early morning rain. Teams : Warriugton—J. Tilley, beck; L. Treharne, W. Lander, J. Daintith, and H. Bradshaw, three-quarter backs; H. Jenkins and S. Lees, half backs; V. Dowell, J. Neill, W. Cheaters, A. Harmer, A. Naylor and G. Thomas, forwards...</p>
        <p>orthyr—J. Padd eson, Lack ; S. James, W. McGregor, Ponty Thomas and Wigattee, three-quarter hacks; Cowmeadow and Harrison, half hacks ; G. Thoplas, W. D. Thomas, T. Jenkins, W. Beyan, D. Lewis, and D. Davies, forwards. Referee, Mr. T. H. King, Salford ; touch judges, Messrs, B. Ennion, iltigan, and Towns, Leigh. Play opened in very desultory fashion, the wet ball preventing accurate handling on both sides. Good work by Penty Thomas and McGregor named Warring- ton to fall back, and Tilley was busily occupied in dealing with several forward rushes. A rapid visit to the Merthyr end almost culminated in a score, for Cheaters charged a kick by Paddeson, but failed to take advantage of the opening. Merthyr responded well,oqly to find Tilley at his best, and at the interval there was no score.</p>
        <p>nresurning,Warrington exerted themselves to effect and a clever breakaway by Lees and Thomas resulted in a try to the former. Thomas missed the goal, but a moment later had hard luck in having a try disallowed after dribbling pest the full back and over the line. Merthyr replied pluckily and got close to the home line bk good forward play. Jenkins relieved, and in a visit to the other end Warrington had a penalty award, and Thomas kicked his first goal of the season. The visitors tiled hard to score. but Tilley's defence was splendid. Final : Warrington, 5 points ; Merthyr, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>LEIGH v. WIDNES.</head>
        <p>LEIGH v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At Leigh, before 4,000 spectators. Leigh attacked at once, and after a dribble smith forced his way over the line,. Sam Johtson kicking a goal. Winstanlev got a try Just afterwards, but no goal resulted. Following a long spell of dull play Lee got over from a scrummage, S. Johnson kickinka goal. At the interval Leigh led by 2 goals and 3 tries (13 points) to nil. On resuming Widncs bungled a very easy chance of scoring. Then Winstanley followed up a kick, and dribbled oNer the line, Smith scoring an unimproved try. Widnes were now a beaten team, and Winstanley rushed over, but again the place kick failed. Tom Johnson thiew smartly to his brother Sam, aho got over behind the posts, und also kicked a goal. G. Aspey and nicks each scored a try for Widnes, the former also kicking a goal. Result :—Leigh 3 goals, 6 tries (21 points) ; Widnes 1 goal, 2 tries (8 points),</p>
        <p>RUNCORN WIN AT KEIGHLEY. Early on Keighley missed an easy goal. Drummond and termer worked hard for Keighley, and Hughes and O'Kell for Runcorn. The latter began to have more control of the tail, and after one fine passing movement, which went wrong•with a forward tranfer, Butterworth got over with an unconverted try, Then. thanks to a grand bit of work by Ferrer, the Keigbley scrurumagers got to close quarters, and Bladcs ploughed his way over, giving Craven a goal. This two points advantage was maintained to a late stage, when Langley tranferred to the Runcorn Walker, who scored. Moran's kick went wide, Lut Runcorn were left the victors by 2 tries points), to Keigbley 1 goal 1 try (5 points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>ST. DELI.NS v. SALFORD.</head>
        <p>ST. DELI.NS v. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>These teams met at St. Helens before 5,000 spectators. St. Helens bad the best of the opening play, but before long, Salford scrummaged at the home end, and Cook kicked to the front of the goal, where Barton mulled, and Thomas scored. Lomas failed at goal. Hull secured at the St. Helens end, and after making a good opening, the ball was passed to Barton, who ran down, and later gave to Lee, who feinting to pass, scored under the post. Smith improved. A scrummage was formed near the Salford line, and Tremwith dashed over, scoring a brilliant try. Half-time score :--St Ilelens, 1 goal 2 tries (9 points) ; Salford, l try (3 points). In the second portion play was evenly contested. Salford opened out through Hymn, and the leather being kicked cowards Cook, he gathered, and after a splendid run be scored behind the posts. Lomas landed an easy goal. Briers made a fine burst for St. Helens, but Ccchraue easily cleared. Near the close Thomas picked up end scored, for Lomas to again improve. Result :—Sallord, 2 goals 3 tries (13 points.), St. Heler,s, I gall 2 tries (8 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>RUGBY UNION.</head>
        <p>RUGBY UNION.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>STdCKTON HEATH V. BIRKENHEAD PARK 2ND.— These teams met at Bhkeehead Park on Saturday before a good attendance. he visitors were the first to become aggressive, and the baciss proved to be in excellent trim, several dashing rues causing the Birkenhead defence serious trouble. Fine passing initiated by Warren eneed in Ferguson scoring a brilliant try. No grill follosced. The home side next bad a spell of attacking, and after Brannan had started a passing movement Bussett completed a try which was not augmented. At haif-time the score was a try each. Stockten Leath played splendidly in the second half, and getting well down the field, Daintith took a pass and a try resulted, Warren putting on a good goal. The visitors had the measure of their opponents throughout this half, and another goal being scored before the close Stockton Heath won by two goals, one try to BM:ea:end Park one try.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH 2ND V. BIRKENHEAD PARK 3RD.— At Stockton Heath on Saturday. From a forwarcrTcah Jones scored for Stockton Heath, Gibbs goaling. Cooper next crossed with a brilliant try. Clare again increased the home 'cad, Gibbs again adding the goal points. Interval : —Stock ton Heath, 13 points ; Birken- head, nil. On natal-Ong, Cooper scored. Gibbs converted. Cooper again crossed, the goal kick failing Result :—Stockton Heath, 'a points ; Birkenhead nil.</p>
      </div>
