      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>QUESTIONS FOR TARIFF REFORMERS</head>
        <p>QUESTIONS FOR TARIFF REFORMERS</p>
        <p>IV.—lf Imports cause unemployment, how is it that as our imports have increased our unemployed have not increased in like proportion?</p>
        <p>On this head let there be quoted the official verdict of the Board of Trade in the Second Fiscal Blue Book. After examining the facts relating to unemployment during the last forty years, this is what the officials wrote in :</p>
        <p>"The average level of employment during the last four years has been almost exactly the same as the average of the preceding forty years." Again, if we compare our imports and the Trade Union unemployment rate for a long period we find : tinem- Import,4 ployinent Imports plovment miTiton £. per cent. million E. per cent. 1887 ... 362 ... 7.5 1898 ... 471 ... 3.0 1888 ... 388 ... 49 1899 ... 485 ... 2.4 1889 ... 427 ... 21. 1900 ... 523 ... 2.9 1890 ... 421 ... 2.1 1901 ... 522 ... 38 1891 ... 435 ... 35 1902 ... 528 ... 4.4 1892 ... 424 ... 6.3 1903 ... 543 ... 51 1893 ... 405 ... 75 1904 ... 551 ... 6.5 1894 ... 408 ... 6.9 1905 .. 565 ... 5.4 1895 ... 417 ... 5-8 1906 ... 608 ... 4.1 1896 ... 442 ... 3.4 1907 ... 646 ... 4.2 1897 ... 451 ... 35 It will be seen that it is certainly not true tbet as imports increase unemployment increases. On the contrary, it is when imports fall that unemployment increases, as in 1892 and 1893.</p>
        <p>L. C. C. 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>Motor-Cars in the Election.</p>
        <p>Sir,—During the recent Municipal Election there was an incident in which one or two motor cars were lent for the occasion to convey voters to the poll. It is alleged that some party applied them to the service of the opponent. Someone has coupled my name wth the matter. If they will come forward and substantiate the statement I will pay £2O to the Infirmary as I have never directly or indirectly interested myself oa either side, and I consider that the report is doing me an injury.—l remain, yours truly, THOMAS CROMPTON.</p>
        <p>Market Place:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Unemployment and Socialism.</head>
        <p>Unemployment and Socialism.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your remarks last week on the Problem of the Hour would be approved by all who believe in legisla- ting upon a sound enonomic basis. What did the panicmongers, led by Mr. Burns's enemies expect. Did they imagine that because Mr. Burns was a firebrand in his earlier days be should remain so in spite of increased knowledge and experience. As an exemplar of moral courage in difficult circumstances he has few equals. He had to choose between popularity and honesty and because he chose the latter he is called a traitor. Le us hope that traitors of that kind will increase an. multiply. Whether Mr. Burns's policy will be as successful as it deserves to be is, I am afraid doubtful, because so much depends upon the promptitude and energy of local authorities. The Unemployed Workmen Act of 1905 should be amended without delay, in order to give more freedom and lati- tude to local bodies. It wou:d seem that this Act only permits local Councils to provide for establishment charges and purchases of land, and does not provide the means for carrying on the schemes, and the Edinburgh Distress Committee is already in difficulties on this point. But in most cases if local authorities go properly to work and fully recognise their responsi- bilities, they are not likely to be checked or hindered much whilst Mr. Burns is at the wheel. In the meantime I am glad to see that a few of the more courageous Socialists are beginning to put some of their visionary ideas into words, because as soon as these illusions are formulated and definitely stated,there will be little difficulty in dealing with them. One of their schemes I notice has just been hatched in Warring- ton. Probably it will be presented to Mr. Victor G rayson when he comes to give us another humorous entertain- ment:shortly. This scheme, which I understand is to be seriously discussed without smiling, suggests that an Imperial Council ot 15 should appoint local committees to deal with unemployment and to carry on all kinds of trade and industry. They have to determine the amount of labour required from each workman for the wages paid, and to provide skilled instruction in the various trades. Each of these skilled workmen I understand will get 12s. 6d.per week in paper and he has to spend it all at the Government retail stores. If there is anything left out 'Of the 12s. 6d after the wife and family have been well fed and properly clothed it will, I suppose, have to go to a common socialist fund. They won't allow any extravagance and riotous living. The amus- ing part of this and similar hare-brained schemes is the large way in which the "State" is referred to as though the State was an automatic machine for providing everything and quite ; independent of cause and effect. I presume we shall ' hear more particulars as to management axpenses, profit and loss, how competition and supply and demand are to be regulated, whether workhouses and Trades Unions will be abolished, what pension will be given and at what age. But perhaps the new Socialist school of political economy does not consider these are matters of any consequence. They don't like to be bothered with facts. How refreshing it is to compare such foolish and Utopian ideals with Sir. C. Furness's Co-operative Shipyard scheme which his workmen have accepted by a majority of ten to one. They are offered shares at 4 per cent, interest guaranteed, to be paid for gradually, out of earnings. This is the most hopeful sign of the times.—Yours, &amp;c., Latchford.</p>
        <p>W. CHIUMES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Mr. James Ball's " Atheism " and the</head>
        <p>Mr. James Ball's " Atheism " and the</p>
        <p>" Guardian's " Neutrality. Sir,—The letter I herewith enclose is a verbatim copy of that which yesterday I sent to the Editor of the "Guardian." _ _</p>
        <p>Why, as the Editor of a " neutral " newspaper, he I should print so reduced a paragraph as that which i appears n the " Guardian " of to-day (Wednesday), is best known to himself. I regret now that I troubled him at all, but since he calls his paper " neutral " and edits it as "Tory," we shall have no difficulty hence- forth in understanding his position. I assume that in your columns' at any rate, I need have no fear of the scissors of the Editor. The cause is bad which either hides itself from criticism, or can vaunt itself in a victory which has been helped by unblushing and slanderous misrepre- sentation. I am sincerely sorry that in these enlightened days, and in this especially enlightened (?) town, there are gentlemen to be found who can boast in a victory which, at least, has been helped by such utterly ignominious methods !—Yours faithfully, JOHN YONGE. Sir,—Will you allow me to bear my testimony publicly in your columns that Mr. James Ball, who was defeated en Monday in St. John's Ward, is not, and never was,an Atheist ? He has been known to me from his boyhood, his family having been connected with Wycliffe Church. He was a Sunday Scholar, and is to this day a seat- holder, and also an enrolled Church member. He is also a very able and acceptable preacher, having exercised his gifts as an occasional preacher in several Nonconformist pulpits, and has preached at Wycliffe with considerable acceptance.</p>
        <p>I desire to place these facts, officially, as his minister before the people of Warrington, as a testimony that is due to him, and as a contradiction which may dispel the slanderous accusation recently made that he is an Atheist. Really ! it is an illustration of what astound- ing ignorance exists in some persons who pass as intelligent, that the idea should prevail that to be a Socialist is the same as to be an Atheist ! It is a.bso- lutely contrary to the actual fact. The present House of Commons contains many Labour representatives, most or all of whom profess Socialist views. Nearly all of them are well-known religious men, belonging to Nonconformist Churches, and many of them doing good service to their denominations as lay-preachers of the Gospel. For such men to be defamed as Atheist is nothing less than infamous slander. Their political -views may not be acceptable to certain religious pro- fessors. But that is no excuse for their being scorned and held up to public execration as deniers and opponents of the Christian faith. If Mr. James Ball was too much of an Atheist to be qualieed to continue as representative of St. John's AVard, why was he deemed Christian enough to address a men's Bible class belonging to St. Peter's Church in that Ward ? What were the defenders of Christianity doing in three times over asking him to undertake so high a duty as that? Christian enough to teach a Bible class, hut so awfully Atheist that he could not be tolerated as a Town Councillor ! The Conservative Churchmen and politicians are not to be congratulated upon their devotion either to fair play or truth ! I write strongly because I feel strongly! My regret is that I did not know what was being publicly said in time to attend one of Mr. Ball's meetings to testify ray entire knowledge of, and confidence in, his Christian character. I trust you will enable me to bear. that testimony in these lines of indignant protest against a cowardly act of defamation,—Faithfully yours, JOHN YONGE. 26, Ellesmere-road, November 3rd, 1009.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>BOWLERS AT DINNER.</head>
        <p>BOWLERS AT DINNER.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE COUNTY ASSOCIATION. Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., Presents Prizes.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., as president of the Lancashire and Cheshire County Bowling Association, presided at the annual dinner in connection with that organization which was held at the Albion Hotel, Piccadilly, Manchester, on Saturday evening. There was an attendance of about 150 followers of the sport, representing clubs from a much wider are than that embraced by the association, some of the visitors coming from Yorkshire, Worcestershire and Staffordshire. A large contingent of Warrington bowlers put in an appearance, and the proceedings were of special interest to them in view of the fact that the good old town figured prominently in the prize list and after dinner the winners had tha pleasure of receiving their awards at the hands of Mr. Crosfield. Amongst those present were Alderman John Evans, Councillors W. B. Roberts and a strong muster of members of the Bull's head and Cemetery teams. It will be remembered that Warrington Bull's Head achieved the proud dis- tinction of winning the club championship which entitles them to held the Caosfield Challenge Cup for twelve months, their friends and rivals, the Warring- 'ton Cemetery team being the runners-up. A further honour to Warrington was that one of the trundlers, T. Ellison, of Warrington Cemetery, proved the winner of the individual merit handicap; and with the one exception of tne runners-up—T. Glover, of Leigh Railway—all the other names on the prize list in this contest were Warringtonians. Mr. Crosfield was accompanied at the cross table by ' Mr. J. W. G. Coombs, the deputy president, Mr. Charles Smith, secretary, and representatives of the various counties. After dinner a toast list was gone through and there were also some enjoyable musical items.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>LOYAL TOAST.</head>
        <p>THE LOYAL TOAST.</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfield, in submitting the toast of " The King and Queen and members of the Royal Family " said he was certain that entirely without distinction of party, creed or sect,everybody in that representative gathering, be he Englishman Irishman, Scotsman or Welshman, would feel truly glad that the King was in such splendid health and vigour. (Loud applause.) He did not think there was anybody in England who did not fully realize and appreciate how much we owed to the influence which His Majesty, with his tact and knowledge of the world, his genial good nature and sagacity and his extra- ordinary energy for a man of his years, had been able to exercise not only in our own country but throughout the length and breadth of Europe and, he was sure ho might add, throughout the civilized world. (Applause). For that very reason they must all feel great and deep satisfaction in knowing that the King, with his strong constitution, enjoyed so fine a measure of health and activity, and they hoped that for many years, at their annual gatherings in Manchester, they would have the pleasure of continuing to driuk his health. (Applause.) Even since she came many years ago, a sea king's daughter, over the wave from Denmark, they had always had a warm place in their hearts—a feeling of affection as well as of respect for Her Majesty the Queen— (applause)—and they all appreciated too, the way in which the Prince and Princess of Wales and the other members of the Royal Family fulfilled so many public functions and duties, to the good of the State— (applause)—making them proud of the Royal Family as they were of the King and Queen. (Loud applause). I The toast was most heartily honoured.</p>
        <p>BRITISH CROWN GREEN BOWLING ASSOCIATION. THE COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfield said he had now the exceedingly pleasant duty of presenting to Mr. Coombs, the Captain of Lancashire, the cup which be was bound to say as a Lancashire man he was very glad that the county of red roses bad succeeded in carrying off. (Applause.) They had been watching of recent years the astonish- ing development of the good old English game of bowls throughout the country ; and he had no doubt they were all sufficiextly sentimental to be very proud of it, because it was a good old English game, going back a very long way in our history. (Hear, hear.) There was the thrice-told story of the Spanish Armada and the English Admirals in the days of good Queen Bess, who were having their game of bowls and who held their foreign foes so remarkably cheap that they finished their game and polished off each other on the green before they went and polished off the Spaniards. (Laughter.) He was not suggesting, added Mr. Cros- field, that we should treat any potential adversaries of ours in these days as cheaply as those admirals treated the Spaniards—(laughter)—neither was he suggesting that we should be too anxious to conjure up war scares first in oneand then in another part of the world—(hear, hear)—but that was by the way. The story bad always appealed to him as a happy illustration of British sangfroid and &amp;so as a very practical proof of the antiquity of the English pastime of bowls. (Hear,hear.) He thought it was partly because the game was so deeply rooted in English history and because one saw those beautiful old bowling greens in quaint old places all over the country, that one felt doubly interested to see the new lease of life which the game had taken in these modern days and particularly the latest develop- ment of county bowls which had proved such a great success and which owed so much to the efforts of his friend Mr.Coombs. (Applause.) He would be antici- dating what it would be his happy lot to say in h moment or two if he expatiated at any further length on that pleasant topic, because they were soon to make a fitting recognition of Mr. Coombs' work. Inter- county bowls now flourished and prospered in England and they all anticipated for it a still greater future. (Applause.) Ile was happy to congratulate the mem- bers of the Lancashire team on their success in winning the cup this year and he had the greatest pleasure in handing the trophy to the Captain, Mr. Coombs (Loud applause.) Mr. Coombs, in accepting the trophy, said he felt deeply grateful to the President of the Association for the active interest he took in that magnificent old pastime. Their friends frona Yorkshire, he was sure, would not think it an undeserved honour for Lancashire to win the cup, because, after all, the probabilities were that in due course Yorkshire would certainly be very severe competitors for that handsome trophy which their friend, the President, had been good enough to present to the executive of the British Crown Green Association, to be competed for annually by the various counties. The Lancashire team felt very proud of the honour of fighting for and winning the cup this year. They did not appreciate it solely for its intrinsic value, but because it had been won in a contest which would go down to history as the first huge county competition that had taken place in organized bowls in thts country. The cup represented what had been accomplished after many years work by those who had been actively identi- fied with the bowling world. He hoped it would never pass into the hands of anyone who would not esteem it a high honour to win it. (Applause). Mr. Crosfield then handed the gold medals to the members of the team, which iucluded the following local players :—G. Holbrook, Rylands's • G. Ball, jun., Warrington „cemetery ; T. Ellison, Warrington Cemetery ; A. Gleave, Bull's Head. Mr. Cartwright, the president of the Yorkshire Asso- ciation, accepted gold-centre medals on behalf of the runners up. He said he was only sorry that the cup I could not go to Yorkshire this year, for the team began ( the season with a determination to win it. Their</p>
        <p>.. Lancashire friends might congratulate themselves that Yorkshire had rather wet weather for two of their matches. (Laughter.) It would not be through want of trying, if the cup did not go to Yorkshire next year. However, they knew how to accept defeat as well as to win, and they heartily congratulated Lancashire on their victory. (Applause.) He hoped the Crown Green Association would have a long and roperous career.</p>
        <p>(Applause.) PRESENTATION TO MR. COONIBS.</p>
        <p>A very pleasing ceremony now took place,namely, the presentation to Mr. Coombs of an illuminated address, a silver tea and ooffee service, silver rose bowl and an _</p>
        <p>Opal and diamond bracelet for Mrs. Coombs as a tribute to the valuable work which the Deputy- President bas done for tha Association during the past 21. years. The address sets forth the members' apprecia- tion of Mr. Coombs' kindness and courtesy and expresses the hope that he may long be spared to con- tinue his good offices. Mr. Crosfield, having read the address, referred to the large amount of useful work done by Mr. Coombs while he had during the past 21 years been so honourably identified with the work ef the Association. That assembly represented a great number of clubs in the counties of Lancashire and Cheshire, and it was of course chiefly in connection with the spread of the game in those counties—though by no means exclusively so—that Mr. Coombs' admirable and valuable services had been rendered (Applause.) He (Mr. Crosfield,) appreciated those services certainly not less than anyone in the rorm, because he was personally indebted to Mr. Coombs for the assistance and advice he had given him when as a mere tyro at the work of the Association, he a few years since, assumed his present position. Having been in contact from time to time with Mr. Coombs, he appreciated his unfailing courtesy and kindness, and he could testify personally to the deep interest Mr. Coombs took in the success of the Association. (Applause.) Besides work connected with the Associa- tion Mr. Coombs had, in the last two or three years,— and their friends from Yorkshire and other Counties would bear testimony to the fact,—sought" fresh fields and pastures new " and had played no small part in bringing about this development of inter-County bowls which so certainly and directly added to the interest and popularity of the game. (Cheers.) The way in which bowling had gone ahead in the last</p>
        <p>few years was even more extraordinary than the way in which another game to which he Mr. Croatiald, was somewhat addicted—(laughter)—had,if he might borrow politisal phrase ` swept the country.' But even the game of golf could not compare in the numbers of its m.ctims—(laughter)—with the hundreds of thousands in England who had taken to bowls with this remarkable enthusiasm. Mr. Coombs, like their humble servant, was hoping to see the Crown Green Bowling Association embrace more and more of the Counties of England, so that besides the honourable competitions which they had in Lancashire and Cheshire bringing their own I Champions to the front, they might in future see the best men from each county brought together in friendly rivalry at a central club like the Marylehone Club in London, so that,to continue the comparison,the championship of bowls,like the championship of cricket might become one of the most notable events of our national pastimes. (Loud applause.) In making the presentation Mr. Crosfleld expressed particular pleasure that Mrs.Coombs had been remembered. For his part, he hoped that no developments of modern life would ever be permitted to destroy the deference and chivalry which was still expected in England from men towards women. (Applause.) They wished Mr. Coombs and his wifelong life and prosperity, and hoped they would be able to make use of those beautiful gifts for many years to come. (Applause.) Mr. J. Knowles, of Didsbury (chairman of the Testi- monial Committee) cordially endorsed Mr. Crosfield's observations, and caused some amusement by quoting the remark of a Scotch friend with reference to Mr. Coombs: "He'd have made a grand member of Parliament." Mr. Coombs, who was much touched by the splendid reception accorded him, responded in feeling terms. He said he had always experiencei the greatest kindness at the hands of the members of the Association and he was deeply indebted to them for that magnificent present and particularly to the President for the kind things he had said in making the presentation. He had endeavoured to do a little to promote the interests of bowls and he hoped his services would not be dispensed with because he had received that present. (Applause.) The kindness and good fellowship shown towards him would be ample recompense for a life's work, let along twenty-one years. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, November 7, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, November 7, 1908.</p>
        <p>" THE PR EST DEN r."</p>
        <p>--- - Mr. Coombs then proposed the health of "The President." He said they all appreciated the help Mr. Crosfield had given to the association. He had been kindness itself and generosity to an extreme and he (Mr. Coombs) was sure their friends from Yorkshire and other countries would re-echo all he was saying. (flear, hear.) Mr. Crosfield was perhaps not able to come among them as often as they would like but his duties in other walks of life made great calls upon him and they believed he was doing good not only to the constituency which he represented but to a still wider field. Last year he (Mr. Coombs) remarked that some day they might see Mr. Crosfield fulfilling the duties of Prime Minister, and he also jokingly added that they hoped soon to see him with a partner in life. Since that occasion Mr. Crosfield, without even asking the consent of the association, had got married. (Laughter.) They certainly had hopes that before taking , such an important step he would have laid the facts before them so that they might be able to judge on the question. (Laughter.) But no doubt Mr. Crosfield had good reason for feeling that he had sufficient judgment of his own to take such a step without consulting them. (Hear, hear.) Joking apart, if they had not at the head of their association a gentleman like Mr. Crosfield who provided such magnificent prizes and whose name and commercial reputation ranked so high, he did not know what they would do to promote the progress of the game and of that Association in particular. They owed a debt of gratitude to supporters like Mr. Crosfield who, while actually engaged in political work, still devoted time to encouraging those who had the interests of the pastime at heart. (Applause). The toast was honoured with great enthusiasm, the. company joining in singing " For he's a jolly good fellow." Cheers were given for Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield</p>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD'S REPLY. BOWLERS AND TEIE DAYLIGHT SAVING BILL. Mr. Crosfield, in responding, said he thanked Mr. Coombs with all his heart for the kind remarks he had made and the meeting for the way in which they had received the toast. "I can assure you " he continued "that lam full not only of gratitude but of astonish- ment because I always used to think that a miserable politician stood not to be patted on the back in this kindly way, but to be shot at. (Laughter.) Surely times must be changed—(laughter)—for it is no t very long since the unfortunate politician was a sort of target for rotten eggs and other missiles (laughter.) Between you and rue, before leaving the House of Commons I told one of the Whips that I was coming down to dear old Edwin Waugb's county and I added' I suppose you have read his poem 'Nomination' (Laughter.) Well, if they are not satisfied with the work of the Government I may get the same sort of reception as that unlucky mortal who went to Nomina- tion—and if I do it will be some time before you will be able to count upon me in the Lobby." (Laughter.) Well, said Mr. Crosfleld, it was a very agreeable surprise to get a very different kind of reception from what a politician was treated to in those good old days. (Laughter.) Fie must confess to not being in the best of form that evening,but for the credit of that great Bowling Association, he would not like any of them to run away I with a false impression and imagine that because they I had read in the Press of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>_ LIVELY TIMES AT _WESTMINSTER, – •</head>
        <p>_ LIVELY TIMES AT _WESTMINSTER, - •</p>
        <p>their President had been mixed up in any of those ructions. (Laughter.) He was glad to assure them he , had managed to keep out of the fray so far. (Laughter.) I Continuing, Mr. Crosfleld said he had seen estimates of the headway which the game of bowls had made in England and particularly in the North Western Division. It was a source of great satisfaction and pride to him to occupy for a time the position of President of such an important association, and to watch the progress the game was making all over the country, but especially in the most thickly populated parts of it in the North and the Midlands. He rsally did not see how any thinking man could view that development of English outdoor life with anything but the very deepest satisfaction. (Hear, hear.) One could not look around without being struck on all hands with what was technically called THE "SPEEDING EP" OF MODERN lODE'STILY a thing which he quite granted might be overdone, but which he daresay up to a reasonable point was necessary in foreign commerce. Every skilled work- man in the country knew it and, to be frank, it was not only in skilled manual work but in brain work as well that the stra:n was felt. This pressure on people in England (as in other parts of the civilized world) was one great reason why we needed outdoor recreation te keep our people in good trim and condition. (Hear, hear.) Another fact bearing oil the same point was the great increase in the population of our busy cities and towns during the last half century or more which made outdoor sports a positive necessity in order to main- tain the stamina of the British race. (Applause.) After all, the matter did not need any argument. He rejoiced in the progress the game had made and con- tinued to make. (Applause.) He hoped projects that lay ahead of them would in the fulness of time be realised and that they would have a fine national com- petition between the various counties; and that Lancashire, Yorkshire and Cheshire would play a lead- ing part in helping to bring about that result. (Applause.) One matter which he thought was import- ant to bowlers was the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>"DAYLIGHT SAVING BILL,"</head>
        <p>"DAYLIGHT SAVING BILL,"</p>
        <p>in which a friend of his on the opposite side of the House—Sir Edward. Sassoon—was taking a great deal of interest, and, as Chairman of the Committee in the House of Commons, helping forward. Without entering into details, the effect of that measure would simply be that we should get up and go to bed at the same time as usual in the winter, but during the summer months we should so arrange matters by altering the times of the clocks all through the country, that we should rise an hour sooner and retire to bed an hour earlier. He thought that to bowlers throughout the summer and perhaps especially in the early part of the season and also towards the close, this measure when passed would be a very great boon indeed. (Applause.) It simply meant that hundreds of thousands of bowlers would have every year a great deal more daylight and sunshine in which to enjoy their favourite recreation on thousands of greens all over the country. Manu- facturers, Chambers of Commerce, and various large organisations representing many different parts of the community had expressed themselves in favour of the Bill, and he would be very glad indeecl if the members of their Bowling Association would give it their back- ing until it became the law of the land. He had considered the arguments on both sides and was bound to say those in favour of it wero overwhelming. Mr. Crosfield concluded by again thanking the meeting for their cordial reception and congratulated every Imember of the Lancashire team on their creditable achievement. He did not quite know what to say to his friend of the county of the white rose ; he spoke so nicely that it almost seemed a mistake to add any- thing to his speech. If Mr. Cartwright was one of the best winners in the world it was evident that he was also one who best knew how to be the runner-up. (Applause.) They were delighted to welcome their Yorkshire friends ; their minds travelled back to the centuries when the relations were at times perhaps a trifle strained—(Laughter)—and when contests were decided on less peaceful fields than the bowling greens of Old England, where, under a happier dispensation they fought their battles to-day. (Applause). INDIVIDUAL MERIT HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfleld then presented the prizes to the winners of the individual merit handicap, observing that he was glad to know that in this contest Warrington did its duty pretty well. (Applause.) The winners were as follows :-1, T. Ellison, War- rington Cemetery ; 2, T. Glover, Leigh Railway ; 3, R. Trigger, Warrington Cemetery ; 4, A. Scott, Warring- ton Cemetery ; 5, T. Humphries, Warrington Cemetery ; 6, A. Rosterne, Warrington Cemetery ; 7, J. Gleave, Warrington Bull's Head ; 8, G. Holbrook, Warrington Itylands'. Mr. Ellison, as the winner of the first prize, received an order entitling him to a bedroom SIRE., Mr. Glover, the runner up,was presented with a beauti- ful case of cutlery. Mr. T. Ellison said he never felt so happy in his life as when he won the prize. He had a bard struggle with Mr. Glover, of Leigh, but he was proud to say he Say ed the honour of the Cemetery team. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>CLUB CHAMPIONSHIP.</head>
        <p>THE CLUB CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfteld, in presenting the Lancashire and Cheshire Club Championship to the Warrington Bull's Head team, said that duty naturally afforded him the greatest pleasure. He would like to say a great deal about the Bull's Head andhis old friend Alderman Evans. He was afraid if he cracked them up too much they might begin to suspect that Warringtonians were given to blowing their own trumpet. (Laughter.) Well, without allowing that dreadful suspicion to enter their minds they would perhaps excuse him if he had a little personal interest and pride in Warrington ; as to his personal responsibililies there, and whether he carried them out or not that was quite another matter. He was sure none of those present would answer for that ; least of all his friend Alderman Evans. (Laughter.) He wished in their name, and on his own account, however, to congratulate Alderman Evans and the President of the team, Councillor Roberts, very heartily, on this splendid achievement of the Bull's Head Club. Since the spread of bowls they had talked of that evening, competition in the game, as in so many other things, had consequently intensified, and it was very difficult either for an individual or a Club to win the first prize, therefore. Their friends from the Bull's Head were entitled to all the more credit on the signal victory on which they were to be most heartily congratulated. (Applause.) Councillor W. B. Roberts, preaident of the Bull's Head team, received the cup and said he esteemed it a very great honour to do so. The trophy had already passed through the hands of worthy clubs and had been the cause of many happy meetings. He felt perfectly certain that in handing it to the custody of the captain, Mr. Gleave, it would lose none of its lustre. (Applause.) Mr. Cresdeld then presented gold medals to the members of the Bull's Head team and gold centre medals to the Cemetery contingent. Mr. G. Ball, jun., made a brief speech on behalf of the Cemetery team, in which he congratulated Bull's Head on their well deserved victory and said the game was an admirable one. OTHER PRIZES.</p>
        <p>Association Prizes : 1, Cadishead Railway Hotel ; 2, Heatley Green Dragon. Handicap : 1, H. Sharples, Pendleton Belmont ; 2 E. House, Heaton Park Conservatives ; 3, J. E. Devon- port, Pendleton Belmont; 4, E. Parr, Chorlton Bowling Greel. 2nd Teams: 1, Whitfield ; 2, \Vinnington Park. Gold Medals : R. Williamson, L. and C.v Yorkshire ; Sa3kville, L. and C. v StAffirdshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>WYCLIFFE COCKHEDGE MISSION.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE COCKHEDGE MISSION.</p>
        <p>Sale of Work In illd of the New Church.</p>
        <p>A sale of work, the object of which was to raise the sum of £lOO required to complete the payment for the site of the proposed new Congregational Church and schools off Padgate-lane,was held at Wycliffe Cockhedge Mission on Thursday and yesterday. The mission workers had entered into the project with great enthusiasm and the room, with its attractively arranged stalls, presented a bright and pleasing appearance. The following is a list of the stallholders &gt;— LADIES' SEWING CLASS.—Mrs. Ashton, Mrs. Shaw, Mrs. Daniels, Mrs. Hibbert, Mrs. Berry. Suspxy SCHOOL—Mrs. W. H. Wallington, Miss Clayton, Miss Annie Clayton, Miss Joynson, Miss Mather. _</p>
        <p>CONGREGA.TIONAL.—Mrs. McClure. Mir. F. Morrall, Mrs. Reid, Mrs. Dell. Novmax' STALL.—Mrs. Syers, Mrs. Crozier. POUND STALL AND ADVERTISING STALL.—Mr. .1. Hibbert, Miss Daniels, and Miss Hibbert. PLANTS, FLOWERS AND FRUIT STALL.—Mr. W. Berry, Mrs. Berry, Miss Annie Hankinson. REFRESHMENT STALL.—Mrs. Wall, Mrs. Phcenix, Misses Senior, Gertrude Clayton, Annie Pearson, Lucy Pearson, Ethel Holbrook, Martha Ashbrook, Mary Senior, Dorothy Hankinson, Ethel Wallington. BRAN TUB: Miss Winnie Evans and Miss Ethel Smith. RIFLE RANGE.—Mr. J. W. Brown' FISH POND.—The Misses Senior. CURIOSITY SHOE' AND ART GALLERY.—Mr. W. Middleton and Mr. H. Wilson.</p>
        <p>The bazaar officials were :—Rev. H. Wall, president • Mr. W. H. Wallington, vice-president ; Mr. E. S. Garland. treasurer : Mr. J. North, financial secretary ; Messrs. A. Ashton and F. Morrall, secretaries ; Mr. J. Phcenix, coupon secretary. The sale was opened on Thursday afternoon by Mrs. Ziba Armitage (Newton Bank), the proceedings being presided over by Mr. J. W. Slack. The Rev. JoHN YON at the commencement of the proceedings made a statement referring to the objects of the sale of work and the money required for the undertaking. He said that they at Wycliffe were in sympathy with their friends at Cockhedge Mission in their long-time cherished desire to have more com- modious premises than those in which they were met together that afternoon. They sometimes heard of a good time coming and it could be accurately repeated with regard to the provision for better premises for their mission. They all knew that a site had been secured on land between Padgate-lane and the Cheshire Lines railway. A sum of £4lO had been paid for the Lind, but they had had to borrow some of that money for the reason they could not get it otherwise. There was in the bauk, gathered and put there through the dilligence and enthusiasm of their mission people, a slim of £274. They had also received a donation of £5O which was promised two or three years ago from Mr. Henry Roberts as a gift in memory of his father, who took a deep interest in the work at the mission. 'There was a deficit therefore of £BO and in addition to that there would be other legal expenses, which were necessarily incurred in carrying through the transfer of the property. The amount now necessary was at least £lOO in order to secure the liability of that site. In conclusion he congratulated the workers on the energy, enthusiasm and spirit which they had put into that occasion. He hoped they would be abundantly encouraged by the generosity of their friends. Other donations had been received from Mr. F. R. Roberts, IMr. G. Walker, Mr. P. Travis and Mr. J. W. Slack. The CHAIRMA.N, before inviting Mrs. Armitage to declare the sale of work open remarked that it was a pleasure for him to be there that afternoon to join with them in helping to aid the mission of the burden of the debt. Anyone who took the trouble to look at the room would wish them" God Speed " not only in the raising of the money to clear off the debt on the land, but also in the raising of a sum of money, in order to build a new church, so that they might get out of that inconvenient and uncomfortable building in which they worshipped every Sunday. He wished their efforts every success and trusted that the naoney required would be raised. (Applause). In a short and happy speech Mrs. Zli A ARMITAGE declared the proceedings open. She said that previous silos of work had been held whilst she was away from home, and she was especially glad that the present one had been fixed in the autumn in order that she could be present. They ought to have a very successful sale and she hoped they would be able to clear off the debt. Their mission funds had worked very hard and earnestly in order to get their new church, and they coulci not doubt that it was very much needed. The present building was quite inadequate for the work which had to be carried forward. A vote of thanks to Mrs. armitage and Mr. Slack,was proposed by Mr. E. Garland. He remarked that Mrs. Armitage was always willing to help on every occasion. Mr. WILLIAM BESNVICE. seconded, and the resolution was carrried unanimously. Mr. SLICK suitably replied on behalf of Mrs. Armitage and himself. There were enjoyable musical entertainments at intervals,-the artistes being :—Miss Gardiner (Hough Green), Miss Bailey, Mrs. Dell, Mr. Eugene Dell, and Miss Lloyd Williams' juvenile orchestra.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>YESTERDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</head>
        <p>YESTERDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>Yesterday afternoon the sale was re-opened by Mrs. C. E. Parker (Penketh Lodge), Mr. Josiah Longland presiding. The Rev. H. WALL again explained the objects of the sale and announced that Thursday's proceeds amounted to .CGI its. 9d.</p>
        <p>The CIIAIRM AN said that this was his first visit to Cockhedge Mission but he knew it had been a valuable adjunct to Wycliffe Church for the past thirty or forty years. The accommodation was, however, inadequate and unsuitable, and he hoped they would soon realize their project of building a new church. Mrs. PARKER said that when she was present at the sale of work at the mission two years ago she was s.ruck with the great need there was for much better `premises and she hoped the workers would soon be in a position to carry the scheme to a successful issue. (Applause). Mr. W. H. WALLINGTON proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Parker and the Chairman. He spoke of the good work which Mrs. Parker and her relatives had done for</p>
        <p>tbeCaUS-e-jf Yjil-ri;t1;1-fiy-fn-Wa-rrington and the county. With regard to that mission he hoped they would SOCHI have more cheery surroundings and then their work would go on better., (Applause). Mr. F. MoartALL seconded and said he was glad the time had come when they could look over the fence of denominationalism and help one another. The vote was heartily accorded and in responding Mrs. PARKER said she believed she was a Free Church- woman first and a ‘Vesleyan next. (Applause.) -The total proceeds of the sale amounted to £9l-</p>
        <p>Sad Case of Neglect. Another sad case of child neglect was heard at the Warrington Police Court on Thursday when Margaret Loftus, Platt-street, was summoned for neglecting her five children. Mr. A. Browne, who prosecuted on behalf of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, detailed the usual sordid story of a wretched home through excessive drinking. The husband of the prisoner was described as a respectable working man, who gave his wife on an average about 303. weekly. The prisoner was in the habit of visit- ing a neighbour's house where drinking amongst INomen was carried on. The house was in a very neglected condition, and the children were dirty and uncared for. The magistrates sent her to gaol for three months.</p>
        <p>Take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, for Coughs and Colds; it never fails. is, lid., 2s, 91—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>"By Ovallst."</p>
        <p>Last Saturday's match with Merthyr gave me a headache, it was such a dull, uninteresting exhibition. Warrington could only beat their South Wales opponents by a beggarly five points to nil, and for a long time it was actually a question whether they would win at all. No doubt the slippery turf and wet ball bad a lot to do with the poor football shown, but under any circumstances Warrington ought to have beaten Merthyr by more than five points at home. Why, it is only a few weeks since Warrington scored 28 points on the Merthyr ground, though 20 of these came directly from Fish. Saturday's match proved one thing—that the back division then doing duty is a long way below the standard required to keep Warrington's name high in Northern Union football. The inexperienced players in the third line did their best, but after all inexperience spells weakness on the football ground.</p>
        <p>A good many of Warrington's crack players were I absentees, some through injury and others from causes which they can themselves remove. Until these men qualify themselves for selection, the Club will have to persevere with the youngsters who, at least, have enthusiasm for the game. The fault was not all their's on Saturday, for two well-tried men like Lees and Jenkins at half back played about as poor a game as it is possible to imagine. They seemed helpless with a ball like a pound of butter, and the Merthyr men really handled better. I don't know how Warrington won, unless it is that fortune awarded them the only vportunity and they took it. The visitors were quite as good in all departments, but they certainly lacked scoring power.</p>
        <p>Warrington want Dickenson, Taylor and Brooks in the team again, and then they will have something like a formidable side. They should find their feet before Christmas, and with a little luck henceforth they ought to make things livelier for the opposition.</p>
        <p>Brooks was away representing his County against Yorkshire on Saturday, and it is very gratifying to learn that he was the best man on the field in the estimation of nearly every critic. Playing at outside half—his proper place—be gave a great display and had a lot to do with Lancashire's handsome win.</p>
        <p>The " Yorkshire Post " says : "A striking contrast in half-back play was fur- nished by Brooks and Jolley on the Lancashire side. These two were past-masters in the art of confounding their opponents. Jolley, the veteran, is rich in his store of football intelligence. Brooks, his partner, commanded admiration by his sound and clever all-round play. Strength, speed, and nippiness are combined in him to some purpose,and if he possessed the added virtue of unselfishness, he would have to be regarded as the best half-back in the Northern Union. Ho does not, however, give sufficient consideration to his colleagues, and at least one try was thrown away in Saturday's match through his holding the ball too long. In every other respect, however, his play was above reproach and in the variety of his methods he set a pattern which liolroyd and Slater might have copied with advantage."</p>
        <p>" Forward" in the " Athletic News " says :-- Brooks was easily the best player on the field. To me he appeared to be the only man in twenty- six who realised the possibilities of a little method in his play. His breakaways were fine, and my only adverse criticism is that near the end he held in preference to passing out, and spoiled a certain try.</p>
        <p>The "Manchester Guardian" says, after criticising the too-orthodox passing of both teams: "There was, fortunately, one back of the two teams who could be returned as not guilty. It was the Lancashire half Brooks. Perhaps because he has not played Northern Union football very long —he is said to be under twenty—he has not yet become a slave to the "passing" habit. He thinks first. That on Saturday distinguished him from all his fellows. As a direct result of his thinking came one or two of not only the most effective, but also the most effectual, bits of play in the match, and to them indirectly was due Lancashire's success. Brooks had for a partner Jolley, of Runcorn, who in his way was equally invaluable, though it was in defence rather than attack. He and Brooks made an almost ideal pair, and they were Lancashire's winning point."</p>
        <p>Brooks will smile when he sees that he is still " under twenty." One can hardly call him " Baby " Brooks much longer. Hats off to the " best half-back in the Northern Union " and a Warrington lad !</p>
        <p>The League table, including Saturday's results, is as follows :</p>
        <p>For Agst. Per P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. centage Oldham . 9 9 0 0 33 43 195 6 6 30 10000 WakeflUlT 8 7 0 1 21 40 163 9 10 48 9375 Wigan ll 10 1 0 46 58 226 15 14 72 9090 Halifax 8 7 1 0 20 33 139 0 10 42 8750 Hunslet 8 6 1 1 16 26 110 8 11 49 8125 Keighley 8 5 2 1 21 30 132 9 18 72 68.75 Runcorn 9 7 2 0 14 24 100 14 11 61 77•77 Leigh Batley Salford Ebbw Vale 9 5 3 1 13 33 125 16 24 104 GNI H'ddersfildlo 6 4 0 20 31 133 20 21 103 6000 Hull 11 6 5 0 30 37 171 21 24 114 5451 Swinton —.ll 6 5 0 21 16 90 12 22 90 54'54 Leeds Broght'n R 9 4 4 1 20 20 100 13 25 101 5000 Hull K.R. 8 3 4 1 27 23 138 19 22 104 43'75 Dewsbury 7 3 4 0 14 14 70 16 16 80 4285 Treherbert 7 3 4 0 8 9 43 5 20 70 4285 Merthyr T 8 3 5 0 12 16 72 25 21 113 37'50 Warring'n 10 3 6 1 18 18 90 18 22 102 35.00 Bradford 9 3 6 0 21 27 123 27 29 141 '33'33 St. Helens 9 3 6 0 21 25 117 26 30 142 33.33 Barrow 9 3 6 0 10 10 50 22 27 125 33.33 Widnes 9 2 6 1 12 15 69 17 21 97 2727 Barry ' 8 2 6 0 2 9 31 26 46 190 2500 Mid- Rh'da 7 I 6 0 9 8 42 15 22 96 14'28 Rochdalell 8 1 7 0 5 9 37 13 19 83 12'50 Bramley York Aberdare 7 0 7 0 4 6 26 30 42 186 0000 I hope the Warrington men will do some special training during the next fourteen days so as to 'be fit for the two biggest home matches of the season Australia and Oldham. It would make up for a lot of this season's disappointments if the Wilderspool brigade won both these engagements. With their best men all available and in s-und condition they onght to be equal to the task. It may be Warring- ton's honour to inflict the first defeat on Oldham. They did this once before when Oldham had an unbroken series of ten victories behind them.</p>
        <p>The first round of the Lancashire Cu n will be decided to-day when Warrington meet St. Helens on the ground of the latter. On paper form the Saints would appear to have an easy victory in store, but Warrington mean to make a big effort and should not be far off gbining the verdict. The fact that so many of the newer men are ineligible may not make much difference, and the team selected ought to give a good account of itself. Dickenson, Taylor, Brooks and Fish will be welcomed back again, and if they can reproduce their best form Warrington will I win.</p>
        <p>The team is : Tilley, full back ; Fish, Dickenson, Taylor and Bradshaw, threequarters ; Jenkins and Brooks, ball backs ; A. Boardman, P. Boardman, Belton, Naylor, Hewitt and Chesters, forwards.</p>
        <p>I suppose Brooks is intended to work the scrum for Jenkins, and vice versa. La my opinion the attack of the team will be much stronger if Brooks plays out. side half —his proper position and the one in which he distinguished himself last Saturday against York- shire. It is unfortunate that neither of the two is a scrummage-ivorker, and that S. Lees is among those who were not signed on soon enough to be eligible. The others in the same fix are Thomas, ,O'Neill, Dowell, and Treharne.</p>
        <p>Cadishead and Irlani, under the captaincy of Burgess, will be the visitors to Wilderspool to-day. Opportunity is being taken to give several local juniors a chance. Knot Gibbs, having worked off his suspension, will also make a first appearance in the Warrington colours. He has a reputation as a goal- kicker as well as a forward. The chosen thirteen are: Mclntyre, full back; Green, Jordan, Cartwright and Landor, three-quarters; S. Lees and Daintith, half backs • Harmer, Bradshaw, Broadhurst, Gibbs, Harrison and Simister, forwards.</p>
        <p>The prosecution undertaken by the Wigan Club against an individual of the betting class who is charged with attempting to bribe Todd and Johnston to lose the Hunslet match in September has aroused</p>
        <p>intense interest throughout the Union. It is the first case of its kind ever brought, and one is glad to think that players who will "8e11" a match are few and far between. The two New Zealanders seem to have acted very straightforwardly in the matter. Owing to the magistrates being equally divided, there was no decision in the case, and it will be re-tried before another bench on Wednesday. Any such attempt at bribery and corruption in professional football ought, when proved, to be put down with a strong hand. The Wigan Club have done the right thing in taking legal action and making the aff air public.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens team will be: Barton, full back . Manchester, Helsby, Hillen and Flannagan, three-' quarters ; Trenwith and Hill, half backs ; W. Briers, Prescott, Lee, Pope, Mavitty, and Mooney, forwards. On Thu/ sday the Kangaroos showed that their form against Dewsbury was all wrong, by defeating York- shire at Hull in handsome fashion, the score being 24 points to 11 in their favour. If they can win to-day at Hunslet there will be no ground for suggesting that the tourists are anything but first-class.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-11-07_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Hockey.</head>
        <p>Hockey.</p>
        <p>Latchford were unfortunate at Preston last week in losing 3-2 because the winning goal was scored by the home team three minutes after time owing to a bungle on the part of the home referee who was a substitute I and made a sad mess of things throughout. Apart from this, the game was a very good one. Preston's goal- keeper played a capital game and had a great deal to do with his side's victory. The visitors with an enforced re-arranged forward line gave a good account of them- selves. Banks and Jones scored for Latchford.</p>
        <p>The second team match had to be cancelled owing to Preston's inability to raise a team.</p>
        <p>To-day Widnes are opposed, the first eleven being at home with the following team :—W. Unsworth, goal; C. Plinston, T. Dixon backs; D. Travis, J. B. Wilkinson, J. Albinson, halves ; P. Newell, H. Parker, H. Scott, B. Banks, W. Brown, forwards. Referee, Mr. W. B. Cobb. Bully-off 3.15 p.m.</p>
        <p>The second eleven at Widnes is as follows :—J. Appleton, goal ; J. Wilkinson, W. H. Pickles, backs ; S. R. Romney, A. Knight, A. Newton, halves; A. Starkey, R. Jones, H. Booth, W. H. Minards, C. Hackney, forwards. Referee, Mr. Harry Warburton. Train lea yes Warrington Central 2.21 p.m.</p>
        <p>• WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH. _— Annual Sale of Work. Fairfield schools presented a scene of brightness and animation on Thursday afternoon when the annual sale of work in connection with the Warrington Parish Church was opened. No effort had been spared to make the affair a success, the workers being most enthusiastic. The stalls, which were very pleasingly arranged were in charge of the following : RECTORY STALL.—MrS. Mrs. Rylands, Miss Massy, Miss Shaw, Mrs. Fowler, Miss White, Miss Lister-Kaye. CHURCH OFFICERS' STALL.—Mesdames Craik, Taylor, Scott, Bottomley, Lees, and Miss Lee. G.F.S. STALL—The Misses White, Mesdames R. W. Collin, J. T. Locker, R. Lunt, and E. Atkin. CHILDREN'S STALL.—The Misses Dorothy and Vera Collin. FLOWER STALL.—Mrs. Ferran, Mrs. Wm. Fairclough, Miss Gorst, Miss Clarke, Miss Annie Taylor, Miss F. Lowe. ST. CLEMENT'S MISSION STALL.—Mrs. Barber, Mrs. Hall, Mrs. Sanders, Mrs. Gray, Mrs. I. Hicks, Mrs. H. Hicks, Mrs. Blease, Miss Baskerville. ST. GEORGE'S MISSION STALL.—Miss Hetherington, Mrs. Barrett, Miss Bowes, Mrs. Knight, Miss Buckley. SIXPENNY STALL.—MiSS Stevenson. BIBLE CLASS AND INSTITUTE STALL—Mrs. Andrews, Mrs. J. Hill, Miss Baldwin Miss Brimelow, Misses Wadsworth, Mrs. Barnes, Ass Stockton Mrs. Rook, Mrs. Ashley, Misses Brandreth, Mrs. Paelmore, Mrs. Butterworth. FANCY STALL AND BRAN TUB.—Miss Grace Leos and Miss Annie Taylor. REFRESHMENT STALL.—Mrs. Brookes, Miss E. Brookes, Miss M. Brookes, Mrs. Lowe, Miss F. Price, the Misses Woods, Mrs. Bate Miss Cuppage, the Misses Lee, Miss Taylor, Miss Maud Wood, Miss P. Taylor, Miss Sankey, Miss Whittle. Mr. G. Brown is the bazaar secretary and Mr. F. G. Taylor the treasurer. The opening ceremony on Thursday was performed by the Hon. Mrs. Willis, of Halsnead Park. The Rector (ltev. Canon Willis) presided and amongst others present were : the Revs. Canon Stevenson, E. C. E. Carleton, T. W. Barber, T. A. J. Ridpath, A. J. Brett, G. W. Dickenson, Councillor Craik, Colonel Buckton, Messrs. R. Willis, C. White, R. Pierpoint, F. Taylor, F. G. Taylor G. Brown, F. M. Darby, R. Lee. The RECTOR said there were five great objects of the sale. First came the Parish Church. He was thankful to say that their income from all sources was excellent, amounting to about £7OO a year, but at the same time it only enabled them to do what they were obliged to do, and there were many other things which they might well carry out if they had the means. Then there was the Church Institute. They had opened the first part and it was to cost £7OO. They had already raised £5OO, leaving a balance of £2OO. Of course, the portion already built was only part of a very large scheme. The third object was the missions. They had already spent about £l,OOO on St. George's but they wanted about £l5O. They wished to pay off the debt on the exten- sion and then to give a donation to St. Clement's Mission which had worked so hard and unselfishly for them. The Sunday schools were also worthy of help which the churchwardens could not give from their present resources, and they had also to buy materials for next year's sale, Their first great motive in work- ing was the highest of all—the spread of religious truth. Ages ago the country was divided into dioceses and parishes and their great aim was to spread the truth. It was only lately that other religious bodies had sprung up. The Church of England was the old Church of the land and they must teach the truth to all their people. The Rector also urged the necessity of providing for the material good of the people and he submitted that the Institute would fulfil that purpose. The Hon. Mrs. WILLIS said she could fully under- stand the numerous needs there must be in a large town like Warrington but knowing the kind support the Rector received from his people she felt there would be no difficulty in raising a large sum of money for the church funds which were so worthy of support. It afforded her the greatest pleasure to help her uncle and Mrs. Willis, knowing the great interest they took in their parish and how much they had the welfare of the people at heart. (Applause). Councillor Cu.A.nr. (Rector's warden) in moving a vote of thanks to the Hon. Mrs. Willis said he did not think it was possible for her help to be given to a worthier clergyman than their esteemed Rector. (Apylause). St. Elphin's was an up-to-date and pro- gressive parish. They believed in taking on plenty of work and in carrying it out successfully. Mr. G. BROWN seconded and the vote was heartily passed. Mr. RICHARD WILLIS responded. Ta RECTOR said the sum they wanted to raise was £3oo'. In the evening there wore some enjoyable entertain- ments. The Misses Broadbent had arranged perform- ances of a play "Compromising Martha " which were creditably given by the following :—Martha, Miss Broadbent ; Mrs. Box, Mrs. George Dakin ; Monica, Miss M. Broadbent ; the Curate, Mr. R. Greener. Eighteen candidates of the G.F.S. gave a pretty cantata "Christmas festivities " for which they had been well trained by Miss White and Mrs. Collin. YESTERDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>Yesterday the sale was re-opened in the presence of a large gathering by Lady Hutton, of Hulton Park, Bolton. The RECTOR in his opening remarks particularly com- mended the Church Institute to the support of all present, for two reasons. They wanted to do their utmost to spread the true faith and bring back to the Church those who in days gone by separated from it and also to promote the material good of their fellow creatures. If there was anything that was likely to do their working people good it was Church Institute where they could have healthy recreation and innocent pleasure. There were great temptations to the working-classes. At present everybody's minds were full of the unemployment question. He saw in the paper only the previous day that Mr. John Burns, the President of the Local Government Board, stated that the average amount spent by the working man upon drink was ss. to 7s. per week. Who could wonder at there being distress if that was tha case ? If men would only save up their money instead of spending it in that foolish way, and enjoy themselves rationally by making good use of all the facilities for healthy recreation and amusement, how better it would be. In conclusion the Rector announced that the previous day's proceeds amounted to £lOO. Lady HELTON, in a few appropriate words, declared the sale open, and a vote of thanks was passed to her on the motion of Mr. S. TAYLOR (people's warden), seconded by Mr. F. G. TAYLOR. The RECTOR announced that he had received a telegram from Mr. A. H. Crosfleld, M.P., asking him to arrange that £5 should be spent on warm clothing and similar articles suitable for the poor of the parish. The sale will be re-opened to-day (Saturday), by Mr. Robert Pierpoint. The total proceeds up to last evening amounted to , £lBO</p>
        <p>There's no use in vain repining, When bronchitis brings you pain, Every cloud has a silver lining, And you'll soon get well again. All who've tried can well assure you, Even if you've suffered long, Woods' Great Peppermint Cure will cure you, Quickly make you well and strong.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
