ause they must go through certain stages to reach certain I erpoints. . iFtriseess said he believed that had it not been for the letters and agitation the matter would n3t have been pushed on as trickly as it had been. The CHAIRMAN: I don't think the people who wrote the letters inferred that the District Council was not pushing the matter forward. It was the County Council they meant. Mr. FortsTrxw : There is no dilatoriness at-all. We are doing all we can. A PADOATF COMPLAINT. The INSPECTOR reported that with reference to the nuisance at the goods yard, Padgate station, caused by bones being left ha the siding during the week-end, be had received a letter from Mr. James Pinion,manager to the Cheshire Lines Committee, stating that the committee were endeavouring to arrange for the bones to be consigned and carted away before the week-end and so avoid the possibility of any further complaint.</p>
