"So I . was," said Jack, "but I thought you would . be alone,- so I came to see you." - My story is done, and it is to thew you o what pleasure you 'can give to &amp;here by deny- ._, img yourself something. EDITIi 13 AVIS. ..: Dear Uncle Nech—l hope you enjoyed your- e self during your holiday. We shall soon have en winter here now. The trees are beginning to lose their leaves, and the birds begin to fly .., away to warmer countries. Enclosed you will nt find a piece •of poetry, which I hope to see published in the paper. .. DAISY'S PENNY. • . ' Little blue-eyed, Daisy sat on father's knee; ~ Daisy was a dimpled little maid, Something under four years, something over . - three— Golden tresses tied with velvet braid. Daisy," " Daisy," said her father, " which would yen o- t, prefer, "'', A penny or a halfpenny from me?" , '. , And, 'twixt a thumb and finger, he held them' e ' out to her, While Daisy sat as thoughtful as could be. eet At last, in timid, accents, she whispered in his, ear— Her golden tresses lying on his breaet— "lf I may choose, the one I think the nicest, ;',.4 daddy, dear, Then I should like the little penny best." e GERLLP APPLEBY. .-',.. ' HOW THE POOR MAN OVERCAME THE :, ' GIANT. Once there was a farmer who was very poor, ._ and had only one field and a little well-kept ~.S garden. I% the -field he grew eatables, and' ia e ~, the garden flowers. Now there was a giant liv- ing near, who was a well-known miser, ;who, after thinking hard how to get something for el' his supper without using his own money, hit 'upon the plan t-o steal it from the poor farmer. 'n, He went out, and, for the shortest ray. went- right through the flowers, and trampled theta e" down. Then he took as many potatoes and other, eatables as he could carry, and returned. When the farmer went to attend to his flowers - : the next morning, he found them not fit to be _ seen. He also found, by the larte beotinarkso . in the soft, soil, that it roust be the giant. ,' who had done it. Then he went to get' some •:;,' vegetables, and found that a lot of them had been stolen by the giant; but, wonder of won- dors, he found, under the'root of a cabbage, e, a large chest f gold. It took all his strength ' to carry 4 to his little cottage. With part of it ' he bought more land, and corn seed to grow en it. One night, a.a he was thinking how to, ~ atop the giant from getting into his:ger:lm and - fields, he heard a voice ,say. " I will tell you how to overcome the giant." Tice farmer ;-- looked round, and saw a lovely fait-y. She - continued, " Take two hungry I:l,.edheunds, let them take his scent, and place them in .e your money chest. Then place , the chest '.: amonerat the shrubs in your garden, and sec --, what n-ill happen. Good-bye." The farmer' , answered. and obeyed as soon as he could. e; When the giant was going to ,get another meal, - he saw the chest, went and picked it up, n thinking it was gold. He chuckled aloud at the thoughts of it. When the hounds 45-melt - - him, and- started barking, the giant opened l' the chest to se-e what was making the noise. -: The hounds sprang at him, put their teeth in -- his- neck, and killed him. The farmer lived ta- happily for a good while after. • ' ' BEN FAREAR. :7-</p>
